/* Reading practice: ten passages for every word.
 *
 * Each passage belongs to one word - `w` - and opens as soon as that word
 * has been introduced. `slot` is its place on that word's shelf, 0-9, which
 * is what the five-at-a-time rounds count through. `also` lists other course
 * words the text happens to contain; they are marked for the tooltip but do
 * not gate the passage.
 *
 * `source` names the public-domain book a passage was taken from. An empty
 * source means the passage was written for this course.
 *
 * `sense` is set only when this passage uses the word in a sense its card
 * definition does not teach - a different meaning, an adjacent one, or the
 * wrong part of speech. The reader's tooltip shows it instead of the card's
 * definition, and the reading check asks about it instead of drawing a
 * fresh question from the card's own examples. No `sense` means the card's
 * definition already fits.
 */
export const passages = [
  {"id": 0, "w": 0, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "The lake looked deep and dark from the boat, but near the island it was so <mark data-word=\"shallow\">shallow</mark> that we could see every stone on the bottom. Dad told us to get out and push. The water hardly came up to our knees, and little fish swam around our feet as if we were trees.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 1, "w": 0, "slot": 1, "also": [3], "text": "The river near our village looks dangerous but it is <mark data-word=\"shallow\">shallow</mark> — in summer the water only reaches your knees. Children cross it with their shoes in their hands. Once I <mark data-word=\"spill\">spilled</mark> a whole bag of apples in the middle of it and spent twenty minutes picking them out of the stones.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 2, "w": 0, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "We dug the hole for the new apple tree too quickly. It was only as deep as my hand, and my grandfather shook his head. 'A <mark data-word=\"shallow\">shallow</mark> hole makes a weak tree,' he said. 'The first strong wind will pull it out.' So we dug for another hour, until the whole spade disappeared into the ground.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 3, "w": 0, "slot": 3, "also": [2], "text": "There it was, black under the darkening sky: no motion, no sound near. She set down her basket, and then sank down herself on the grass, <mark data-word=\"tremble\">trembling</mark>. The pool had its wintry depth now: by the time it got <mark data-word=\"shallow\">shallow</mark>, as she remembered the pools did at Hayslope, in the summer, no one could find out that it was her body. But then there was her basket—she must hide that too.", "source": "Adam Bede — George Eliot"},
  {"id": 4, "w": 0, "slot": 4, "also": [3], "text": "Don't put the soup in that plate — it's too <mark data-word=\"shallow\">shallow</mark>. It will <mark data-word=\"spill\">spill</mark> before you reach the table. My mother said this to my father every winter, because he liked flat plates for everything. And every time he ignored her, a little river of soup ran across the tablecloth.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 5, "w": 0, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "\"Look, look, Emil, there's Ivar's big pond!\" Alexandra pointed to a shining sheet of water that lay at the bottom of a <mark data-word=\"shallow\">shallow</mark> draw. At one end of the pond was an earthen dam, planted with green willow bushes, and above it a door and a single window were set into the hillside. You would not have seen them at all but for the reflection of the sunlight upon the four panes of window-glass. And that was all you saw.", "source": "O Pioneers! — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 6, "w": 0, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "In the <mark data-word=\"shallow\">shallow</mark> end of the pool the small children were jumping and laughing, because they could stand with their heads above the water. At the other end the water was three metres deep, and only the older swimmers were allowed there. My sister stayed in the <mark data-word=\"shallow\">shallow</mark> end all summer, even after she learned to swim.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 7, "w": 0, "slot": 7, "also": [71], "text": "After a week without rain the stream had become very <mark data-word=\"shallow\">shallow</mark>. The water that used to cover our boots now <mark data-word=\"barely\">barely</mark> wet our toes, and the ducks walked where they once swam. The farmer said that if the dry weather continued, there would soon be no stream at all.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 8, "w": 0, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Her breathing was quick and <mark data-word=\"shallow\">shallow</mark>, as if she could not fill her lungs. The nurse sat her down by the open window. 'Slowly,' she said. 'Breathe deep, into your stomach, not just the top of your chest.' After a few minutes the colour came back to the girl's face.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 9, "w": 0, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Miriam knew now how strong was the attraction of Clara for him; but still she was certain that the best in him would triumph. His feeling for Mrs. Dawes—who, moreover, was a married woman—was <mark data-word=\"shallow\">shallow</mark> and temporal, compared with his love for herself. He would come back to her, she was sure; with some of his young freshness gone, perhaps, but cured of his desire for the lesser things which other women than herself could give him.", "sense": "superficial and insincere; lacking real depth of feeling", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 10, "w": 1, "slot": 0, "also": [2], "text": "My grandmother refused a walking stick for three years. She was <mark data-word=\"stubborn\">stubborn</mark> about it: a stick was for old people, she said, and she was not old. Then one winter her hands began to <mark data-word=\"tremble\">tremble</mark> when she carried the kettle, and she asked me to buy one. She used it for a week and then hid it behind the door.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 11, "w": 1, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "Our donkey was the most <mark data-word=\"stubborn\">stubborn</mark> animal in the village. When he decided to stop, he stopped, and no carrot, stick or kind word could move him. My father would pull the rope until his face turned red, and the donkey would simply look at him and wait. Ten minutes later he would walk on by himself, as if nothing had happened.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 12, "w": 1, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "He stood facing her, two paces away, waiting and gazing at her with wild determination, with feverishly passionate, <mark data-word=\"stubborn\">stubborn</mark>, set eyes. Dounia saw that he would sooner die than let her go. \"And... now, of course she would kill him, at two paces!\" Suddenly she flung away the revolver.", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 13, "w": 1, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "Soldiers when in desperate straits lose the sense of fear. If there is no place of refuge, they will stand firm. If they are in the heart of a hostile country, they will show a <mark data-word=\"stubborn\">stubborn</mark> front. If there is no help for it, they will fight hard.", "source": "The Art of War — active 6th century B.C. Sunzi"},
  {"id": 14, "w": 1, "slot": 4, "also": [55], "text": "With a writhing motion she settled herself more securely in the hammock. She perceived that her will had blazed up, <mark data-word=\"stubborn\">stubborn</mark> and resistant. She could not at that moment have done other than <mark data-word=\"deny\">denied</mark> and resisted. She wondered if her husband had ever spoken to her like that before, and if she had submitted to his command. Of course she had; she remembered that she had.", "source": "The Awakening, and Selected Short Stories — Kate Chopin"},
  {"id": 15, "w": 1, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Everybody told Tom that the bridge was closed, but he was too <mark data-word=\"stubborn\">stubborn</mark> to listen. He had driven that road for twenty years, he said, and he knew it better than any sign. An hour later he called us from the other side of the river, asking for someone to come and pull his car out of the mud.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 16, "w": 1, "slot": 6, "also": [37], "text": "\"Hum... ha... well, if the boy held his tongue because he promised, and not from obstinacy, I'll forgive him. He's a <mark data-word=\"stubborn\">stubborn</mark> fellow and hard to manage,\" said Mr. Laurence, <mark data-word=\"rub\">rubbing</mark> up his hair till it looked as if he had been out in a gale, and smoothing the frown from his brow with an air of relief.", "source": "Little Women — Louisa May Alcott"},
  {"id": 17, "w": 1, "slot": 7, "also": [53, 92], "text": "When the doctor told my grandfather to stop eating salt, he simply refused. 'I have eaten salt for eighty years,' he said, 'and I am still here.' My mother begged him and the doctor <mark data-word=\"warn\">warned</mark> him twice more, but he was <mark data-word=\"stubborn\">stubborn</mark>. In the end my grandmother stopped putting salt on the table and cooked with herbs instead, and he never <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticed</mark> the difference.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 18, "w": 1, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The contention that raged within her had no softening in it now; it was wholly between passionate defiance and <mark data-word=\"stubborn\">stubborn</mark> defiance. Her rich colour, her quick blood, her rapid breath, were all setting themselves against the opportunity of retracing their steps. 'I won't. I won't. I won't!' she repeated in a low, thick voice.", "source": "Little Dorrit — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 19, "w": 1, "slot": 9, "also": [54], "text": "My sister and I argued about the map for half an hour. She was sure the path went left; I was sure it went right. We were both <mark data-word=\"stubborn\">stubborn</mark>, and neither of us would <mark data-word=\"admit\">admit</mark> being wrong. In the end a farmer walked past and showed us the way — it was straight ahead.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 20, "w": 2, "slot": 0, "also": [8, 9], "text": "The dog at the gate looked <mark data-word=\"fierce\">fierce</mark>, but it was old and chained. When the postman came it barked so hard that the birds <mark data-word=\"scatter\">scattered</mark> off the roof. My little cousin used to <mark data-word=\"tremble\">tremble</mark> at the sound of it. Now she feeds it bread through the fence every morning.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 21, "w": 2, "slot": 1, "also": [38], "text": "The man from the West unfolded the little piece of paper handed him. His hand was <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark> when he began to read, but it <mark data-word=\"tremble\">trembled</mark> a little by the time he had finished. The note was rather short.", "source": "The Four Million — O. Henry"},
  {"id": 22, "w": 2, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "He had a violent fit of <mark data-word=\"tremble\">trembling</mark>, and then sunk into his chair. After a time he controlled himself, and, resting with an elbow on one knee, and his head upon that hand, could look towards Rachael. Seen across the dim candle with his moistened eyes, she looked as if she had a glory shining round her head. He could have believed she had.", "source": "Hard Times — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 23, "w": 2, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "'Hush!' said the child, laying her finger on her lip and looking round. 'When he comes home in the morning, which is generally just before day, I let him in. Last night he was very late, and it was quite light. I saw that his face was deadly pale, that his eyes were bloodshot, and that his legs <mark data-word=\"tremble\">trembled</mark> as he walked.", "source": "The Old Curiosity Shop — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 24, "w": 2, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "In the dimly lighted room with the dead woman the young man began to have thoughts. His mind played with thoughts of life as his mother's mind had played with the thought of death. He closed his eyes and imagined that the red young lips of Helen White touched his own lips. His body <mark data-word=\"tremble\">trembled</mark> and his hands shook.", "source": "Winesburg, Ohio: A Group of Tales of Ohio Small Town Life — Sherwood Anderson"},
  {"id": 25, "w": 2, "slot": 5, "also": [30], "text": "The kitten had spent the whole night outside in the rain. When I found it under the car, it was so cold that its tiny body was <mark data-word=\"tremble\">trembling</mark>, and it could not even stand. I <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark> it in a towel and held it against my chest until the shaking stopped and it began, very quietly, to purr.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 26, "w": 2, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "\"Oh—my boy!\" she faltered. Her lip <mark data-word=\"tremble\">trembled</mark>, her face broke, and, snatching up the child, she buried her face in his shoulder and cried painfully. She was one of those women who cannot cry; whom it hurts as it hurts a man. It was like ripping something out of her, her sobbing.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 27, "w": 2, "slot": 7, "also": [38], "text": "Marta had practised the speech for weeks, but when she stood in front of the whole school, her knees began to <mark data-word=\"tremble\">tremble</mark>. The paper in her hand shook so much that she could hardly read it. Then she saw her friend in the front row, smiling, and her voice slowly grew <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark>.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 28, "w": 2, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Doctor Reefy did not see the woman he had held in his arms again until after her death. On the summer afternoon in the office when he was on the point of becoming her lover a half grotesque little incident brought his love-making quickly to an end. As the man and woman held each other tightly heavy feet came tramping up the office stairs. The two sprang to their feet and stood listening and <mark data-word=\"tremble\">trembling</mark>.", "source": "Winesburg, Ohio: A Group of Tales of Ohio Small Town Life — Sherwood Anderson"},
  {"id": 29, "w": 2, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "How it happened he did not know. But all at once something seemed to seize him and fling him at her feet. He wept and threw his arms round her knees. For the first instant she was terribly frightened and she turned pale. She jumped up and looked at him <mark data-word=\"tremble\">trembling</mark>.", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 30, "w": 3, "slot": 0, "also": [4], "text": "The boy always filled his cup to the very top, because he did not want to walk back for more. He was <mark data-word=\"greedy\">greedy</mark> like that at every meal. Of course he <mark data-word=\"spill\">spilled</mark> half of it on the table before he even sat down, and his sister laughed at him for an hour.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 31, "w": 3, "slot": 1, "also": [12], "text": "I am <mark data-word=\"clumsy\">clumsy</mark> in other people's kitchens. I do not know where anything lives, so I open the wrong door, reach across the wrong hand, and <mark data-word=\"spill\">spill</mark> the milk on somebody else's clean table. At home I never break a thing.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 32, "w": 3, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "I took it from her hand: she pointed me out the parlour door. The tray shook as I held it; the water <mark data-word=\"spill\">spilt</mark> from the glass; my heart struck my ribs loud and fast. Mary opened the door for me, and shut it behind me.", "source": "Jane Eyre: An Autobiography — Charlotte Brontë"},
  {"id": 33, "w": 3, "slot": 3, "also": [97], "text": "The waiter was carrying four glasses of red wine on a small tray. Just as he reached our table, a child ran into his legs. The tray tipped, and the wine <mark data-word=\"spill\">spilled</mark> all over the white tablecloth and my new shirt. He <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologised</mark> again and again, and brought me a cloth and a glass of cold water for the stain.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 34, "w": 3, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "'Be careful with that bucket — it's full to the top. If you run, you'll <mark data-word=\"spill\">spill</mark> it all over the floor, and I've just cleaned it,' said Mother. Tim walked slowly down the hall, holding the bucket with both hands and watching the water move from side to side. He reached the garden without losing a drop.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 35, "w": 3, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "All about them were other small fires surrounded by men with their little black utensils. From one of these near came sudden sharp voices in a row. It appeared that two light-footed soldiers had been teasing a huge, bearded man, causing him to <mark data-word=\"spill\">spill</mark> coffee upon his blue knees. The man had gone into a rage and had sworn comprehensively. Stung by his language, his tormentors had immediately bristled at him with a great show of resenting unjust oaths.", "source": "The Red Badge of Courage: An Episode of the American Civil War — Stephen Crane"},
  {"id": 36, "w": 3, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "\"Stop!\" she cried, \"don't sing that. I don't want you to sing it,\" and she laid her glass so impetuously and blindly upon the table as to shatter it against a carafe. The wine <mark data-word=\"spill\">spilled</mark> over Arobin's legs.", "source": "The Awakening, and Selected Short Stories — Kate Chopin"},
  {"id": 37, "w": 3, "slot": 7, "also": [50], "text": "My grandfather's hands shook in his last years, and at every meal he <mark data-word=\"spill\">spilled</mark> a little soup on the tablecloth. My mother never said a word about it. She simply put a small towel next to his plate, and when the soup ran over the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark> of his spoon, she wiped it away and went on talking.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 38, "w": 3, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Before we left, Peter put ripe cucumbers into a sack for Mrs. Shimerda and gave us a lard-pail full of milk to cook them in. I had never heard of cooking cucumbers, but Ántonia assured me they were very good. We had to walk the pony all the way home to keep from <mark data-word=\"spill\">spilling</mark> the milk.", "source": "My Ántonia — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 39, "w": 3, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "I knocked my coffee over at breakfast. It <mark data-word=\"spill\">spilled</mark> across the table, ran onto the newspaper and dripped onto the cat, who jumped up with an angry cry. By the time I found a cloth, there was a brown lake on the floor and no coffee left in the cup.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 40, "w": 4, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "There were twelve cakes on the plate, one for each child at the party. Oliver took three while nobody was looking and hid two in his pockets. When his mother found out, she was ashamed of him. 'Don't be so <mark data-word=\"greedy\">greedy</mark>,' she said. 'Now little Anna has none.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 41, "w": 4, "slot": 1, "also": [5], "text": "The olive tree behind the house is <mark data-word=\"ancient\">ancient</mark> — my grandfather said it was already big when he was a boy. Every autumn we picked from it, and every autumn my uncle wanted the whole crop for himself. He was <mark data-word=\"greedy\">greedy</mark>, but nobody argued, because the tree kept giving.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 42, "w": 4, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The old landlord already owned half the street, but it was never enough for him. Every year he raised the rent, and every year he bought another house. 'He is not poor, he is <mark data-word=\"greedy\">greedy</mark>,' my father said. 'A poor man wants bread. A <mark data-word=\"greedy\">greedy</mark> man wants the whole bakery.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 43, "w": 4, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The boy had once heard the farmer's wife say, \"I have a pot with poison in it under my bed.\" She, however, had only said that to keep away <mark data-word=\"greedy\">greedy</mark> people, for there was honey in it. The boy crept under the bed, brought out the pot, and ate all that was in it.", "source": "Household Tales by Brothers Grimm — Jacob Grimm"},
  {"id": 44, "w": 4, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The seagulls on the beach were terribly <mark data-word=\"greedy\">greedy</mark>. As soon as someone opened a bag of chips, ten of them landed nearby, screaming and pushing each other. One even took a sandwich right out of a little boy's hand, although the birds had already eaten half of our lunch.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 45, "w": 4, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "In the old story, a <mark data-word=\"greedy\">greedy</mark> king asked that everything he touched should turn to gold. At first he was delighted. Then he sat down to dinner, and the bread turned to gold in his hand, and the wine turned to gold in his mouth. He had more gold than any man alive, and he was dying of hunger.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 46, "w": 4, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "'Just one more,' said my brother, reaching for the last piece of pizza. He had already eaten five. 'You're being <mark data-word=\"greedy\">greedy</mark>,' I told him. 'You're not even hungry any more.' He thought about it for a moment, sighed, and gave the piece to the dog.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 47, "w": 4, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "Two brothers had to share their father's farm. The older one wanted the house, the best fields and all the cows, and said the younger one could have the rocky hill. The neighbours shook their heads at how <mark data-word=\"greedy\">greedy</mark> he was. But on that hill the younger brother found fresh spring water, and soon the whole valley came to buy it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 48, "w": 4, "slot": 8, "also": [29], "text": "He glared at the girls with his fiery red eyes and cried: 'Why do you stand there? Can you not come here and help me?' 'What are you up to, little man?' asked Rose-red. 'You stupid, prying goose!' answered the dwarf: 'I was going to split the tree to get a little wood for cooking. The little bit of food that we people get is immediately burnt up with heavy logs; we do not <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallow</mark> so much as you coarse, <mark data-word=\"greedy\">greedy</mark> folk.", "source": "Grimms' Fairy Tales — Jacob Grimm"},
  {"id": 49, "w": 4, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "When the old woman heard of the exquisite salad, she was <mark data-word=\"greedy\">greedy</mark>, and said, \"Dear countryman, let me just taste this wonderful salad.\" \"Why not?\" answered he, \"I have brought two heads with me, and will give you one of them,\" and he opened his pouch and handed her the bad cabbage. The witch suspected nothing amiss, and her mouth watered so for this new dish that she herself went into the kitchen and dressed it.", "source": "Household Tales by Brothers Grimm — Jacob Grimm"},
  {"id": 50, "w": 5, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "And through another winter they wandered on the obliterated trails of men who had gone before. Once, they came upon a path blazed through the forest, an <mark data-word=\"ancient\">ancient</mark> path, and the Lost Cabin seemed very near. But the path began nowhere and ended nowhere, and it remained mystery, as the man who made it and the reason he made it remained mystery. Another time they chanced upon the time-graven wreckage of a hunting lodge, and amid the shreds of rotted blankets John Thornton found a long-barrelled flint-lock.", "source": "The Call of the Wild — Jack London"},
  {"id": 51, "w": 5, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "Behind the school there was an <mark data-word=\"ancient\">ancient</mark> oak tree. Its trunk was so wide that five children holding hands could not reach around it. A sign next to it said the tree was more than six hundred years old — older than the town, and older than any house in it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 52, "w": 5, "slot": 2, "also": [15], "text": "On Sunday evenings the whole street <mark data-word=\"stroll\">strolls</mark>. Not to go anywhere — just up to the church and back, slowly, greeting everybody twice. It is an <mark data-word=\"ancient\">ancient</mark> habit, and I only understood it after I had lived in a city where nobody does it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 53, "w": 5, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The Greeks built this theatre more than two thousand years ago, and it is still used today. Sitting on the <mark data-word=\"ancient\">ancient</mark> stone seats, you can hear an actor whisper from far below. People have watched plays in exactly the same place since long before anyone had heard of electricity.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 54, "w": 5, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The sitting-room of our client opened by a long, low, latticed window on to the <mark data-word=\"ancient\">ancient</mark> lichen-tinted court of the old college. A Gothic arched door led to a worn stone staircase. On the ground floor was the tutor's room. Above were three students, one on each story. It was already twilight when we reached the scene of our problem.", "source": "The Return of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 55, "w": 5, "slot": 5, "also": [24], "text": "We had broken suddenly into a lovely glade of greensward surrounded by <mark data-word=\"ancient\">ancient</mark> trees. On the farther side of it, under the <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> of a mighty oak, there stood a singular group of three people. One was a woman, our client, drooping and faint, a handkerchief round her mouth.", "source": "The Return of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 56, "w": 5, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "In the middle of the town square stands an <mark data-word=\"ancient\">ancient</mark> well. Nobody knows exactly who built it, but its stones are worn smooth, and the old people say it was there before the church, more than eight hundred years ago. Children still throw coins into it and make wishes.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 57, "w": 5, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The museum had a room full of <mark data-word=\"ancient\">ancient</mark> tools: stone knives, bone needles, and pots made by people who lived here thousands of years before anyone could write. My son pressed his face to the glass. 'Did they have phones?' he asked. 'No,' I said. 'They didn't even have iron.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 58, "w": 5, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The months went by, binding stronger and stronger the covenant between dog and man. This was the <mark data-word=\"ancient\">ancient</mark> covenant that the first wolf that came in from the Wild entered into with man. And, like all succeeding wolves and wild dogs that had done likewise, White Fang worked the covenant out for himself. The terms were simple. For the possession of a flesh-and-blood god, he exchanged his own liberty.", "source": "White Fang — Jack London"},
  {"id": 59, "w": 5, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "On the hill above our village are the ruins of an <mark data-word=\"ancient\">ancient</mark> castle. Only one tower and part of a wall are left. My teacher said it was built by a king whose name everyone has forgotten, and that the stones have been standing there for almost a thousand years.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 60, "w": 6, "slot": 0, "also": [7], "text": "The room I rented had <mark data-word=\"bare\">bare</mark> walls and one small window. I bought a yellow cloth for the table because I could not afford anything else, and it made the whole place look <mark data-word=\"cheerful\">cheerful</mark>. Small things do that when you own nothing.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 61, "w": 6, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "In winter the trees along our street are <mark data-word=\"bare\">bare</mark>. Every leaf has fallen, and you can see the birds' nests in the branches, empty and dark, like little baskets someone left behind. In April the first green comes back, and by May you cannot see a single nest.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 62, "w": 6, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The new flat was completely <mark data-word=\"bare\">bare</mark> when we moved in: no carpets, no curtains, no pictures, not even a light bulb. Our voices echoed off the walls. That first night we ate pizza on the floor and slept on our coats, and it felt like camping.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 63, "w": 6, "slot": 3, "also": [30], "text": "Clara took off her coat on the stairs, and he discovered she was in a sort of semi-evening dress, that left her arms and neck and part of her breast <mark data-word=\"bare\">bare</mark>. Her hair was done fashionably. The dress, a simple thing of green crape, suited her. She looked quite grand, he thought. He could see her figure inside the frock, as if that were <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark> closely round her.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 64, "w": 6, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The fisherman pulled the rope out of the icy water with his <mark data-word=\"bare\">bare</mark> hands. He had lost his gloves in the storm, and his fingers were red and stiff with cold. When he got back to the harbour, his wife made him hold them over the stove for ten minutes before he could move them again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 65, "w": 6, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Emil took a step nearer and stood frowning down at her bent head. He stood in an attitude of self-defense, his feet well apart, his hands clenched and drawn up at his sides, so that the cords stood out on his <mark data-word=\"bare\">bare</mark> arms. \"I can't play with you like a little boy any more,\" he said slowly.", "source": "O Pioneers! — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 66, "w": 6, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Mrs. Pontellier was by that time thoroughly awake. She began to cry a little, and wiped her eyes on the sleeve of her nightgown. Blowing out the candle, which her husband had left burning, she slipped her <mark data-word=\"bare\">bare</mark> feet into a pair of satin slippers at the foot of the bed and went out on the porch.", "source": "The Awakening, and Selected Short Stories — Kate Chopin"},
  {"id": 67, "w": 6, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The walls of my new classroom were completely <mark data-word=\"bare\">bare</mark> on the first day — no maps, no posters, no pictures, just white paint. By the end of September the children had covered every centimetre with their drawings, and the room finally felt like ours.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 68, "w": 6, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "In the autumn the farmers cut the corn, and the fields are <mark data-word=\"bare\">bare</mark> until spring: only brown earth, with nothing growing on it. The birds walk up and down the empty rows, looking for the seeds the machines have dropped.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 69, "w": 6, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The latter was at home in his garret, busily writing at the moment, and he opened the door himself. It was four months since they had seen each other. Razumihin was sitting in a ragged dressing-gown, with slippers on his <mark data-word=\"bare\">bare</mark> feet, unkempt, unshaven and unwashed. His face showed surprise.", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 70, "w": 7, "slot": 0, "also": [96], "text": "Your father, Jo. He never loses patience, never doubts or <mark data-word=\"complain\">complains</mark>, but always hopes, and works and waits so <mark data-word=\"cheerful\">cheerfully</mark> that one is ashamed to do otherwise before him. He helped and comforted me, and showed me that I must try to practice all the virtues I would have my little girls possess, for I was their example. It was easier to try for your sakes than for my own.", "source": "Little Women — Louisa May Alcott"},
  {"id": 71, "w": 7, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "\"I guess it is ourselves and not the house that we have to blame!\" said the baronet. \"We were tired with our journey and chilled by our drive, so we took a gray view of the place. Now we are fresh and well, so it is all <mark data-word=\"cheerful\">cheerful</mark> once more.\"", "sense": "pleasant and bright, so that it makes you feel happy (of a place)", "source": "The Hound of the Baskervilles — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 72, "w": 7, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "Our new neighbour is the most <mark data-word=\"cheerful\">cheerful</mark> person I know. She sings while she hangs out the washing, she waves at every car, and even when it rains for a week she says the garden needed it. At first I thought nobody could really be that happy. Now I think it is simply a habit, like brushing your teeth.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 73, "w": 7, "slot": 3, "also": [84], "text": "The nurse on the night <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark> was always <mark data-word=\"cheerful\">cheerful</mark>. She came into the ward smiling, told the old men silly jokes and laughed at her own mistakes. The patients said they felt better as soon as she opened the door, even before she gave them their medicine.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 74, "w": 7, "slot": 4, "also": [23], "text": "After the exam Leo came home <mark data-word=\"cheerful\">cheerful</mark>, <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistling</mark> on the stairs and hugging the dog. 'It went well?' asked his mother. 'No idea,' he said happily. 'But it's over, and that's enough for today.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 75, "w": 7, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Grandpa was ill for most of that winter, but he stayed <mark data-word=\"cheerful\">cheerful</mark>. He told stories from his bed, made us play cards with him, and laughed louder than anyone when he lost. Only once, late at night, did I see him look sad, and in the morning he was smiling again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 76, "w": 7, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "I began to carry her downstairs every morning, and upstairs every night. She would clasp me round the neck and laugh, the while, as if I did it for a wager. Jip would bark and caper round us, and go on before, and look back on the landing, breathing short, to see that we were coming. My aunt, the best and most <mark data-word=\"cheerful\">cheerful</mark> of nurses, would trudge after us, a moving mass of shawls and pillows.", "source": "David Copperfield — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 77, "w": 7, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "He was so quaintly <mark data-word=\"cheerful\">cheerful</mark> that I could not long be otherwise, and was almost ashamed of having been otherwise at all. When I went to bed, I cried. I am bound to confess that I cried; but I hope it was with pleasure, though I am not quite sure it was with pleasure. I repeated every word of the letter twice over.", "source": "Bleak House — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 78, "w": 7, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The bird put his tiny head on one side and looked up at him with his soft bright eye which was like a black dewdrop. He seemed quite familiar and not the least afraid. He hopped about and pecked the earth briskly, looking for seeds and insects. It actually gave Mary a queer feeling in her heart, because he was so pretty and <mark data-word=\"cheerful\">cheerful</mark> and seemed so like a person.", "source": "The Secret Garden — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 79, "w": 7, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The children marched into the classroom, <mark data-word=\"cheerful\">cheerful</mark> and noisy after the summer holidays, telling each other about the sea, the mountains and their grandmothers' cats. The teacher waited, smiling, and did not ask them to be quiet for a full five minutes.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 80, "w": 8, "slot": 0, "also": [22, 24], "text": "Tink said that the <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> was in the big box. She meant the chest of drawers, and Peter jumped at the drawers, <mark data-word=\"scatter\">scattering</mark> their contents to the floor with both hands, as kings toss ha'pence to the <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark>. In a moment he had recovered his shadow, and in his delight he forgot that he had shut Tinker Bell up in the drawer.", "source": "Peter Pan — J. M. Barrie"},
  {"id": 81, "w": 8, "slot": 1, "also": [34], "text": "Before them was a great <mark data-word=\"stretch\">stretch</mark> of country having a floor as smooth and shining and white as the bottom of a big platter. <mark data-word=\"scatter\">Scattered</mark> around were many houses made entirely of china and painted in the brightest colors. These houses were quite small, the biggest of them reaching only as high as Dorothy's waist. There were also pretty little barns, with china fences around them; and many cows and sheep and horses and pigs and chickens, all made of china, were standing about in groups.", "sense": "positioned at various points across an area, spread out and not clustered together", "source": "The Wonderful Wizard of Oz — L. Frank Baum"},
  {"id": 82, "w": 8, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "All night, with burning brands, he fought off the hungry pack. When he dozed despite himself, the whimpering and snarling of the dogs aroused him. Morning came, but for the first time the light of day failed to <mark data-word=\"scatter\">scatter</mark> the wolves. The man waited in vain for them to go. They remained in a circle about him and his fire, displaying an arrogance of possession that shook his courage born of the morning light.", "sense": "to make a group of animals or people break apart and flee in different directions", "source": "White Fang — Jack London"},
  {"id": 83, "w": 8, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The wind blew my papers off the table and <mark data-word=\"scatter\">scattered</mark> them all over the garden. One landed in the pond, two flew over the fence, and the rest danced across the grass while I ran after them like a madman.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 84, "w": 8, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Every morning the old man comes to the park with a bag of bread. He <mark data-word=\"scatter\">scatters</mark> the crumbs across the path, and in a moment a hundred pigeons are around his feet, fighting for the pieces. Then he sits on the bench and watches them, very pleased with himself.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 85, "w": 8, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Mia dropped the box of buttons, and they <mark data-word=\"scatter\">scattered</mark> across the kitchen floor — under the table, behind the fridge, into every corner. We spent half an hour on our knees looking for them, and we still find one now and then when we sweep.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 86, "w": 8, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "In spring the farmer walks across the field and <mark data-word=\"scatter\">scatters</mark> the seeds by hand, throwing them wide with a swing of his arm. My daughter walked behind him, trying to copy him, but most of her seeds landed on her own shoes.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 87, "w": 8, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "As the couple walked out of the church, the guests <mark data-word=\"scatter\">scattered</mark> rice and flower petals over their heads. For weeks afterwards the cleaner kept finding little pink petals between the benches and in the corners of the steps.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 88, "w": 8, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "My little brother opened the new jigsaw puzzle and, before anyone could stop him, turned the box upside down and <mark data-word=\"scatter\">scattered</mark> a thousand pieces over the carpet. Then he looked at us, very proud, as if he had already done the hardest part.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 89, "w": 8, "slot": 9, "also": [20], "text": "\"We shall see.\" He pushed past the servant and rushed into the drawing-room, followed by the King and myself. The furniture was <mark data-word=\"scatter\">scattered</mark> about in every direction, with dismantled <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelves</mark> and open drawers, as if the lady had hurriedly ransacked them before her flight. Holmes rushed at the bell-pull, tore back a small sliding shutter, and, plunging in his hand, pulled out a photograph and a letter.", "source": "The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 90, "w": 9, "slot": 0, "also": [41], "text": "In the meantime the astonished team-dogs had burst out of their nests only to be set upon by the <mark data-word=\"fierce\">fierce</mark> invaders. Never had Buck seen such dogs. It seemed as though their bones would burst through their skins. They were mere skeletons, draped <mark data-word=\"loose\">loosely</mark> in draggled hides, with blazing eyes and slavered fangs.", "source": "The Call of the Wild — Jack London"},
  {"id": 91, "w": 9, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "The farmer kept a <mark data-word=\"fierce\">fierce</mark> dog by the gate. It barked and showed its teeth at anyone who came near, and the postman refused to bring letters to the house. Only the farmer's little daughter could walk past it; with her it rolled onto its back like a puppy.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 92, "w": 9, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "That night a <mark data-word=\"fierce\">fierce</mark> storm hit the coast. The wind tore tiles from the roofs, pulled trees out of the ground and threw boats onto the road. In the morning people walked through the streets in silence, looking at what one night had done.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 93, "w": 9, "slot": 3, "also": [96], "text": "The two teams had hated each other for years, and the match was <mark data-word=\"fierce\">fierce</mark> from the first minute. Players shouted, pushed and fell. The referee showed four yellow cards before half-time, and by the end both teams were too tired to celebrate or <mark data-word=\"complain\">complain</mark>.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 94, "w": 9, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The door was open. Beyond, in the dimly lighted room, sat Leslie Moore, with her arms flung out on the table and her head bent upon them. She was weeping horribly — with low, <mark data-word=\"fierce\">fierce</mark>, choking sobs, as if some agony in her soul were trying to tear itself out. An old black dog was sitting by her, his nose resting on his lap, his big doggish eyes full of mute, imploring sympathy and devotion. Anne drew back in dismay.", "source": "Anne's House of Dreams — L. M. Montgomery"},
  {"id": 95, "w": 9, "slot": 5, "also": [53], "text": "A mother bear is gentle with her cubs but <mark data-word=\"fierce\">fierce</mark> with everyone else. If you come between her and her young, she will attack without <mark data-word=\"warn\">warning</mark>. That is why the park rangers tell visitors to make noise on the path and never to walk in the forest alone.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 96, "w": 9, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The merchant took his hat from a rack by the door and prepared to depart for the evening. At the door he stopped and glared back. So <mark data-word=\"fierce\">fierce</mark> was his manner that Louise was frightened and ran upstairs to her own room. The daughters began to speak of their own affairs.", "source": "Winesburg, Ohio: A Group of Tales of Ohio Small Town Life — Sherwood Anderson"},
  {"id": 97, "w": 9, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "Mr. Havisham knew his hard, <mark data-word=\"fierce\">fierce</mark> ways by heart, and he was thinking of him as he looked out of the window into the narrow, quiet street. And there rose in his mind, in sharp contrast, the picture of the cheery, handsome little fellow sitting in the big chair and telling his story of his friends, Dick and the apple-woman, in his generous, innocent, honest way.", "source": "Little Lord Fauntleroy — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 98, "w": 9, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The Premier sprang to his feet with that quick, <mark data-word=\"fierce\">fierce</mark> gleam of his deep-set eyes before which a Cabinet has cowered. \"I am not accustomed, sir,\" he began, but mastered his anger and resumed his seat. For a minute or more we all sat in silence.", "source": "The Return of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 99, "w": 9, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "In August the heat in the desert town was <mark data-word=\"fierce\">fierce</mark>. By ten in the morning the streets were empty, the shops were closed, and even the dogs lay in the shade without moving. Only at sunset did people come out, to sit on their doorsteps and wait for the cool night air.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 100, "w": 10, "slot": 0, "also": [13], "text": "The gate had not been opened for years. It was <mark data-word=\"rusty\">rusty</mark> at the hinges and it screamed when we pushed it. Behind it stood an iron barrel; I knocked on the side and the sound was <mark data-word=\"hollow\">hollow</mark>, so we knew it was empty and rolled it out of the way.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 101, "w": 10, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "The tailor thought, \"It gets worse and worse! No one can endure that?\" and threw his bundle on his back, and went forth. When he came to the <mark data-word=\"hollow\">hollow</mark> tree, he sat down and hung his head. The bees came flying out, and the Queen-bee asked him if he had a stiff neck, since he held his head so awry?", "source": "Household Tales by Brothers Grimm — Jacob Grimm"},
  {"id": 102, "w": 10, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The old tree by the river was <mark data-word=\"hollow\">hollow</mark>. Lightning had struck it years ago, and now its trunk was an empty tube, big enough for a child to stand inside. We used it as our secret house, and once we found an owl sleeping at the very top.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 103, "w": 10, "slot": 3, "also": [94], "text": "Chocolate eggs at Easter are <mark data-word=\"hollow\">hollow</mark>, which always disappointed my little brother. He would bite into one, <mark data-word=\"expect\">expecting</mark> a mouthful of chocolate, and find only air and a tiny plastic toy. 'They're cheating,' he said, and ate it anyway.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 104, "w": 10, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Bamboo grows fast and tall, and its stems are <mark data-word=\"hollow\">hollow</mark>, so they are light but very strong. In some villages people cut them into pipes to carry water down from the hills, and children make simple flutes from the thinnest pieces.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 105, "w": 10, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The detective knocked on the wall with his knuckles. In most places it sounded solid, but near the fireplace the sound changed. 'It's <mark data-word=\"hollow\">hollow</mark> here,' he said. 'There is a space behind this wall.' They took out a few bricks and found a small room, empty except for an old wooden box.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 106, "w": 10, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "A bird's bones are <mark data-word=\"hollow\">hollow</mark>, full of air instead of heavy marrow. That is one reason birds can fly: their skeleton is much lighter than ours. If you break a chicken bone after dinner, you can see the empty tube inside.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 107, "w": 10, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The children scooped out the inside of a big pumpkin until it was <mark data-word=\"hollow\">hollow</mark>, then cut eyes and a mouth into its side. When they put a candle inside, the empty pumpkin glowed orange, and its face grinned at everyone who came up the path.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 108, "w": 10, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The old statue in the square was <mark data-word=\"hollow\">hollow</mark>, and for three years a gang of thieves hid stolen rings inside it. People walked past it every day without knowing. The rings were only found when a boy kicked a ball against the statue and heard something rattle inside.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 109, "w": 10, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "When you are really hungry, your stomach feels <mark data-word=\"hollow\">hollow</mark>, as if there is nothing inside you at all. After twelve hours of walking in the hills, that is exactly how I felt, and the first bowl of soup in the village tasted better than any meal I have ever eaten.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 110, "w": 11, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "I forgot to hang the washing out last night, and this morning my shirt was still <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark>. Not wet enough to drip, but cold and heavy against my skin. I wore it anyway, and I shivered all the way to the bus stop.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 111, "w": 11, "slot": 1, "also": [14], "text": "After the funeral the house stayed cold and <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark> for weeks, because nobody thought to light the stove. My mother's <mark data-word=\"grief\">grief</mark> was a quiet one. She cooked, she washed, she answered questions — and then once, in the middle of a Tuesday, she sat down on the kitchen floor and stayed there.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 112, "w": 11, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The cellar was cold and <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark>. Water ran down the walls in thin lines, and everything we kept there — books, boxes, old shoes — smelled of mould after a month. In the end we carried everything upstairs and left the cellar to the spiders.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 113, "w": 11, "slot": 3, "also": [29], "text": "The pills were too big and the water was warm. I had to <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallow</mark> twice and it still felt like a stone going down. Outside the window the morning was grey and <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark>, and I sat on the edge of the bed for a minute before I could stand up.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 114, "w": 11, "slot": 4, "also": [47], "text": "Rain was the only real <mark data-word=\"threat\">threat</mark> to the schedule. Three wet days together and the whole floor stayed <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark>, and nothing could be painted, and we sat in the van reading the same newspaper until somebody sent us home.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 115, "w": 11, "slot": 5, "also": [96], "text": "\"I can never do that,\" said Anne determinedly and darkly. \"You can punish me in any way you like, Marilla. You can shut me up in a dark, <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark> dungeon inhabited by snakes and toads and feed me only on bread and water and I shall not <mark data-word=\"complain\">complain</mark>.\"", "source": "Anne of Green Gables — L. M. Montgomery"},
  {"id": 116, "w": 11, "slot": 6, "also": [30], "text": "After her swim she <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark> her <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark> hair in a towel and sat in the sun. By the time she had finished her book, it was dry and warm, and full of sand.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 117, "w": 11, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "I was walking alone in the garden, one evening, about a fortnight afterwards. I remember that evening well. It was the second in Mr. Micawber's week of suspense. There had been rain all day, and there was a <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark> feeling in the air.", "source": "David Copperfield — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 118, "w": 11, "slot": 8, "also": [32], "text": "'If he could <mark data-word=\"lean\">lean</mark> upon anybody's arm, he would come to me, I know,' he said, returning into the room. 'He always came into the garden to say good night. But perhaps his illness has only just taken a favourable turn, and it's too late for him to come out, for it's very <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark> and there's a heavy dew.", "source": "The Old Curiosity Shop — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 119, "w": 11, "slot": 9, "also": [31], "text": "'Wipe the table with a <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark> cloth, not a wet one,' my grandmother told me. 'Too much water and the old wood will go white.' She showed me how to <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeeze</mark> the cloth over the sink until only a few drops came out, and then how to dry the table straight after.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 120, "w": 12, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "I am terribly <mark data-word=\"clumsy\">clumsy</mark> in the mornings. Yesterday I dropped a cup, knocked the milk off the table and walked into the bathroom door — all before seven o'clock. My wife now makes the coffee herself and tells me to sit down and not touch anything.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 121, "w": 12, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "The puppy was big for his age and very <mark data-word=\"clumsy\">clumsy</mark>. His paws were too large for his body, and when he ran he tripped over them and rolled across the grass. He knocked over his water bowl every time he drank from it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 122, "w": 12, "slot": 2, "also": [3], "text": "'Sorry, sorry!' said the new waiter. He was <mark data-word=\"clumsy\">clumsy</mark> on his first day: he dropped a fork, <mark data-word=\"spill\">spilled</mark> water on a customer and broke two plates in the kitchen. By the end of the week, though, he could carry four plates at once without a single accident.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 123, "w": 12, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "My thick winter gloves made my fingers <mark data-word=\"clumsy\">clumsy</mark>. I tried to put the key into the lock three times and dropped it into the snow twice. In the end I took the gloves off, opened the door quickly and ran inside with my hands under my arms.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 124, "w": 12, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The baby took her first steps in the kitchen — four <mark data-word=\"clumsy\">clumsy</mark> steps, her arms up, swaying from side to side like a sailor on a ship. Then she sat down hard and looked surprised. We all clapped, and she laughed and tried again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 125, "w": 12, "slot": 5, "also": [92], "text": "In the dance class I was the most <mark data-word=\"clumsy\">clumsy</mark> student. I stepped on my partner's feet, turned left when everybody turned right, and once fell over my own shoes. The teacher was patient, but I <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticed</mark> that she never chose me to show the class a new step.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 126, "w": 12, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Everyone in our family knows that Dad is <mark data-word=\"clumsy\">clumsy</mark>. When he helps in the kitchen, something always breaks. So at Christmas we give him the safest job there is: sitting at the table and cutting the bread. Last year he cut his finger.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 127, "w": 12, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The bear looked slow and <mark data-word=\"clumsy\">clumsy</mark> as it crossed the road, rolling from side to side on its heavy legs. But when a fish jumped in the river, it moved so fast that I could hardly see it. A moment later the fish was in its mouth.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 128, "w": 12, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "When I was twelve I grew fifteen centimetres in one year, and suddenly I was <mark data-word=\"clumsy\">clumsy</mark>. My arms and legs seemed too long for me. I hit my head on doors, knocked glasses off tables and fell up the stairs. My mother said I would grow into my body, and after a year or so, I did.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 129, "w": 12, "slot": 9, "also": [20], "text": "The <mark data-word=\"clumsy\">clumsy</mark> tourist turned round with his big rucksack and hit the glass <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelf</mark> behind him, and twenty little painted cups fell to the floor. The shop owner closed her eyes for a moment. Then she smiled a tight smile and said, 'That will be ninety euros, please.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 130, "w": 13, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "We found my grandfather's old bicycle in the shed. The chain was <mark data-word=\"rusty\">rusty</mark>, the wheels had turned brown and the bell would not ring. It took us a whole weekend with oil and sandpaper, but by Sunday evening it was shining again, and my sister rode it round the block.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 131, "w": 13, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "The gate at the bottom of the garden was so <mark data-word=\"rusty\">rusty</mark> that it screamed every time someone opened it. Orange flakes of old metal came off on your hands. 'Nobody has painted it for forty years,' said the neighbour. 'The rain has been eating it slowly.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 132, "w": 13, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "'Don't step on that <mark data-word=\"rusty\">rusty</mark> nail!' my mother shouted, but it was too late. The nail went through my shoe and into my foot. At the hospital the doctor gave me an injection and explained that old brown metal can carry dirt deep into a wound.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 133, "w": 13, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The ship had lain on the beach for fifty years. Its sides were <mark data-word=\"rusty\">rusty</mark> and full of holes, and the sea air had turned the white paint to brown and orange. Children climbed on it in summer, and every winter the waves carried away another piece.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 134, "w": 13, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The old box of tools had stood in the wet garage all winter. When I opened it, the scissors were <mark data-word=\"rusty\">rusty</mark> and would not close, and the knives were covered with brown spots. 'You must keep metal dry,' said my father, 'or water will ruin it.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 135, "w": 13, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "After a month at sea the anchor chain was <mark data-word=\"rusty\">rusty</mark> from end to end. The sailors spent a whole morning scraping off the brown layer with wire brushes and painting the chain black again, so that the salt water could not eat into the metal.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 136, "w": 13, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "\"Coverlet dingy and worn, blanket thin, sheets patched and ragged,\" he said. \"What a bed for a child to sleep in — and in a house which calls itself respectable! There has not been a fire in that grate for many a day,\" glancing at the <mark data-word=\"rusty\">rusty</mark> fireplace.", "source": "A Little Princess — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 137, "w": 13, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "Behind the farm stood an old <mark data-word=\"rusty\">rusty</mark> tractor, half hidden in the long grass. Nobody had driven it for years. Its red paint had turned brown, and a small tree was growing up through the seat. My grandfather said he would repair it one day, but we all knew he never would.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 138, "w": 13, "slot": 8, "also": [39, 59], "text": "The <mark data-word=\"rusty\">rusty</mark> old swing in the park creaked so loudly that you could hear it from the street. Its chains were brown and <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark>, and they left orange marks on your hands. In spring the town finally <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaced</mark> it with a new one, and the park became strangely quiet.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 139, "w": 13, "slot": 9, "also": [87], "text": "At last they reached the passage on the ground floor, where the snoring of Mr Quilp and his legal friend sounded more terrible in their ears than the roars of lions. The <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolts</mark> of the door were <mark data-word=\"rusty\">rusty</mark>, and difficult to unfasten without noise. When they were all drawn back, it was found to be locked, and worst of all, the key was gone.", "source": "The Old Curiosity Shop — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 140, "w": 14, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "\"He was not wicked, my dear,\" she answered. \"He did not really lose your papa's money. He only thought he had lost it; and because he loved him so much his <mark data-word=\"grief\">grief</mark> made him so ill that for a time he was not in his right mind. He almost died of brain fever, and long before he began to recover your poor papa was dead.\"", "source": "A Little Princess — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 141, "w": 14, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "I was in Peggotty's arms before I got to the door, and she took me into the house. Her <mark data-word=\"grief\">grief</mark> burst out when she first saw me; but she controlled it soon, and spoke in whispers, and walked softly, as if the dead could be disturbed. She had not been in bed, I found, for a long time. She sat up at night still, and watched.", "source": "David Copperfield — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 142, "w": 14, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "A woman, young and beautiful, was lying dead upon the bed. Her calm pale face, with dim, wide-opened blue eyes, looked upward from amid a great tangle of golden hair. At the foot of the bed, half sitting, half kneeling, his face buried in the clothes, was a young man, whose frame was racked by his sobs. So absorbed was he by his bitter <mark data-word=\"grief\">grief</mark>, that he never looked up until Holmes's hand was on his shoulder.", "source": "The Return of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 143, "w": 14, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "When their old dog died, the children's <mark data-word=\"grief\">grief</mark> surprised their parents. They did not want to eat, they would not go to school, and every evening they sat by his empty basket. Their father dug a grave under the apple tree, and each child put a flower on it. Slowly, over many weeks, the sadness became softer.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 144, "w": 14, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "When he was aware of the voice, he looked down and reined in his horse. He looked into the herd's face, and then put his hands before his eyes as if he were trying to remember something, but he soon rode onwards and was out of sight. \"Alas!\" said she, \"he no longer knows me,\" and her <mark data-word=\"grief\">grief</mark> was ever greater.", "source": "Household Tales by Brothers Grimm — Jacob Grimm"},
  {"id": 145, "w": 14, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "In an instant the poor dear fellow was overwhelmed with <mark data-word=\"grief\">grief</mark>. It seemed to me that all that he had of late been suffering in silence found a vent at once. He grew quite hysterical, and raising his open hands, beat his palms together in a perfect agony of <mark data-word=\"grief\">grief</mark>. He stood up and then sat down again, and the tears rained down his cheeks. I felt an infinite pity for him, and opened my arms unthinkingly.", "source": "Dracula — Bram Stoker"},
  {"id": 146, "w": 14, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "There was once a King who had an illness, and no one believed that he would come out of it with his life. He had three sons who were much distressed about it, and went down into the palace-garden and wept. There they met an old man who inquired as to the cause of their <mark data-word=\"grief\">grief</mark>. They told him that their father was so ill that he would most certainly die, for nothing seemed to cure him.", "source": "Household Tales by Brothers Grimm — Jacob Grimm"},
  {"id": 147, "w": 14, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "If my <mark data-word=\"grief\">grief</mark> were selfish, I did not know it to be so. I mourned for my child-wife, taken from her blooming world, so young. I mourned for him who might have won the love and admiration of thousands, as he had won mine long ago.", "source": "David Copperfield — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 148, "w": 14, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "My grandmother lived for fifteen years after my grandfather died, and her <mark data-word=\"grief\">grief</mark> never completely left her. Every morning she put two cups on the table out of habit, then quietly put one back. 'You don't stop missing someone,' she told me. 'You just learn to carry it.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 149, "w": 14, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The whole village came to the funeral of the young fisherman who had drowned in the storm. His mother stood at the front, too full of <mark data-word=\"grief\">grief</mark> to cry, holding his old cap in both hands. Nobody knew what to say to her, so the women simply stood close around her, one hand on her shoulder.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 150, "w": 15, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "The following evening the Mole, who had risen late and taken things very easy all day, was sitting on the bank fishing, when the Rat, who had been looking up his friends and gossiping, came <mark data-word=\"stroll\">strolling</mark> along to find him. \"Heard the news?\" he said.", "source": "The Wind in the Willows — Kenneth Grahame"},
  {"id": 151, "w": 15, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "We went home early in the evening. It was a very fine evening, and my mother and he had another <mark data-word=\"stroll\">stroll</mark> by the sweetbriar, while I was sent in to get my tea. When he was gone, my mother asked me all about the day I had had, and what they had said and done.", "sense": "a slow walk for pleasure (noun)", "source": "David Copperfield — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 152, "w": 15, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "'Thank you. Allow me.' He placed a chair for her, but remained himself carelessly lounging against the table. 'I left my servant at the railway looking after the luggage—very heavy train and vast quantity of it in the van—and <mark data-word=\"stroll\">strolled</mark> on, looking about me.", "source": "Hard Times — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 153, "w": 15, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "Now, the whole place was, or it should have been, quite as delightful a place as ever; and yet it did not impress me in the same way. I felt rather disappointed with it. Perhaps it was because little Em'ly was not at home. I knew the way by which she would come, and presently found myself <mark data-word=\"stroll\">strolling</mark> along the path to meet her.", "source": "David Copperfield — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 154, "w": 15, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "On Sunday afternoons my grandparents liked to <mark data-word=\"stroll\">stroll</mark> along the river. They never hurried and never had a plan; they walked slowly, arm in arm, stopping to look at the boats and to feed the ducks, and came home when they felt like tea.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 155, "w": 15, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "After dinner in the little Italian town, everybody goes out to <mark data-word=\"stroll\">stroll</mark>. Families walk slowly up and down the main street, eating ice cream, greeting their neighbours and looking in the shop windows. Nobody is going anywhere in particular — that is the whole point.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 156, "w": 15, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Before breakfast in the morning, Arthur walked out to look about him. As the morning was fine and he had an hour on his hands, he crossed the river by the ferry, and <mark data-word=\"stroll\">strolled</mark> along a footpath through some meadows.", "source": "Little Dorrit — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 157, "w": 15, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "We had two hours before our train, so we <mark data-word=\"stroll\">strolled</mark> through the old part of the city with no map, turning wherever the street looked pretty. We found a tiny bakery, a square full of pigeons and a man playing the violin under a bridge. We almost missed the train.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 158, "w": 15, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The tourists <mark data-word=\"stroll\">strolled</mark> along the beach at sunset, their shoes in their hands, stopping now and then to pick up a shell. Behind them a runner went past, red-faced and breathing hard — the only person on the sand who was moving fast.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 159, "w": 15, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "'Don't run, we have plenty of time,' said my mother. 'Let's just <mark data-word=\"stroll\">stroll</mark>.' So we walked slowly down the hill to the market, looking at the gardens and the cats on the walls, and for once nobody in the family was in a hurry.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 160, "w": 16, "slot": 0, "also": [18], "text": "My father was a <mark data-word=\"blunt\">blunt</mark> man. He did not say 'perhaps' or 'we shall see'; he said yes or no and went back to work. On my last evening he made me <mark data-word=\"vow\">vow</mark> that I would finish what I had started, and I said the words out loud so that they would cost me something.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 161, "w": 16, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "The King had a daughter who was very beautiful, but she was also very strange. She had made a <mark data-word=\"vow\">vow</mark> to take no one as her lord and husband who did not promise to let himself be buried alive with her if she died first. \"If he loves me with all his heart,\" said she, \"of what use will life be to him afterwards?\" On her side she would do the same, and if he died first, would go down to the grave with him.", "sense": "a serious promise (noun)", "source": "Household Tales by Brothers Grimm — Jacob Grimm"},
  {"id": 162, "w": 16, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "On their wedding day they stood in front of their families and <mark data-word=\"vow\">vowed</mark> to love each other in good times and bad. Forty years later, in a small hospital room, he held her hand every afternoon and read her the newspaper. He had kept his promise.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 163, "w": 16, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "After losing his job because he always came in late, Sam <mark data-word=\"vow\">vowed</mark> that he would never be late again. He bought two alarm clocks and put them on the other side of the room, so that he had to get out of bed to stop them. At his new job he was always the first to arrive.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 164, "w": 16, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "When he was nine, Leo nearly drowned in the lake, and that evening he <mark data-word=\"vow\">vowed</mark> to learn to swim properly. He went to the pool every Saturday that winter, even when he was ill and even when it snowed. The next summer he swam across the same lake while his parents watched from the boat.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 165, "w": 16, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "'I will come back for you,' the young soldier <mark data-word=\"vow\">vowed</mark> as he left the village. 'Whatever happens, I will come back.' The war lasted four years. But one spring morning the girl heard the gate open, and there he was, thinner and older, keeping his promise.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 166, "w": 16, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The old fisherman had watched the factory poison his river for years. When it finally closed and the water ran clean again, he <mark data-word=\"vow\">vowed</mark> to protect it for the rest of his life. He kept his word: every Saturday for twenty years he walked the banks, picking up bottles and chasing away anyone who dumped rubbish.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 167, "w": 16, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "My mother <mark data-word=\"vow\">vowed</mark> never to smoke again after her father died of a lung disease. She threw her cigarettes into the stove that same evening and never bought another packet. 'A promise to the dead,' she said, 'is the only kind you cannot break.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 168, "w": 16, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Before the race the captain made the whole team <mark data-word=\"vow\">vow</mark> not to give up, however far behind they were. In the last kilometre they were fourth, and every muscle hurt. But nobody stopped, because nobody wanted to be the first to break the promise. They finished second.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 169, "w": 16, "slot": 9, "also": [94], "text": "My sister made me <mark data-word=\"vow\">vow</mark> that I would not tell our mother about the car until she had sold it herself. I kept that promise for eleven days, which is longer than anybody in the family had <mark data-word=\"expect\">expected</mark> of me.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 170, "w": 17, "slot": 0, "also": [19], "text": "By eight o'clock we were <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark> and nobody wanted to talk. Then Marek started to <mark data-word=\"mock\">mock</mark> the way the new foreman walks — the shoulders, the little steps — and even the quiet men laughed until they had to hold on to the table. We forgot how tired we were for ten minutes.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 171, "w": 17, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "The soldiers came back from the march grey with dust and <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark> to the bone. They had walked for three days with heavy packs, and some of them fell asleep sitting up, their boots still on, before anyone could bring them food.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 172, "w": 17, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "While he smoked out his cigar in peace, she made his bed, and put the small room in order for his repose. Being <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark> then, owing to the advanced hour and his emotions, he came out of his chair to bless her and wish her Good night. All this time he had never once thought of her dress, her shoes, her need of anything. No other person upon earth, save herself, could have been so unmindful of her wants.", "source": "Little Dorrit — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 173, "w": 17, "slot": 3, "also": [32], "text": "The priest passed his hand several times over his face. Then, resting his forehead against his hand, he <mark data-word=\"lean\">leaned</mark> towards the grating and, with eyes still averted, spoke slowly. His voice was <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark> and old.", "source": "A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 174, "w": 17, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "After twelve hours on the hospital ward, the young doctor was <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark>. Her feet ached, her eyes burned, and when she sat down in the staff room she could not remember whether she had eaten. She closed her eyes for a second, and woke up an hour later with a blanket over her.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 175, "w": 17, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The master had retired to rest before we came in. Cathy stole to his room to inquire how he was; he had fallen asleep. She returned, and asked me to sit with her in the library. We took our tea together; and afterwards she lay down on the rug, and told me not to talk, for she was <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark>.", "source": "Wuthering Heights — Emily Brontë"},
  {"id": 176, "w": 17, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The travellers reached the inn late at night, <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark> after a long day on the mountain road. They were too tired to talk and almost too tired to eat. As soon as they had finished their soup, they climbed the stairs and fell asleep in their clothes.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 177, "w": 17, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "By the end of the harvest the farmers were <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark>. They had worked in the fields from sunrise to sunset for three weeks, and their backs and hands ached. On the last evening they sat together at a long table, too tired to dance, but glad the work was done.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 178, "w": 17, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Climbing the last few hundred metres to the top of the mountain, I grew more and more <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark>. Every step felt heavier than the one before, and I had to stop to breathe after every ten. But when I reached the top and saw the whole valley below me, the tiredness disappeared.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 179, "w": 17, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "At last he arose in a <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark> manner, as though all the resilience had gone out of his body, and proceeded to fasten the dogs to the sled. He passed a rope over his shoulder, a man-trace, and pulled with the dogs. He did not go far.", "source": "White Fang — Jack London"},
  {"id": 180, "w": 18, "slot": 0, "also": [43], "text": "There is a difference between <mark data-word=\"blunt\">blunt</mark> and <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark>, and it took me a year to hear it. Dave would say 'no, do that again' with no soft words around it, and that is not <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark>. The man who never learned my name in eight months — that was something else.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 181, "w": 18, "slot": 1, "also": [50], "text": "'This knife is so <mark data-word=\"blunt\">blunt</mark> it wouldn't cut butter,' said my father, pushing the bread knife across the table. He was right: I had been sawing at the loaf for a minute and had only crushed it. He took the knife to the garage and came back with an <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark> so sharp that it went through the bread like paper.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 182, "w": 18, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "My grandmother was always <mark data-word=\"blunt\">blunt</mark>. When I showed her my new haircut, she looked at it for a long time and said, 'It was better before.' My mother was shocked, but I laughed. At least with Grandma you always knew exactly what she thought.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 183, "w": 18, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The old axe was <mark data-word=\"blunt\">blunt</mark>, and every blow just bounced off the log. After twenty minutes of work I had made only a small mark in the wood. My neighbour watched me over the fence, then came round with a stone and showed me how to sharpen it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 184, "w": 18, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Children's scissors have <mark data-word=\"blunt\">blunt</mark>, rounded ends, so that nobody gets hurt if a child runs with them or drops them. They cut paper well enough, but you cannot poke anyone with them — which is exactly what a teacher of thirty six-year-olds wants.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 185, "w": 18, "slot": 5, "also": [96], "text": "'I'll be <mark data-word=\"blunt\">blunt</mark> with you,' said the manager. 'Your work is late, your reports are full of mistakes, and two customers have <mark data-word=\"complain\">complained</mark>. If nothing changes in the next month, we will have to let you go.' Tom had hoped for a softer conversation, but at least he knew where he stood.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 186, "w": 18, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "\"Why don't you say you'd have a splendid, wise, good husband and some angelic little children? You know your castle wouldn't be perfect without,\" said <mark data-word=\"blunt\">blunt</mark> Jo, who had no tender fancies yet, and rather scorned romance, except in books.", "source": "Little Women — Louisa May Alcott"},
  {"id": 187, "w": 18, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The doctor was kind, but <mark data-word=\"blunt\">blunt</mark>. She did not hide the truth behind soft words. 'If you don't lose weight, your knees will not carry you in five years,' she told my uncle. He was angry for a day. Then he started walking to work.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 188, "w": 18, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The knife in that kitchen was so <mark data-word=\"blunt\">blunt</mark> you could press it into a tomato and nothing at all would happen. I sharpened it on the back step one Sunday, and my aunt said the whole business of cooking had changed for her.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 189, "w": 18, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "He is a <mark data-word=\"blunt\">blunt</mark> man. When I asked whether the roof could wait one more winter, he did not say 'perhaps' or 'we shall see'. He said no, and then he told me what it would cost.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 190, "w": 19, "slot": 0, "also": [65], "text": "When Tom first came to school with his thick glasses, some of the older boys <mark data-word=\"mock\">mocked</mark> him. They <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretended</mark> they could not see, walked into walls and laughed loudly whenever he passed. Tom said nothing, but at home he cried into his pillow.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 191, "w": 19, "slot": 1, "also": [97], "text": "'Please don't <mark data-word=\"mock\">mock</mark> your sister's singing,' said Dad. 'She is only learning, and laughing at her will not make her better — it will only make her stop.' My brother stopped copying her high notes, looked down at his plate, and <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologised</mark> to her after dinner.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 192, "w": 19, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "My accent was strong when I moved to London, and a few people at work <mark data-word=\"mock\">mocked</mark> it. They repeated my words in a silly voice and laughed together behind my back. One day my manager heard them. She did not shout; she only asked them, very quietly, how many languages they spoke. Nobody laughed after that.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 193, "w": 19, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "In the old story, the other animals <mark data-word=\"mock\">mocked</mark> the tortoise because he was so slow. 'You'll arrive next year!' laughed the hare. But the tortoise kept walking, step by step, while the hare fell asleep under a tree, and at the end of the race it was the tortoise who crossed the line first.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 194, "w": 19, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Oh, how they enjoyed themselves, ridiculing that young man! For five good minutes they stood there, shouting ribaldry at him, deriding him, <mark data-word=\"mock\">mocking</mark> him, jeering at him. They peppered him with stale jokes, they even made a few new ones and threw at him.", "source": "Three men in a boat (to say nothing of the dog) — Jerome K. Jerome"},
  {"id": 195, "w": 19, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The comedian on television <mark data-word=\"mock\">mocked</mark> the prime minister every week. He copied his voice, his walk and his favourite phrases, and the audience roared with laughter. The prime minister's friends found it cruel; everyone else waited all week for the show.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 196, "w": 19, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "When Maria said she wanted to be a pilot, her classmates <mark data-word=\"mock\">mocked</mark> her. 'Girls don't fly planes,' one of them laughed, and the others joined in. Twenty years later she flew one of them home from his holiday, and she made sure to say hello to him over the speaker.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 197, "w": 19, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "'Don't <mark data-word=\"mock\">mock</mark> people who are learning your language,' my teacher always said. 'Every mistake they make in yours is a mistake you would make in theirs. Laughing at them is easy. Helping them is not.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 198, "w": 19, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The first time I tried to ride a horse, I fell off in front of the whole family. My brothers laughed and <mark data-word=\"mock\">mocked</mark> me for weeks, calling me 'the flying cowboy' at every meal. I got back on the horse anyway, and by the end of the summer I rode better than any of them.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 199, "w": 19, "slot": 9, "also": [22, 23], "text": "The <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> <mark data-word=\"mock\">mocked</mark> the young runner when she fell at the start, <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistling</mark> and laughing unkindly. But she stood up, brushed the dirt from her knees and ran. She did not win, but when she crossed the line the same people stood and clapped for her.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 200, "w": 20, "slot": 0, "also": [22], "text": "The little shop by the station has one <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelf</mark> of bread and nothing else worth buying, but at seven in the morning there is a <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> inside it. Everybody who starts work at eight stops there, and everybody buys the same thing.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 201, "w": 20, "slot": 1, "also": [28], "text": "My room is <mark data-word=\"tidy\">tidy</mark> because it is small. One <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelf</mark>, one chair, one bag under the bed. When you own five things, mess is a decision and not an accident. I met men in that building who had lived there eleven years and never unpacked.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 202, "w": 20, "slot": 2, "also": [53], "text": "Jake and Otto served us to the last. They moved us into town, put down the carpets in our new house, made <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelves</mark> and cupboards for grandmother's kitchen, and seemed loath to leave us. But at last they went, without <mark data-word=\"warn\">warning</mark>. Those two fellows had been faithful to us through sun and storm, had given us things that cannot be bought in any market in the world.", "source": "My Ántonia — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 203, "w": 20, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "They were in a book, and it happened to be in the next room—our sitting-room, dividing Ada's chamber from mine. I took a candle and went softly in to fetch it from its <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelf</mark>. After I had it in my hand, I saw my beautiful darling, through the open door, lying asleep, and I stole in to kiss her.", "source": "Bleak House — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 204, "w": 20, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The top <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelf</mark> in our kitchen is so high that I have to stand on a chair to reach it. That is where my mother keeps the chocolate, the good glasses and anything she does not want the children to find. When we were small, we thought that <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelf</mark> was the most important place in the house.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 205, "w": 20, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "He was a very good old fellow, in his familiar way; and having stirred the vestry fire, he looked round the <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelves</mark> of registers for a particular volume. 'Here you are, you see,' he said, taking it down and turning the leaves. 'Here you'll find yourself, as large as life.", "source": "Little Dorrit — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 206, "w": 20, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Dr. Grimesby Roylott's chamber was larger than that of his step-daughter, but was as plainly furnished. A camp-bed, a small wooden <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelf</mark> full of books, mostly of a technical character, an armchair beside the bed, a plain wooden chair against the wall, a round table, and a large iron safe were the principal things which met the eye. Holmes walked slowly round and examined each and all of them with the keenest interest.", "source": "The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 207, "w": 20, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "When Dorothy was left alone she began to feel hungry. So she went to the cupboard and cut herself some bread, which she spread with butter. She gave some to Toto, and taking a pail from the <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelf</mark> she carried it down to the little brook and filled it with clear, sparkling water. Toto ran over to the trees and began to bark at the birds sitting there.", "source": "The Wonderful Wizard of Oz — L. Frank Baum"},
  {"id": 208, "w": 20, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "He remembered the books of poetry upon his <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelves</mark> at home. He had bought them in his bachelor days and many an evening, as he sat in the little room off the hall, he had been tempted to take one down from the bookshelf and read out something to his wife. But shyness had always held him back; and so the books had remained on their <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelves</mark>. At times he repeated lines to himself and this consoled him.", "source": "Dubliners — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 209, "w": 20, "slot": 9, "also": [2], "text": "Frances said, when she told grandmother and me about this scene, that every pan and plate and cup on the <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelves</mark> <mark data-word=\"tremble\">trembled</mark> when her mother walked out of the kitchen. Mrs. Harling declared bitterly that she wished she had never let herself get fond of Ántonia.", "source": "My Ántonia — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 210, "w": 21, "slot": 0, "also": [23], "text": "He <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistled</mark> all the way home from the shop, which is exactly how his mother knew. A boy who has done nothing does not <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistle</mark> like that. She waited two days, and then he told her about the broken window himself, because feeling <mark data-word=\"guilty\">guilty</mark> is heavier than being punished.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 211, "w": 21, "slot": 1, "also": [48], "text": "I cut a pipe forty centimetres too short because I misheard one number. Nobody shouted, which was worse. The <mark data-word=\"shame\">shame</mark> sat with me for two days, and I felt <mark data-word=\"guilty\">guilty</mark> every time somebody walked past that wall.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 212, "w": 21, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "I ate the last piece of my sister's birthday cake while she was at school. It tasted wonderful for about a minute. Then I felt <mark data-word=\"guilty\">guilty</mark> all afternoon, and when she came home and opened the fridge, I could not look at her. In the end I told her the truth and bought her a new cake with my pocket money.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 213, "w": 21, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The dog knew he had done something wrong. When we came home and found the sofa cushion torn to pieces, he was hiding under the table, looking <mark data-word=\"guilty\">guilty</mark>, with his ears down and his eyes turned away. He did not even come to the door to greet us.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 214, "w": 21, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Anna forgot her best friend's birthday. She remembered two days later and felt so <mark data-word=\"guilty\">guilty</mark> that she could not sleep. The next morning she knocked on her friend's door with flowers, a card and a long apology.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 215, "w": 21, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Every time I skip the gym, I feel a little <mark data-word=\"guilty\">guilty</mark>. I paid for the whole year in January, full of good plans. Now it is May, I have been four times, and the card in my wallet looks at me like a sad dog whenever I open it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 216, "w": 21, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Jack took a chocolate bar from the corner shop without paying. Nobody saw him, but he felt so <mark data-word=\"guilty\">guilty</mark> that he could not eat it, or his dinner either. At eight o'clock he walked back to the shop and put the money on the counter. The old shopkeeper looked at him for a long moment and then simply said, 'Thank you.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 217, "w": 21, "slot": 7, "also": [63, 90], "text": "It was felt to be a reckless extravagance. Paul had only been in an eating-house once or twice in his life, and then only to have a cup of tea and a bun. Most of the people of Bestwood <mark data-word=\"consider\">considered</mark> that tea and bread-and-butter, and perhaps potted beef, was all they could <mark data-word=\"afford\">afford</mark> to eat in Nottingham. Real cooked dinner was <mark data-word=\"consider\">considered</mark> great extravagance. Paul felt rather <mark data-word=\"guilty\">guilty</mark>.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 218, "w": 21, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "My grandfather worked every day of my childhood, and he missed most of my school plays. Many years later he told me that he still felt <mark data-word=\"guilty\">guilty</mark> about it. 'I thought money was the most important thing,' he said. 'I was wrong.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 219, "w": 21, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "When I shouted at my little brother for losing my headphones, he started to cry. An hour later I found them in my own bag. I felt terribly <mark data-word=\"guilty\">guilty</mark>. I went to his room, said sorry, and let him choose the film that evening.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 220, "w": 22, "slot": 0, "also": [45], "text": "For the first months I was <mark data-word=\"anxious\">anxious</mark> every morning before I had even opened my eyes. Not about the work — about the small things: the bus, the <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> at the door, the question somebody might ask me that I would not understand.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 221, "w": 22, "slot": 1, "also": [71], "text": "When the band came on stage, the <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> went wild. Thousands of people jumped, sang and waved their arms, and I was pushed so far forward that my feet <mark data-word=\"barely\">barely</mark> touched the ground. I lost my friends in the first five minutes and found them again only at the bus stop.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 222, "w": 22, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "A <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> of men, a doctor and his assistant, the officers of his regiment, ran up to him. To his misery he felt that he was whole and unhurt. The mare had broken her back, and it was decided to shoot her. Vronsky could not answer questions, could not speak to anyone. He turned, and without picking up his cap that had fallen off, walked away from the race course, not knowing where he was going.", "source": "Anna Karenina — graf Leo Tolstoy"},
  {"id": 223, "w": 22, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "At that moment Polenka forced her way through the <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> at the door. She came in panting from running so fast, took off her kerchief, looked for her mother, went up to her and said, \"She's coming, I met her in the street.\" Her mother made her kneel beside her.", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 224, "w": 22, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "He knew she was there by the rapture and the terror that seized on his heart. She was standing talking to a lady at the opposite end of the ground. There was apparently nothing striking either in her dress or her attitude. But for Levin she was as easy to find in that <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> as a rose among nettles. Everything was made bright by her.", "source": "Anna Karenina — graf Leo Tolstoy"},
  {"id": 225, "w": 22, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "A <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> had gathered outside the bakery by seven in the morning. It was the first day after the holidays, and half the town seemed to want fresh bread at once. People stood shoulder to shoulder on the pavement, chatting, until the door finally opened.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 226, "w": 22, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "At last he reached the turning. He turned down it more dead than alive. Here he was half way to safety, and he understood it; it was less risky because there was a great <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> of people, and he was lost in it like a grain of sand. But all he had suffered had so weakened him that he could scarcely move.", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 227, "w": 22, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "At the end of the match the <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> ran onto the field, and the players disappeared among thousands of happy fans. It took the police half an hour to clear the grass. The young goalkeeper came out with his shirt missing and a huge smile on his face.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 228, "w": 22, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "She stood among the swaying <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> in the station at the North Wall. He held her hand and she knew that he was speaking to her, saying something about the passage over and over again. The station was full of soldiers with brown baggages. Through the wide doors of the sheds she caught a glimpse of the black mass of the boat, lying in beside the quay wall, with illumined portholes. She answered nothing.", "source": "Dubliners — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 229, "w": 22, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "I hate shopping on the Saturday before Christmas. The <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> in the main street is so thick that you cannot walk at your own speed; you move where everyone else moves, like a leaf on a river. Last year I went out for a pair of gloves and came back two hours later with nothing.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 230, "w": 23, "slot": 0, "also": [39], "text": "Louise frightened the farm hand still more by turning and putting her cheek down upon his shoulder. Vaguely she hoped that he like that young man who had stood in the darkness with Mary would put his arms about her and kiss her, but the country boy was only alarmed. He struck the horse with the whip and began to <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistle</mark>. \"The road is <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark>, eh?\" he said loudly.", "source": "Winesburg, Ohio: A Group of Tales of Ohio Small Town Life — Sherwood Anderson"},
  {"id": 231, "w": 23, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "My grandfather could <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistle</mark> any song he heard on the radio. He put two fingers to his lips and blew, and out came a clear, high note like a bird's. I practised for a whole summer and only ever managed a sad little sound, like air going out of a tyre.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 232, "w": 23, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "Dad <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistled</mark> while he cooked, always the same old tune, a little out of key. We knew he was in a good mood when we heard it from the kitchen. On the days he was quiet, we kept away.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 233, "w": 23, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "One evening they were walking down by the canal, and something was troubling him. She knew she had not got him. All the time he <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistled</mark> softly and persistently to himself. She listened, feeling she could learn more from his <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistling</mark> than from his speech.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 234, "w": 23, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "\"You'll find you're always tumbling over the things you've put behind you,\" he said. Then he took his departure to the cowsheds. He felt he had been witty, and his manly pride was high. He <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistled</mark> as he went down the brick track.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 235, "w": 23, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "\"I like you! I like you!\" she cried out, pattering down the walk; and she chirped and tried to <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistle</mark>, which last she did not know how to do in the least. But the robin seemed to be quite satisfied and chirped and <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistled</mark> back at her.", "source": "The Secret Garden — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 236, "w": 23, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The shepherd put his fingers in his mouth and <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistled</mark> — one long, sharp note that we heard across the whole valley. At once the dog far up on the hill stopped, turned and began to drive the sheep down towards us.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 237, "w": 23, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "My little brother is trying to learn to <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistle</mark>. He pushes his lips forward, blows hard and turns red, but only air comes out. Yesterday, for the first time, there was a tiny high sound, and he ran around the house to tell everybody.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 238, "w": 23, "slot": 8, "also": [24], "text": "Walking home alone through the dark park, Lena began to <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistle</mark> a happy tune. She was not happy at all — she was scared of every <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> — but the sound of her own music made the trees feel less empty.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 239, "w": 23, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "When the young man saw the new car, he <mark data-word=\"whistle\">whistled</mark> in surprise — a long note that went up and then down. 'Is that really yours?' he asked. His friend grinned and threw him the keys.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 240, "w": 24, "slot": 0, "also": [67], "text": "The <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> moved with the sun, but she never moved from his side, except to wait upon him. The sun went down and she was still there. She had done her work now, and her hand, faltering on the arm of his chair since its last tending of him, was <mark data-word=\"hesitate\">hesitating</mark> there yet.", "source": "Little Dorrit — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 241, "w": 24, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "I crept forward and looked across at the familiar window. As my eyes fell upon it, I gave a gasp and a cry of amazement. The blind was down, and a strong light was burning in the room. The <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> of a man who was seated in a chair within was thrown in hard, black outline upon the luminous screen of the window.", "source": "The Return of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 242, "w": 24, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "In the late afternoon our <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadows</mark> grew long on the beach, like tall thin giants lying on the sand. My daughter jumped on mine and laughed. 'I caught you!' she shouted, and I moved away, and she chased my <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> all the way back to the car.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 243, "w": 24, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "Minute by minute the time passed. The sun dropped lower in the west and the <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadows</mark> grew deeper under the trees. Pollyanna sat so still she hardly seemed to breathe. A bird alighted fearlessly within reach of her hand, and a squirrel whisked his bushy tail on a tree-branch almost under her nose — yet with his bright little eyes all the while on the motionless dog.", "source": "Pollyanna — Eleanor H. Porter"},
  {"id": 244, "w": 24, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "At noon, when the sun is right above you, your <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> is small, just a dark spot around your feet. In the evening, when the sun is low, it becomes long and thin. Before there were clocks, people used the length of a <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> to tell the time.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 245, "w": 24, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The children made <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> animals on the bedroom wall. With a torch behind their hands, a rabbit appeared, then a dog, then a bird flapping its wings. Their father joined in with a crocodile that ate the rabbit, and nobody went to sleep for another hour.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 246, "w": 24, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "In summer we eat lunch in the <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> of the big walnut tree, where the sun cannot reach us. Even on the hottest day it is cool there. The dog knows it too: at noon he moves from the grass into the dark patch under the branches and falls asleep.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 247, "w": 24, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "As the plane flew over the fields, I could see its <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> moving across the ground below — a small dark cross racing over roads, farms and rivers. When we came down to land, the <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> grew bigger and bigger until it met the plane on the runway.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 248, "w": 24, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "I saw his dark face lit up with a boyish enthusiasm as he gazed about him. The light beat upon him where he stood, but long <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadows</mark> trailed down the walls and hung like a black canopy above him. Barrymore had returned from taking our luggage to our rooms. He stood in front of us now with the subdued manner of a well-trained servant. He was a remarkable-looking man, tall, handsome, with a square black beard and pale, distinguished features.", "source": "The Hound of the Baskervilles — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 249, "w": 24, "slot": 9, "also": [12], "text": "With a look that plainly says, \"You know you have the power to remain if you will,\" she tells him it is not necessary and moves towards a chair. Mr. Tulkinghorn brings it a little forward for her with his <mark data-word=\"clumsy\">clumsy</mark> bow and retires into a window opposite. Interposed between her and the fading light of day in the now quiet street, his <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> falls upon her, and he darkens all before her. Even so does he darken her life.", "source": "Bleak House — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 250, "w": 25, "slot": 0, "also": [27], "text": "Marek had a <mark data-word=\"spare\">spare</mark> kettle that he never used, and he gave it to me without being asked. Two weeks later I had to <mark data-word=\"borrow\">borrow</mark> his iron as well. He said the only rule in that building was that you give a thing back better than you took it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 251, "w": 25, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "'Can I <mark data-word=\"borrow\">borrow</mark> your pen? I'll give it back after the lesson,' whispered Tom. I gave it to him. He did not give it back after the lesson, or the next day, or ever. Since then, when Tom asks to <mark data-word=\"borrow\">borrow</mark> something, I give him my worst pen.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 252, "w": 25, "slot": 2, "also": [86], "text": "We don't have a <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark>, so every autumn my father <mark data-word=\"borrow\">borrows</mark> one from our neighbour to clean the gutters. He always returns it the same afternoon, with a bag of apples from our tree as a thank-you.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 253, "w": 25, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "I <mark data-word=\"borrow\">borrowed</mark> three books from the library and forgot about them for two months. When I finally took them back, the woman at the desk looked at the dates, looked at me, and asked for four euros. It was the most expensive reading I have ever done.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 254, "w": 25, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Captain Hopkins lent me the knife and fork, with his compliments to Mr. Micawber. There was a very dirty lady in his little room, and two wan girls, his daughters, with shock heads of hair. I thought it was better to <mark data-word=\"borrow\">borrow</mark> Captain Hopkins's knife and fork, than Captain Hopkins's comb. The Captain himself was in the last extremity of shabbiness, with large whiskers, and an old, old brown great-coat with no other coat below it.", "source": "David Copperfield — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 255, "w": 25, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "\"No names, please!\" said Holmes, as we knocked at Gilchrist's door. A tall, flaxen-haired, slim young fellow opened it, and made us welcome when he understood our errand. There were some really curious pieces of mediæval domestic architecture within. Holmes was so charmed with one of them that he insisted on drawing it in his notebook, broke his pencil, had to <mark data-word=\"borrow\">borrow</mark> one from our host and finally <mark data-word=\"borrow\">borrowed</mark> a knife to sharpen his own.", "source": "The Return of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 256, "w": 25, "slot": 6, "also": [72], "text": "My sister <mark data-word=\"borrow\">borrows</mark> my clothes without asking. Yesterday I saw my favourite jumper walking down the street — with my sister inside it. She brings things back <mark data-word=\"eventually\">eventually</mark>, but usually with a new stain and a story.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 257, "w": 25, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "When our car broke down, my uncle let us <mark data-word=\"borrow\">borrow</mark> his for a week. We drove it very carefully, washed it before we gave it back and filled the tank. 'You can have it again any time,' he said, looking at the shining car.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 258, "w": 25, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Tom had to <mark data-word=\"borrow\">borrow</mark> money from his brother to pay the rent that month. He hated asking. He wrote the amount on a piece of paper and stuck it on the fridge, and every Friday he paid a little back, until the paper could finally go in the bin.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 259, "w": 25, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "One Sunday I rode over there with Jake to get a horse-collar which Ambrosch had <mark data-word=\"borrow\">borrowed</mark> from him and had not returned. It was a beautiful blue morning. The buffalo-peas were blooming in pink and purple masses along the roadside, and the larks, perched on last year's dried sunflower stalks, were singing straight at the sun, their heads thrown back and their yellow breasts a-quiver. The wind blew about us in warm, sweet gusts. We rode slowly, with a pleasant sense of Sunday indolence.", "source": "My Ántonia — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 260, "w": 26, "slot": 0, "also": [30], "text": "The cat <mark data-word=\"curl\">curled</mark> up in the basket by the fire, with her tail <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark> around her nose, until she looked like a round grey cushion. She stayed like that all evening, and only her ears moved when someone opened the fridge.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 261, "w": 26, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "When a hedgehog is afraid, it <mark data-word=\"curl\">curls</mark> into a tight ball. Its soft face and legs disappear, and all you can see is a round ball of sharp spines. A dog that tries to bite it soon learns to leave it alone.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 262, "w": 26, "slot": 2, "also": [50], "text": "The hot summer had dried the garden. The leaves on the young trees <mark data-word=\"curl\">curled</mark> at the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edges</mark>, rolling up into little brown tubes, and the grass broke under our feet. When the rain finally came, my mother stood outside in it and laughed.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 263, "w": 26, "slot": 3, "also": [50], "text": "The old photograph had lain in the sun on the windowsill for years, and its corners had <mark data-word=\"curl\">curled</mark> up like the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edges</mark> of a dry leaf. My grandmother pressed it flat under a heavy book for a week before she put it back in the frame.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 264, "w": 26, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The baby's tiny fingers <mark data-word=\"curl\">curled</mark> around my thumb and held on surprisingly hard. She was only two days old, and she was already stronger than she looked. I did not move for twenty minutes, afraid that if I did, she would let go.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 265, "w": 26, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Every morning my sister spent half an hour <mark data-word=\"curl\">curling</mark> her hair with a hot iron, turning it from straight into soft round rings. By the evening, after a day in the rain, it was always straight again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 266, "w": 26, "slot": 6, "also": [24], "text": "The snake lay <mark data-word=\"curl\">curled</mark> on a warm rock, its long body bent into a neat circle, round and round, with its head resting on top. It looked asleep, but when my <mark data-word=\"shadow\">shadow</mark> fell across it, the head rose at once.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 267, "w": 26, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "In the cold tent I <mark data-word=\"curl\">curled</mark> up inside my sleeping bag, pulling my knees up to my chest to keep warm. Outside, the wind shook the walls all night. In the morning I was so stiff that I could hardly stand up straight.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 268, "w": 26, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The smoke from the chimney <mark data-word=\"curl\">curled</mark> slowly up into the still winter air, turning round and round like a grey ribbon. From the hill we could see it long before we could see the house, and we knew that someone had lit the fire for us.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 269, "w": 26, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The dog's tail <mark data-word=\"curl\">curled</mark> over its back in an almost perfect circle. When it was happy, the whole circle wagged. When it was afraid, the tail straightened and went down between its legs.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 270, "w": 27, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "'Always keep a <mark data-word=\"spare\">spare</mark> key somewhere outside the house,' my father said. 'One day you will lock yourself out.' I laughed at him for years. Then one rainy night I did lock myself out, and I remembered the key he had hidden under the flower pot.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 271, "w": 27, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "We were forty kilometres from the nearest town when the tyre went flat. Luckily there was a <mark data-word=\"spare\">spare</mark> one in the back of the car. It took my brother twenty minutes to change it, and we reached the town just before dark.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 272, "w": 27, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "My grandmother always had a <mark data-word=\"spare\">spare</mark> bed ready in the little room upstairs, in case somebody needed a place to sleep. Over the years cousins, friends and even a lost tourist slept in it. 'A <mark data-word=\"spare\">spare</mark> bed is never empty for long,' she used to say.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 273, "w": 27, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "'Take a <mark data-word=\"spare\">spare</mark> pair of socks,' said my mother as I packed for the camping trip. I didn't. On the first day I stepped into a river, and I spent the rest of the weekend with wet feet, thinking about her words.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 274, "w": 27, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Our teacher kept a box of <mark data-word=\"spare\">spare</mark> pencils on her desk for students who forgot theirs. By the end of each month the box was empty, and she filled it again without a word.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 275, "w": 27, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The <mark data-word=\"spare\">spare</mark> room in our flat is really the room for everything we don't know where to put: suitcases, old toys, the Christmas tree, a bicycle nobody rides. When guests come to stay, we spend the whole morning moving boxes to make space for the bed.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 276, "w": 27, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "I could not go to his assistance, because, as ill-luck would have it, we had not taken the proper precaution to bring out a <mark data-word=\"spare\">spare</mark> pole with us. I could only sit and look at him. His expression as the pole slowly sank with him I shall never forget; there was so much thought in it.", "source": "Three men in a boat (to say nothing of the dog) — Jerome K. Jerome"},
  {"id": 277, "w": 27, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "Before a long walk in the mountains, experienced walkers always pack <mark data-word=\"spare\">spare</mark> clothes, extra food and more water than they think they will need. The weather can change in an hour, and a warm jumper at the bottom of a bag has saved many lives.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 278, "w": 27, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "My phone died in the middle of the city, far from home. Luckily, I always carry a <mark data-word=\"spare\">spare</mark> battery in my bag, just in case. Five minutes later I had the map back on the screen and found my way to the station.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 279, "w": 27, "slot": 9, "also": [31], "text": "The restaurant was full, but the waiter found a <mark data-word=\"spare\">spare</mark> chair in the kitchen and <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeezed</mark> it in at the end of our table, so that my cousin could sit down too.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 280, "w": 28, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "\"Oh! please don't make such faces, my dear!\" she cried out, quite forgetting that the King couldn't hear her. \"You make me laugh so that I can hardly hold you! And don't keep your mouth so wide open! All the ashes will get into it—there, now I think you're <mark data-word=\"tidy\">tidy</mark> enough!\" she added, as she smoothed his hair, and set him upon the table near the Queen.", "source": "Through the Looking-Glass — Lewis Carroll"},
  {"id": 281, "w": 28, "slot": 1, "also": [94], "text": "\"Very well, and I'll tell her to <mark data-word=\"tidy\">tidy</mark> up here. It's dirty and stinking here, I <mark data-word=\"expect\">expect</mark>. Marya! clear up the room,\" the sick man said with effort. \"Oh, and when you've cleared up, go away yourself,\" he added, looking inquiringly at his brother.", "sense": "to make a place neat by putting things where they belong (verb)", "source": "Anna Karenina — graf Leo Tolstoy"},
  {"id": 282, "w": 28, "slot": 2, "also": [92], "text": "\"They don't keep this room so <mark data-word=\"tidy\">tidy</mark> as the other,\" Alice thought to herself, as she <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticed</mark> several of the chessmen down in the hearth among the cinders: but in another moment, with a little \"Oh!\" of surprise, she was down on her hands and knees watching them. The chessmen were walking about, two and two!", "source": "Through the Looking-Glass — Lewis Carroll"},
  {"id": 283, "w": 28, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "When she got downstairs, she would find the house <mark data-word=\"tidy\">tidy</mark>, but dirty. She could not rest until she had thoroughly cleaned; so she went down to the ash-pit with her dustpan. Mrs. Kirk, spying her, would contrive to have to go to her own coal-place at that minute.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 284, "w": 28, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "All the pink left Mary's cheeks. Her heart began to thump and she felt herself changing into a stiff, plain, silent child again. She did not even answer Mrs. Medlock, but turned and walked into her bedroom, followed by Martha. She said nothing while her dress was changed, and her hair brushed, and after she was quite <mark data-word=\"tidy\">tidy</mark> she followed Mrs. Medlock.", "source": "The Secret Garden — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 285, "w": 28, "slot": 5, "also": [20], "text": "My room is only <mark data-word=\"tidy\">tidy</mark> one day a week — on Sunday evening, before my mother comes to check. Then the books go back on the <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelf</mark>, the clothes go into the cupboard, and the floor appears again. By Wednesday you cannot see it any more.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 286, "w": 28, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The little shop was small but very <mark data-word=\"tidy\">tidy</mark>. Every tin stood in a straight line, every box had a label, and the floor was so clean you could see the lights in it. The old owner knew exactly where everything was and could find it with her eyes closed.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 287, "w": 28, "slot": 7, "also": [39], "text": "\"It's naughty to fret, but I do think washing dishes and keeping things <mark data-word=\"tidy\">tidy</mark> is the worst work in the world. It makes me cross, and my hands get so stiff, I can't practice well at all.\" And Beth looked at her <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark> hands with a sigh that any one could hear that time.", "source": "Little Women — Louisa May Alcott"},
  {"id": 288, "w": 28, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Jeremiah Barker was my new master's name, but as every one called him Jerry, I shall do the same. Polly, his wife, was just as good a match as a man could have. She was a plump, trim, <mark data-word=\"tidy\">tidy</mark> little woman, with smooth, dark hair, dark eyes, and a merry little mouth.", "source": "Black Beauty — Anna Sewell"},
  {"id": 289, "w": 28, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Before the visitors arrived, we made the house <mark data-word=\"tidy\">tidy</mark>: we put the toys in their boxes, hung up the coats, washed the dishes and pushed the chairs under the table. For one hour our home looked like a picture in a magazine. Then the visitors' children came in.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 290, "w": 29, "slot": 0, "also": [32], "text": "On coming back with the wine in his hand, he found that she had placed her father in his easy chair, and had loosened his shirt and neckcloth. They filled a tumbler with wine, and held it to his lips. When he had <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallowed</mark> a little, he took the glass himself and emptied it. Soon after that, he <mark data-word=\"lean\">leaned</mark> back in his chair and cried, with his handkerchief before his face.", "source": "Little Dorrit — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 291, "w": 29, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "The time was come, now, when the wants of this little life were passing away from her. She could not have <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallowed</mark> food, though a table had been spread for her in the next field. The day was cold and wet, but she scarcely knew it.", "source": "Our Mutual Friend — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 292, "w": 29, "slot": 2, "also": [92], "text": "The pill was so big that the little boy could not <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallow</mark> it. He held it in his mouth, drank half a glass of water, and still it would not go down. In the end his mother crushed it into a spoonful of honey, and he ate it without <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticing</mark>.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 293, "w": 29, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "Of a sudden, he stopped, took the cork out of his bottle, and tossed it away. Light as it was, I heard it fall like a plummet. He <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallowed</mark> slowly, tilting up the bottle by little and little, and now he looked at me no more. The last few drops of liquor he poured into the palm of his hand, and licked up.", "source": "Great Expectations — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 294, "w": 29, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "'Chew your food properly before you <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallow</mark>,' my grandmother told us at every meal. 'Your stomach has no teeth.' We laughed, but when my brother tried to <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallow</mark> a big piece of meat whole and started coughing, nobody laughed any more.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 295, "w": 29, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "In the end, the god tossed the meat on the snow at White Fang's feet. He smelled the meat carefully; but he did not look at it. While he smelled it he kept his eyes on the god. Nothing happened. He took the meat into his mouth and <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallowed</mark> it.", "source": "White Fang — Jack London"},
  {"id": 296, "w": 29, "slot": 6, "also": [66], "text": "At this point, Mrs. Poppets knocked at the door to know if we were ready for supper. We smiled sadly at one another, and said we <mark data-word=\"suppose\">supposed</mark> we had better try to <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallow</mark> a bit.", "source": "Three men in a boat (to say nothing of the dog) — Jerome K. Jerome"},
  {"id": 297, "w": 29, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The snake opened its jaws unbelievably wide and began to <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallow</mark> the egg whole. Slowly, slowly the egg disappeared, and for a while we could see its round shape moving down inside the snake's thin body.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 298, "w": 29, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "\"Really, Lily, you are old enough to manage your own affairs, and after frightening me to death by your performance of last night you might at least choose a better time to worry me with such matters.\" Mrs. Peniston glanced at the clock, and <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallowed</mark> a tablet of digitalis. \"If you owe Celeste another thousand, she may send me her account,\" she added, as though to end the discussion at any cost.", "source": "The House of Mirth — Edith Wharton"},
  {"id": 299, "w": 29, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "My throat was so sore that it hurt to <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallow</mark>. Even water felt like broken glass going down. The doctor looked inside my mouth with a light, said 'Ah', and gave me a medicine that tasted of strawberries and worked in two days.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 300, "w": 30, "slot": 0, "also": [31], "text": "You <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrap</mark> a mirror in two blankets, never one. Then two men lift it and a third watches the door, because the doors in old buildings are narrow and you have to <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeeze</mark> everything through them centimetre by centimetre.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 301, "w": 30, "slot": 1, "also": [33], "text": "My work gloves got wet in October and <mark data-word=\"shrink\">shrank</mark> on the radiator overnight. By morning they would have fitted a child. I <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark> my hands in tape instead and worked like that for two days, until the shop opened on the Monday.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 302, "w": 30, "slot": 2, "also": [89], "text": "On Christmas Eve my father <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark> the presents at the kitchen table, very late, when he thought we were asleep. He was terrible at it. The paper was always torn somewhere and there was far too much <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark>, so we could guess every present by its shape.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 303, "w": 30, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The nurse <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark> the newborn baby in a soft white blanket, folding it neatly around his arms and legs until only his small red face showed. Then she gave him to his mother, who had been waiting nine months for that moment.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 304, "w": 30, "slot": 4, "also": [11, 50], "text": "He got up and sat on the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark> of the bedstead with his back to the window. \"It's better not to sleep at all,\" he decided. There was a cold <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark> draught from the window, however; without getting up he drew the blanket over him and <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark> himself in it. He was not thinking of anything and did not want to think. But one image rose after another, incoherent scraps of thought without beginning or end passed through his mind.", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 305, "w": 30, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "At the market the old woman <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark> each egg in a piece of newspaper before she put it in my bag. 'The road home is long and the bus is bumpy,' she said. I got home with twelve eggs and not one of them broken.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 306, "w": 30, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "When they got home, Signa had a fire burning in the sitting-room stove. She undressed Alexandra and gave her a hot footbath, while Ivar made ginger tea in the kitchen. When Alexandra was in bed, <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark> in hot blankets, Ivar came in with his tea and saw that she drank it. Signa asked permission to sleep on the slat lounge outside her door. Alexandra endured their attentions patiently, but she was glad when they put out the lamp and left her.", "source": "O Pioneers! — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 307, "w": 30, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "It was so cold at the bus stop that I <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark> my scarf twice around my face, until only my eyes were showing. The woman next to me did the same. We looked at each other like two robbers and started to laugh.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 308, "w": 30, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "I entered my consulting-room and found a gentleman seated by the table. He was quietly dressed in a suit of heather tweed with a soft cloth cap which he had laid down upon my books. Round one of his hands he had a handkerchief <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark>, which was mottled all over with bloodstains.", "source": "The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 309, "w": 30, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The fish seller <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark> the fish in thick paper, folded the ends and tied it with string. 'Keep it cold and cook it tonight,' he said. On the way home, the cat from the next building followed me for two whole streets.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 310, "w": 31, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "Before the operation my mother was nervous, so I held her hand. When the doctor came in, she <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeezed</mark> it so hard that my rings hurt my fingers. Afterwards she said she did not remember doing it at all.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 311, "w": 31, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "I finally crawled to my feet and ascended the companion stairs. Fair weather was over, and there was nothing left but to return to the galley. My left arm was numb, as though paralysed, and days passed before I could use it, while weeks went by before the last stiffness and pain went out of it. And he had done nothing but put his hand upon my arm and <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeeze</mark>. There had been no wrenching or jerking.", "source": "The Sea-Wolf — Jack London"},
  {"id": 312, "w": 31, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "\"I shall never live to do it again if you treat me so savagely,\" replied he, pushing me from him. \"You've nearly <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeezed</mark> the breath out of my body.\" He pressed his hand to his heart, and looked really agitated and ill.", "source": "The Tenant of Wildfell Hall — Anne Brontë"},
  {"id": 313, "w": 31, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "\"Sit down! I have to speak to you,\" he said, putting the portfolio under his arm, and <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeezing</mark> it so tightly with his elbow that his shoulder stood up. Amazed and intimidated, she gazed at him in silence.", "source": "Anna Karenina — graf Leo Tolstoy"},
  {"id": 314, "w": 31, "slot": 4, "also": [6], "text": "To make fresh lemonade, cut three lemons in half and <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeeze</mark> them over a glass until all the juice runs out. Add water, a little sugar and plenty of ice. My grandmother always <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeezed</mark> them with her <mark data-word=\"bare\">bare</mark> hands, and her lemonade was the best in the village.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 315, "w": 31, "slot": 5, "also": [11], "text": "All this time he was <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeezing</mark> my hand with his <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark> fishy fingers, while I made every effort I decently could to get it away. But I was quite unsuccessful. He drew it under the sleeve of his mulberry-coloured great-coat, and I walked on, almost upon compulsion, arm-in-arm with him.", "source": "David Copperfield — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 316, "w": 31, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Kitty was standing with her sleeves tucked up over the baby in the bath. Hearing her husband's footstep, she turned towards him, summoning him to her with her smile. With one hand she was supporting the fat baby that lay floating and sprawling on its back, while with the other she <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeezed</mark> the sponge over him.", "source": "Anna Karenina — graf Leo Tolstoy"},
  {"id": 317, "w": 31, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The last bit of toothpaste was stuck at the bottom of the tube. My father rolled it up from the end and <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeezed</mark> it hard between his thumb and fingers, and out came one more tiny line of paste. 'See?' he said. 'Never throw anything away too early.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 318, "w": 31, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "\"Fetching? You said fetching?\" roared Razumihin and he flew at Zossimov and seized him by the throat. \"If you ever dare.... Do you understand? Do you understand?\" he shouted, shaking him by the collar and <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeezing</mark> him against the wall.", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 319, "w": 31, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The doctor put a small rubber ball in my hand and asked me to <mark data-word=\"squeeze\">squeeze</mark> it as hard as I could. After the accident my right hand was weak, and the ball hardly changed its shape. But I practised every evening, and a month later I could press it almost flat.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 320, "w": 32, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "After the long climb, the old man stopped and <mark data-word=\"lean\">leaned</mark> on his walking stick to catch his breath. He looked down at the village far below and smiled. 'Every year the hill gets a little higher,' he said, 'but I still get to the top.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 321, "w": 32, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "'Don't <mark data-word=\"lean\">lean</mark> on that fence!' shouted the farmer. 'It's broken!' But it was too late. The boy had already rested his whole weight on it, and he fell backwards into the cow field with half the fence on top of him.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 322, "w": 32, "slot": 2, "also": [38], "text": "Dickon began to push the wheeled chair slowly and <mark data-word=\"steady\">steadily</mark>. Mistress Mary walked beside it and Colin <mark data-word=\"lean\">leaned</mark> back and lifted his face to the sky.", "source": "The Secret Garden — F. H. Burnett"},
  {"id": 323, "w": 32, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The tired builder <mark data-word=\"lean\">leaned</mark> against the wall of the house and closed his eyes for a moment in the sun. His back rested on the warm bricks, his hands hung down, and for a few minutes he looked like part of the building.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 324, "w": 32, "slot": 4, "also": [86], "text": "Anne turned pale, but there was clearly only one thing to be done. She walked toward the house, where a <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark> was <mark data-word=\"lean\">leaning</mark> against the kitchen roof. All the fifth-class girls said, \"Oh!\" partly in excitement, partly in dismay.", "source": "Anne of Green Gables — L. M. Montgomery"},
  {"id": 325, "w": 32, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Putting her apron over her head and her arms, she hurried again to the side garden, to the window of the kitchen. <mark data-word=\"lean\">Leaning</mark> on the sill, she could just see, under the blind, her husband's arms spread out on the table, and his black head on the board. He was sleeping with his face lying on the table. Something in his attitude made her feel tired of things.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 326, "w": 32, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "In the old town, the tall houses <mark data-word=\"lean\">lean</mark> towards each other over the narrow street, as if they want to whisper. In some places the top windows are so close that neighbours can pass a cup of sugar from one side to the other.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 327, "w": 32, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The famous tower in Pisa <mark data-word=\"lean\">leans</mark> to one side. It began to tip while it was still being built, because the ground under it was too soft. Tourists love to take photos in which they seem to be pushing it back up with their hands.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 328, "w": 32, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "A ghostly light from the street lamp lay in a long shaft from one window to the door. Gabriel threw his overcoat and hat on a couch and crossed the room towards the window. He looked down into the street in order that his emotion might calm a little. Then he turned and <mark data-word=\"lean\">leaned</mark> against a chest of drawers with his back to the light.", "source": "Dubliners — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 329, "w": 32, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Mr Dorrit was ashamed. He went back to the window, and <mark data-word=\"lean\">leaned</mark> his forehead against the glass for some time. When he turned, he had his handkerchief in his hand, and he had been wiping his eyes with it, and he looked tired and ill.", "source": "Little Dorrit — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 330, "w": 33, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "I washed my new wool jumper in hot water, and it <mark data-word=\"shrink\">shrank</mark>. When I took it out of the machine, it was the size of a child's jumper. My little niece was delighted — she wore it every day that winter.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 331, "w": 33, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "As the man walked away across the snow, his figure grew smaller and smaller, <mark data-word=\"shrink\">shrinking</mark> to a black dot on the white hill, until at last we could not see him at all.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 332, "w": 33, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "In the dry summer, the lake <mark data-word=\"shrink\">shrank</mark> a little more every week. The water pulled back from the beach, leaving a wide band of hard, broken mud, and the old wooden pier stood on dry ground for the first time in fifty years.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 333, "w": 33, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "Old people often <mark data-word=\"shrink\">shrink</mark> a little as they get older. My grandfather used to be taller than my father; now my son, who is fourteen, can look over the top of his head. 'I'm getting closer to the ground,' Grandpa jokes, 'so I don't have so far to fall.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 334, "w": 33, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "When you put a balloon in the fridge, it slowly <mark data-word=\"shrink\">shrinks</mark>, because cold air takes up less space than warm air. Take it out again, and as it warms up in the room it grows back to its old size. My students never believe this until they see it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 335, "w": 33, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Ten years ago there were forty children in the village school. Now there are twelve. As the young families moved to the city, the village <mark data-word=\"shrink\">shrank</mark>, and the shop, the bakery and the post office closed one after another.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 336, "w": 33, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "My savings <mark data-word=\"shrink\">shrank</mark> quickly in my first year at university. The money I had earned in the summer seemed like a lot in September, but by Christmas there was almost nothing left, and I started to eat a lot of rice.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 337, "w": 33, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "When meat cooks, it <mark data-word=\"shrink\">shrinks</mark>. A large burger goes into the pan and a small one comes out, because the water and fat run out of it in the heat. That is why cooks always make them a little bigger than the bread.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 338, "w": 33, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The old woman herself came to open it. She had <mark data-word=\"shrink\">shrunk</mark> all up with her rheumatism since Jurgis had seen her last, and her yellow parchment face stared up at him from a little above the level of the doorknob. She gave a start when she saw him.", "source": "The Jungle — Upton Sinclair"},
  {"id": 339, "w": 33, "slot": 9, "also": [11, 40], "text": "Our wooden front door <mark data-word=\"shrink\">shrinks</mark> in the dry summer air, and a thin line of light appears around it. In winter the wood takes in water from the <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark> air and grows again, and the door becomes so <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark> that we have to kick it open.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 340, "w": 34, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "A rubber band can <mark data-word=\"stretch\">stretch</mark> to three times its length and then jump back to its old size. My son discovered this in class, pulled one as far as it would go and let it fly. It hit the teacher's ear, and he spent the break writing a letter of apology.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 341, "w": 34, "slot": 1, "also": [40], "text": "My new leather shoes were too <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark> at first, but the man in the shop said they would <mark data-word=\"stretch\">stretch</mark>. 'Wear them for an hour every evening at home,' he said. After two weeks the leather had grown a little wider, and they fitted perfectly.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 342, "w": 34, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "My brother always wears my jumpers, and he is much bigger than me. After a month my favourite blue one was completely <mark data-word=\"stretch\">stretched</mark>: the sleeves hung over my hands and the neck was so wide that it fell off one shoulder.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 343, "w": 34, "slot": 3, "also": [50], "text": "The baker took a ball of dough and began to <mark data-word=\"stretch\">stretch</mark> it with her fingers, pulling it gently from the middle to the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edges</mark>, turning it and pulling again, until it was thin enough to see the light through it. Then she put on the tomato and cheese.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 344, "w": 34, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Chewing gum can <mark data-word=\"stretch\">stretch</mark> a very long way. My little sister pulled a piece from her mouth until it was as long as her arm and as thin as a hair. Then it broke, half of it stuck in her hair, and our mother had to cut it out.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 345, "w": 34, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "When he awoke the sun was shining hot in his face. He sat up and <mark data-word=\"stretch\">stretched</mark> his arms, and then gazed at the water sliding by. There was a deep pool, sheltered and silent, below him, and a sudden wonderful idea rushed upon him. He might have a bath! The water was free, and he might get into it—all the way into it!", "sense": "to make your arms or legs as long as you can, e.g. after sleeping", "source": "The Jungle — Upton Sinclair"},
  {"id": 346, "w": 34, "slot": 6, "also": [50], "text": "\"Well, Mr. Holmes, have you solved this mystery yet?\" He shoved the large tin of cigarettes which stood on a table beside him towards my companion. Holmes <mark data-word=\"stretch\">stretched</mark> out his hand at the same moment, and between them they tipped the box over the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark>. For a minute or two we were all on our knees retrieving stray cigarettes from impossible places.", "sense": "to reach out a hand or arm towards something", "source": "The Return of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 347, "w": 34, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The climbers checked their rope carefully before every climb. A good climbing rope will <mark data-word=\"stretch\">stretch</mark> a little when someone falls, like a very strong rubber band, so that the stop is soft and not a sudden hard pull that breaks bones.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 348, "w": 34, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "'Don't hang your wet wool jumper on a hanger,' my grandmother said. 'The water makes it heavy, and it will <mark data-word=\"stretch\">stretch</mark> and grow long like a dress. Dry it flat on a towel.' I didn't listen, of course, and now I have a very long jumper.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 349, "w": 34, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "I knew he had broken the saw, and then hid it and lied about it. I began to wish I had not stayed for supper. Everything was disagreeable to me. Ántonia ate so noisily now, like a man, and she yawned often at the table and kept <mark data-word=\"stretch\">stretching</mark> her arms over her head, as if they ached. Grandmother had said, 'Heavy field work'll spoil that girl.", "sense": "to make your arms or legs as long as you can, e.g. when they are stiff", "source": "My Ántonia — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 350, "w": 35, "slot": 0, "also": [39], "text": "You cannot <mark data-word=\"drag\">drag</mark> a wardrobe across a wooden floor — it leaves two grey lines that nobody can clean afterwards. The yard outside was <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark> with broken stones, so we carried it out there as well, and my hands were red for the rest of the day.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 351, "w": 35, "slot": 1, "also": [38], "text": "'Hold the light <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark>,' Dave said, and I tried, but after four hours my arm shook anyway. In the end he put the lamp down on a box, and we <mark data-word=\"drag\">dragged</mark> the box along the wall as we moved.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 352, "w": 35, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The suitcase had lost a wheel, so I had to <mark data-word=\"drag\">drag</mark> it all the way from the station. It scraped along the pavement, bumping over every stone, and people turned to look at the noise. By the time I reached the hotel, one corner had a hole in it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 353, "w": 35, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "\"Where is the money?\" she cried — \"Mercy on us, can he have drunk it all? There were twelve silver roubles left in the chest!\" and in a fury she seized him by the hair and <mark data-word=\"drag\">dragged</mark> him into the room. Marmeladov seconded her efforts by meekly crawling along on his knees.", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 354, "w": 35, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "This advice pleased the lion, so he laid himself down quietly for the fox to make him fast to the horse. But the fox managed to tie his legs together and bound all so hard and fast that with all his strength he could not set himself free. When the work was done, the fox clapped the horse on the shoulder, and said, 'Jip! Dobbin! Jip!' Then up he sprang, and moved off, <mark data-word=\"drag\">dragging</mark> the lion behind him.", "source": "Grimms' Fairy Tales — Jacob Grimm"},
  {"id": 355, "w": 35, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Two fishermen <mark data-word=\"drag\">dragged</mark> the heavy net up the beach, pulling it slowly over the sand with ropes over their shoulders. It was full of fish, seaweed and one very angry crab. The children ran behind them, shouting.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 356, "w": 35, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The dog found a huge branch in the park and decided to take it home. It was three times longer than he was, so he <mark data-word=\"drag\">dragged</mark> it behind him, holding one end in his mouth and walking backwards, leaving a long line in the dirt all the way to our gate.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 357, "w": 35, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "Curtis Hartman never remembered how he got out of the church. With a cry he arose, <mark data-word=\"drag\">dragging</mark> the heavy desk along the floor. The Bible fell, making a great clatter in the silence. When the light in the house next door went out he stumbled down the stairway and into the street. Along the street he went and ran in at the door of the Winesburg Eagle.", "source": "Winesburg, Ohio: A Group of Tales of Ohio Small Town Life — Sherwood Anderson"},
  {"id": 358, "w": 35, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The two boys <mark data-word=\"drag\">dragged</mark> the old Christmas tree down the stairs and out into the street, leaving a long line of pine needles behind them. It was too big to carry, so they pulled it by the trunk, one step at a time, while their mother followed with the vacuum cleaner.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 359, "w": 35, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "It occurred to me that perhaps I ought to bury him. At any rate, I ought to hide him. I reflected coolly, and then put my gun within easy reach and <mark data-word=\"drag\">dragged</mark> him by the arm towards a place where the mud seemed soft, and thrust him in. His powder-flask slipped from his loin-cloth, and I went back to get it.", "source": "Tono-Bungay — H. G. Wells"},
  {"id": 360, "w": 36, "slot": 0, "also": [37], "text": "There was a <mark data-word=\"crack\">crack</mark> in the wall of the flat we were emptying, running from the floor up to the window. I <mark data-word=\"rub\">rubbed</mark> it with my thumb and old paint came away as dust. It made me think of my grandmother's kitchen, which had the same line in the same place.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 361, "w": 36, "slot": 1, "also": [74], "text": "The pipe was <mark data-word=\"slightly\">slightly</mark> bent — you could not see it, you could only feel it with your hand. I showed Dave and he held it up to the light and found a hair-thin <mark data-word=\"crack\">crack</mark> at the bend. 'Small things,' he said, and dropped it in the bin.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 362, "w": 36, "slot": 2, "also": [92], "text": "'Did you <mark data-word=\"notice\">notice</mark> the <mark data-word=\"crack\">crack</mark> above that door?' Dave asked, and I had not. He walked me back and made me stand under it for a minute without saying anything. After that I saw cracks everywhere for a month, in buildings that were perfectly sound.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 363, "w": 36, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The winter twilight was fading. The sick man heard his wife strike a match in the kitchen, and the light of a lamp glimmered through the <mark data-word=\"crack\">cracks</mark> of the door. It seemed like a light shining far away. He turned painfully in his bed and looked at his white hands, with all the work gone out of them. He was ready to give up, he felt.", "sense": "a narrow gap through which light can pass", "source": "O Pioneers! — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 364, "w": 36, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "I dropped my phone on the kitchen floor, and when I picked it up, there was a long <mark data-word=\"crack\">crack</mark> across the screen, from one corner to the other. It still worked, but every message I read after that had a thin line running through the middle.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 365, "w": 36, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The ice on the lake looked thick, but my father pointed to a long dark <mark data-word=\"crack\">crack</mark> near the middle. 'Water is coming up through that,' he said. 'Nobody goes past the tree today.' We skated near the shore, and an hour later the ice broke there.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 366, "w": 36, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The old teacup had a thin <mark data-word=\"crack\">crack</mark> down one side, so thin that you could only see it when you held the cup up to the light. No tea came out of it, and my grandmother drank from it every morning for thirty years. 'It's like me,' she said. 'Broken, but still working.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 367, "w": 36, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "After the earthquake there were <mark data-word=\"crack\">cracks</mark> in the walls of almost every house in the street. Some were small, like lines drawn with a pencil; others were wide enough to push a finger into. Engineers checked each building before the families were allowed back inside.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 368, "w": 36, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The long dry summer left deep <mark data-word=\"crack\">cracks</mark> in the ground. The mud of the field had broken into hundreds of pieces, like an old plate, and some of the lines were so wide that a small stone could fall into them.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 369, "w": 36, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "When I looked closely at the egg, I saw a tiny <mark data-word=\"crack\">crack</mark> in the shell. A moment later a small beak pushed through it, the line grew longer, and a wet little chick broke its way out into the world.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 370, "w": 37, "slot": 0, "also": [82], "text": "The room was silent again. Then a bustling little man with a snuffling nose and very cold ears pushed in the door. He walked over quickly to the fire, <mark data-word=\"rub\">rubbing</mark> his hands as if he intended to produce a <mark data-word=\"spark\">spark</mark> from them.", "source": "Dubliners — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 371, "w": 37, "slot": 1, "also": [65], "text": "There was silence. Morel stared at the sugar-basin instead of eating his dinner. His black arm, with the hand all gnarled with work lay on the table. His wife <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretended</mark> not to see him <mark data-word=\"rub\">rub</mark> the back of his hand across his eyes, nor the smear in the coal-dust on his black face.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 372, "w": 37, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "As we walked through the apple orchard, grown up in tall bluegrass, Ántonia kept stopping to tell me about one tree and another. 'I love them as if they were people,' she said, <mark data-word=\"rub\">rubbing</mark> her hand over the bark. 'There wasn't a tree here when we first came. We planted every one, and used to carry water for them, too—after we'd been working in the fields all day. Anton, he was a city man, and he used to get discouraged.", "source": "My Ántonia — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 373, "w": 37, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "When you are cold and have no gloves, <mark data-word=\"rub\">rub</mark> your hands together fast. The skin gets warm from moving back and forth. My grandfather did this every morning before he started the old car, and then he blew on his fingers for luck.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 374, "w": 37, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "\"The man is rather deaf, and in any case we must take our chance of that. We'll sit up in my room to-night and wait until he passes.\" Sir Henry <mark data-word=\"rub\">rubbed</mark> his hands with pleasure, and it was evident that he hailed the adventure as a relief to his somewhat quiet life upon the moor.", "source": "The Hound of the Baskervilles — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 375, "w": 37, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "\"I knew you'd hurt your feet with those silly shoes. I'm sorry. But I don't see what you can do, except get a carriage, or stay here all night,\" answered Jo, softly <mark data-word=\"rub\">rubbing</mark> the poor ankle as she spoke.", "source": "Little Women — Louisa May Alcott"},
  {"id": 376, "w": 37, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "She <mark data-word=\"rub\">rubbed</mark> her eyes with her handkerchief until they looked quite red. After which she got up and went out of the room, without venturing to say another word. When her older sister looked and spoke as she had done just now, the wisest course to pursue was to obey orders without any comment. Miss Minchin walked across the room. She spoke to herself aloud without knowing that she was doing it.", "source": "A Little Princess — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 377, "w": 37, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The waiter <mark data-word=\"rub\">rubbed</mark> the glasses with a clean cloth until they shone, holding each one up to the window to look for marks. If he found even one, he began again. The café was old and cheap, but its glasses were the cleanest in town.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 378, "w": 37, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The cat walked in and began to <mark data-word=\"rub\">rub</mark> herself against my legs, moving her head and her back along them, first one way and then the other. It meant only one thing: it was dinner time, and she thought I had forgotten.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 379, "w": 37, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The beggar girl was still huddled up in the corner of the step. She looked frightful in her wet and dirty rags. She was staring straight before her with a stupid look of suffering, and Sara saw her suddenly draw the back of her roughened black hand across her eyes to <mark data-word=\"rub\">rub</mark> away the tears. She was muttering to herself.", "source": "A Little Princess — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 380, "w": 38, "slot": 0, "also": [46], "text": "At the end of a bad week Dave gave me a <mark data-word=\"reward\">reward</mark> that cost him nothing: I could choose the music in the van. It sounds like a joke. But my hands were <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark> that Friday for the first time in a month, and I knew exactly why.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 381, "w": 38, "slot": 1, "also": [64], "text": "I met her several times out in the country, going a good <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark> pace, and looking as gay and contented as a horse could be. I was very glad to see her, for she <mark data-word=\"deserve\">deserved</mark> a good place.", "source": "Black Beauty — Anna Sewell"},
  {"id": 382, "w": 38, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "Captain Jim put away the fishing net upon which he was working, and welcomed them joyfully. In the searching light of the spring evening he looked older than Anne had ever seen him. His hair had grown much grayer, and the strong old hand shook a little. But his blue eyes were clear and <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark>, and the staunch soul looked out through them gallant and unafraid.", "source": "Anne's House of Dreams — L. M. Montgomery"},
  {"id": 383, "w": 38, "slot": 3, "also": [86], "text": "The old <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark> moved every time I climbed a step, so my father held it <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark> at the bottom with both hands. 'Go on,' he said. 'It won't move now.' I climbed to the top, cleaned the gutter and came down again, and the <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark> did not shake once.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 384, "w": 38, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "\"Yes,\" Sara answered, after a moment's silence. \"But it is not in my body.\" Then she added something in a low voice which she tried to keep quite <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark>, and it was this: \"Do you love your father more than anything else in all the whole world?\"", "source": "A Little Princess — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 385, "w": 38, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "It rained all night — not a storm, just a <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark> rain that fell at the same speed hour after hour, without stopping and without getting heavier. In the morning the garden was soft and green, and the river had risen by half a metre.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 386, "w": 38, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The surgeon's hands had to be completely <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark>. One small shake could cut the wrong thing. That is why, she told me, she never drank coffee on the morning of an operation, and why she slept at least eight hours the night before.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 387, "w": 38, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "To take a clear photo at night, you need to keep the camera <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark>. If your hands move even a little, the picture comes out blurred. I put mine on a wall, held my breath and pressed the button, and the stars came out sharp and bright.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 388, "w": 38, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "\"Five-and-twenty minutes. Well, she went on to say that what remained now to be done was to bring about a marriage between them. She spoke of it, Fanny, with a <mark data-word=\"steady\">steadier</mark> voice than I can.\" He was obliged to pause more than once as he continued.", "source": "Mansfield Park — Jane Austen"},
  {"id": 389, "w": 38, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Since the new factory opened, the town has seen <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark> growth: a few more families every month, a new shop every year. Nothing sudden, nothing dramatic — just slow, regular change that never seems to stop.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 390, "w": 39, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "The old farmer's hands were <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark> from sixty years of work. When he shook my hand, his skin felt like the bark of a tree, hard and dry, nothing like the soft hands of people who work in offices.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 391, "w": 39, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "The path up the mountain was <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark> and full of stones, and after an hour my feet hurt in my thin city shoes. On the way down we took the new road, which was smooth and flat, and it felt like walking on a carpet.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 392, "w": 39, "slot": 2, "also": [78], "text": "It was a very strange thing <mark data-word=\"indeed\">indeed</mark>. She quite caught her breath as she stopped to look at it. A boy was sitting under a tree, with his back against it, playing on a <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark> wooden pipe.", "source": "The Secret Garden — F. H. Burnett"},
  {"id": 393, "w": 39, "slot": 3, "also": [37], "text": "Before you paint the old door, <mark data-word=\"rub\">rub</mark> it with sandpaper until the wood is no longer <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark>. Run your hand over it: if you can feel any bumps, keep going. Paint on a smooth surface lasts twice as long.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 394, "w": 39, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "A cat's tongue feels <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark>, like sandpaper, when she licks your hand. It is covered in tiny hooks, which help her to clean her fur and to take the last bits of meat off a bone.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 395, "w": 39, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "'I don't know the exact number of people who came to the festival,' said the organiser, 'but I can give you a <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark> idea: somewhere between five and six thousand.' Nobody had counted them one by one, but she had seen the full car parks.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 396, "w": 39, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Before building anything, my uncle draws a <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark> plan on the back of an envelope — just a few quick lines to show where the walls and the door will go. The exact measurements come later. 'First the idea,' he says, 'then the numbers.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 397, "w": 39, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The sea was <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark> that day, with high grey waves that threw the little boat up and down. Half the passengers were sick, and the captain decided to turn back to the harbour. The next morning the water was as smooth as glass.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 398, "w": 39, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "'Make a <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark> guess,' said the teacher, holding up a jar of sweets. 'How many are inside? You don't need to count them exactly.' Most of us said about a hundred. My friend Leo said three hundred and twelve, and he was almost right.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 399, "w": 39, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The carpenter ran his hand along the new table. In one place the wood was still <mark data-word=\"rough\">rough</mark>, and a small splinter caught his finger. He took out his tools and worked on that spot until it was as smooth as the rest.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 400, "w": 40, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "The lid of the jam jar was so <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark> that nobody in the family could open it. My father tried, my brother tried, and I tried with a towel. In the end my grandmother held it under hot water for a minute and opened it with one easy turn.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 401, "w": 40, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "'Hold on <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark>!' shouted the man at the fair as the ride began to move. My little brother held the metal bar so hard that his fingers went white, and he did not let go until the car had stopped completely.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 402, "w": 40, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "Before a long journey, my father always checks that the ropes on the roof of the car are <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark>. He pulls each knot hard, and if a rope moves even a little, he ties it again. He has never lost a suitcase on the motorway, and he is very proud of that.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 403, "w": 40, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The screw was so <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark> that I could not turn it. It had been in the old door for fifty years, and rust had fixed it in place. I put a drop of oil on it, waited ten minutes, and then it moved at last with a small squeak.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 404, "w": 40, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "My ring has become too <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark>. It used to slide on and off easily, but now it will not move past my knuckle. The jeweller told me to put my hand in cold water and use a little soap, and after five minutes of pulling it finally came off.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 405, "w": 40, "slot": 5, "also": [32], "text": "Presently Pavel began to talk to Mr. Shimerda, scarcely above a whisper. He was telling a long story, and as he went on, Ántonia took my hand under the table and held it <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark>. She <mark data-word=\"lean\">leaned</mark> forward and strained her ears to hear him.", "source": "My Ántonia — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 406, "w": 40, "slot": 6, "also": [41], "text": "The knot in my shoelace was so <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark> that I had to use my teeth to open it. My son had tied it that morning, very proud of himself, pulling as hard as he could. 'So it doesn't come <mark data-word=\"loose\">loose</mark>,' he said. It certainly didn't.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 407, "w": 40, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "'Pull the bandage <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark>, but not too <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark>,' said the first-aid teacher. 'It must hold the arm still, but the blood must still get to the fingers.' She showed us how to check: press a fingernail, and it should turn pink again at once.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 408, "w": 40, "slot": 8, "also": [11], "text": "The drawer was <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark> and hard to open after the <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark> winter. I pulled and pulled, and when it finally came out, it came out all at once, and knives, forks and spoons flew across the kitchen floor.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 409, "w": 40, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "O Heavens, it had come at last! He would find it was weak, he would say it was weak, and I was lost! I held <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark> to the leg of the table under the cloth, with both hands, and awaited my fate.", "source": "Great Expectations — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 410, "w": 41, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "One of my front teeth was <mark data-word=\"loose\">loose</mark>. I could move it with my tongue, backwards and forwards, and every evening I showed my father how far it went. Then one day I bit into an apple, and the tooth stayed in the apple.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 411, "w": 41, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "A <mark data-word=\"loose\">loose</mark> tile fell from the roof in the storm and broke on the path, just where my mother had been standing a moment before. The next morning my father climbed up and fixed every other tile that moved under his hand.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 412, "w": 41, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The screws in the old chair had come <mark data-word=\"loose\">loose</mark>, and it moved from side to side every time someone sat on it. One evening my uncle sat down heavily, and the chair fell apart under him. After that, my father went through the whole house with a screwdriver.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 413, "w": 41, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "There was a <mark data-word=\"loose\">loose</mark> board on the stairs of the old house. Everybody knew to step over it, because if you stood on it, it moved and made a terrible noise. My brother used it as an alarm: if anyone came up at night, he heard them.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 414, "w": 41, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The button on my coat was <mark data-word=\"loose\">loose</mark>, hanging from one thin thread. I meant to sew it on again, but I forgot, and on the bus it fell off and rolled under the seats. A little girl found it and gave it back to me with a very serious face.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 415, "w": 41, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "She put her hands under the leaves and began to pull and push them aside. Thick as the ivy hung, it nearly all was a <mark data-word=\"loose\">loose</mark> and swinging curtain, though some had crept over wood and iron. Mary's heart began to thump and her hands to shake a little in her delight and excitement. The robin kept singing and twittering away and tilting his head on one side, as if he were as excited as she was.", "source": "The Secret Garden — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 416, "w": 41, "slot": 6, "also": [11], "text": "The tears came so fast to Mrs. Pontellier's eyes that the <mark data-word=\"damp\">damp</mark> sleeve of her nightgown no longer served to dry them. She was holding the back of her chair with one hand; her <mark data-word=\"loose\">loose</mark> sleeve had slipped almost to the shoulder of her uplifted arm. Turning, she thrust her face, steaming and wet, into the bend of her arm, and she went on crying there, not caring any longer to dry her face, her eyes, her arms.", "source": "The Awakening, and Selected Short Stories — Kate Chopin"},
  {"id": 417, "w": 41, "slot": 7, "also": [87], "text": "The back wheel of my bicycle felt <mark data-word=\"loose\">loose</mark>; it moved a little from side to side when I rode. I stopped and checked it: the <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolt</mark> that held it was only half turned. Two turns with a spanner, and the wheel was firm again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 418, "w": 41, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The door handle was so <mark data-word=\"loose\">loose</mark> that it came off in my hand. I stood in the bathroom holding it and looking at the small hole in the door. It took twenty minutes and a phone call to my neighbour before I got out.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 419, "w": 41, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "After the storm, a few bricks in the garden wall were <mark data-word=\"loose\">loose</mark>. You could pull them out with your fingers, because the old cement between them had turned to sand. My grandfather spent the weekend taking them out, one by one, and setting them in fresh cement.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 420, "w": 42, "slot": 0, "also": [44], "text": "Dave was <mark data-word=\"strict\">strict</mark> about time. Six thirty meant six twenty-five with your boots already on. I was so <mark data-word=\"eager\">eager</mark> to show him that I was serious that for a whole month I arrived at six and sat alone in the van in the dark, feeling clever.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 421, "w": 42, "slot": 1, "also": [96], "text": "Our grandmother was <mark data-word=\"strict\">strict</mark> about meals. Nobody could sit down with dirty hands, nobody could leave the table before everyone had finished, and phones stayed in a basket by the door. We <mark data-word=\"complain\">complained</mark>, of course, but we still follow her rules in our own homes.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 422, "w": 42, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The school had a <mark data-word=\"strict\">strict</mark> rule about uniforms: dark blue jumper, white shirt, black shoes, no exceptions. One morning my friend came in red trainers, and the head teacher sent him home to change before he had even taken off his coat.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 423, "w": 42, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "Mr Novak was the most <mark data-word=\"strict\">strict</mark> teacher in the school. If you were one minute late, you waited outside until the break. If you forgot your homework, you did it twice. We were afraid of him — but every one of us passed his exam.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 424, "w": 42, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The library in our town has <mark data-word=\"strict\">strict</mark> rules: no food, no phone calls, and no talking louder than a whisper. An old woman at the front desk watches everyone over the top of her glasses, and even the children are as quiet as mice when they come in.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 425, "w": 42, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "My parents were not very <mark data-word=\"strict\">strict</mark>. I could go to bed when I wanted and eat what I liked, as long as I did my homework. My friend's parents were the opposite: she had to be home by six, even in summer, and she was never allowed to sleep at our house.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 426, "w": 42, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The doctor put my father on a <mark data-word=\"strict\">strict</mark> diet: no sugar, no salt, no bread after six o'clock, and a walk every day, rain or sun. He grumbled for weeks, but he followed every rule, and by the summer he had lost eleven kilos.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 427, "w": 42, "slot": 7, "also": [0], "text": "At the swimming pool the rules are <mark data-word=\"strict\">strict</mark>: no running, no pushing, and no jumping in at the <mark data-word=\"shallow\">shallow</mark> end. The lifeguard blows her whistle at anyone who breaks them, and if you break the same rule twice, you have to leave.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 428, "w": 42, "slot": 8, "also": [79], "text": "Of course there was a public-house in the village, and of course Joe liked sometimes to smoke his pipe there. I had received <mark data-word=\"strict\">strict</mark> orders from my sister to call for him at the Three Jolly Bargemen, that evening, on my way from school, and bring him home at my peril. To the Three Jolly Bargemen, <mark data-word=\"therefore\">therefore</mark>, I directed my steps.", "source": "Great Expectations — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 429, "w": 42, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The museum was very <mark data-word=\"strict\">strict</mark> about photographs. A guard stood in every room, and as soon as someone lifted a phone, he walked over and pointed to the sign. One tourist tried three times, and the third time he was politely shown the exit.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 430, "w": 43, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "The man in front of me in the queue was very <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark> to the young cashier. He shouted at her because the machine was slow, called her stupid and threw his money on the counter. When he left, the whole queue was quiet, and the woman behind me said, 'Don't listen to him, dear.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 431, "w": 43, "slot": 1, "also": [94], "text": "Sara's cheeks felt warm. She went back to her seat and opened the book. She looked at the first page with a grave face. She knew it would be <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark> to smile, and she was very determined not to be <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark>. But it was very odd to find herself <mark data-word=\"expect\">expected</mark> to study a page which told her that \"le pere\" meant \"the father,\" and \"la mere\" meant \"the mother.\"", "source": "A Little Princess — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 432, "w": 43, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "\"Why, Mina, have I been asleep! Oh, do forgive me for being so <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark>. Come, and we'll have a cup of tea somewhere.\" He had evidently forgotten all about the dark stranger, as in his illness he had forgotten all that this episode had reminded him of.", "source": "Dracula — Bram Stoker"},
  {"id": 433, "w": 43, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The people at the beershop were <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark>. They merely laughed at us. There were only three beds in the whole house, and they had seven single gentlemen and two married couples sleeping there already. A kind-hearted bargeman, however, who happened to be in the tap-room, thought we might try the grocer's, next door to the Stag, and we went back.", "source": "Three men in a boat (to say nothing of the dog) — Jerome K. Jerome"},
  {"id": 434, "w": 43, "slot": 4, "also": [48], "text": "Margaret's cheeks burnt. She could not finish her breakfast. She was on fire with <mark data-word=\"shame\">shame</mark>. Helen had told her that the youth was leaving England, but other things had seemed more important, and she had forgotten. All her absurd anxieties fell to the ground, and in their place arose the certainty that she had been <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark> to Mrs. Wilcox.", "source": "Howards End — E. M. Forster"},
  {"id": 435, "w": 43, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "In some countries it is <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark> to eat with your left hand; in others it is <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark> to finish everything on your plate, because it means your host did not give you enough. Before I travel, I always read about what is polite and what is not.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 436, "w": 43, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "'Hold your tongue!' said Mr Boffin. 'You oughtn't to like it in any case. There! I didn't mean to be <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark>, but you put me out so, and after all I'm master. I didn't intend to tell you to hold your tongue.", "source": "Our Mutual Friend — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 437, "w": 43, "slot": 7, "also": [29], "text": "'It's <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark> to talk with your mouth full,' my mother said, for the hundredth time. My little brother <mark data-word=\"swallow\">swallowed</mark> his potato, said 'Sorry,' and then immediately started talking with his mouth full again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 438, "w": 43, "slot": 8, "also": [65], "text": "When the old lady got on the busy bus, a teenager <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretended</mark> to be asleep so that he would not have to give her his seat. The driver saw him in the mirror. 'That's <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark>, young man,' he said loudly, and everyone turned to look until the boy stood up, red in the face.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 439, "w": 43, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "My friend never says thank you, never asks how you are and always looks at his phone while you are talking. I don't think he means to be <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark>; he just never learned. But people who don't know him think he doesn't care about them at all.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 440, "w": 44, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "She clapped her hands and murmured, 'Blue sky, blue eyes,' as if it amused her. While we snuggled down there out of the wind, she learned a score of words. She was alive, and very <mark data-word=\"eager\">eager</mark>. We were so deep in the grass that we could see nothing but the blue sky over us and the gold tree in front of us.", "source": "My Ántonia — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 441, "w": 44, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "The puppy was <mark data-word=\"eager\">eager</mark> to go out. As soon as I took the lead from the hook, he ran to the door, jumping and turning in circles and scratching at the wood with his paws. He could not wait one more second.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 442, "w": 44, "slot": 2, "also": [94], "text": "\"Well now, I reckon it's going to be a pretty good concert. And I <mark data-word=\"expect\">expect</mark> you'll do your part fine,\" he said, smiling down into her <mark data-word=\"eager\">eager</mark>, vivacious little face. Anne smiled back at him. Those two were the best of friends and Matthew thanked his stars many a time and oft that he had nothing to do with bringing her up.", "source": "Anne of Green Gables — L. M. Montgomery"},
  {"id": 443, "w": 44, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "On the first day of school, my little daughter was so <mark data-word=\"eager\">eager</mark> to go that she was dressed and waiting by the door at seven o'clock, with her new bag on her back. School started at half past eight.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 444, "w": 44, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "They arrived at Bath. Catherine was all <mark data-word=\"eager\">eager</mark> delight—her eyes were here, there, everywhere, as they approached its fine and striking environs, and afterwards drove through those streets which conducted them to the hotel. She was come to be happy, and she felt happy already.", "source": "Northanger Abbey — Jane Austen"},
  {"id": 445, "w": 44, "slot": 5, "also": [25], "text": "The new students were <mark data-word=\"eager\">eager</mark> to learn. They asked questions all through the lesson, stayed after class to <mark data-word=\"borrow\">borrow</mark> books, and came back the next week having read all of them.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 446, "w": 44, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Immediately he had finished tea he rose with alacrity to go out. It was this alacrity, this haste to be gone, which so sickened Mrs. Morel. As she heard him sousing heartily in cold water, heard the <mark data-word=\"eager\">eager</mark> scratch of the steel comb on the side of the bowl, as he wetted his hair, she closed her eyes in disgust.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 447, "w": 44, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "She ran then to the other window. That, too, soon flew up under her <mark data-word=\"eager\">eager</mark> hands. A big fly swept past her nose, and buzzed noisily about the room. Then another came, and another; but Pollyanna paid no heed. Pollyanna had made a wonderful discovery — against this window a huge tree flung great branches.", "source": "Pollyanna — Eleanor H. Porter"},
  {"id": 448, "w": 44, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "By the time they came to the pine-trees Miriam was getting very <mark data-word=\"eager\">eager</mark> and very tense. Her bush might be gone. She might not be able to find it; and she wanted it so much. Almost passionately she wanted to be with him when he stood before the flowers. They were going to have a communion together—something that thrilled her, something holy.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 449, "w": 44, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "After two years in another country, Marco was <mark data-word=\"eager\">eager</mark> to see his family again. He counted the days on a calendar, bought presents months too early and was at the airport three hours before his flight.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 450, "w": 45, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "Now I had lived in this happy place three years, but sad changes were about to come over us. We heard from time to time that our mistress was ill. The doctor was often at the house, and the master looked grave and <mark data-word=\"anxious\">anxious</mark>. Then we heard that she must leave her home at once, and go to a warm country for two or three years.", "source": "Black Beauty — Anna Sewell"},
  {"id": 451, "w": 45, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "\"I am growing quite fond of him,\" she said to Ermengarde; \"I should not like him to be disturbed. I have adopted him for a friend. You can do that with people you never speak to at all. You can just watch them, and think about them and be sorry for them, until they seem almost like relations. I'm quite <mark data-word=\"anxious\">anxious</mark> sometimes when I see the doctor call twice a day.\"", "source": "A Little Princess — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 452, "w": 45, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "My mother is always <mark data-word=\"anxious\">anxious</mark> when I travel. She asks me to send a message when I get to the airport, another when the plane lands, and another when I reach the hotel. If one of them is late, she calls me, sure that something terrible has happened.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 453, "w": 45, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The night before the exam, Sofia was too <mark data-word=\"anxious\">anxious</mark> to sleep. She lay in bed going through the answers in her head, worried that she had forgotten everything. In the morning she ate nothing, but when she saw the questions, she knew almost all of them.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 454, "w": 45, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The farmers looked at the sky with <mark data-word=\"anxious\">anxious</mark> faces. It had not rained for six weeks, and the corn was turning yellow in the fields. Every evening they checked the weather forecast, and every evening it said the same thing: sunny and dry.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 455, "w": 45, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "His mother was glad, he seemed so pleased. And his lodging in Walthamstow was so dreary. But now there seemed to come a kind of fever into the young man's letters. He was unsettled by all the change, he did not stand firm on his own feet, but seemed to spin rather giddily on the quick current of the new life. His mother was <mark data-word=\"anxious\">anxious</mark> for him.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 456, "w": 45, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Worn out by <mark data-word=\"anxious\">anxious</mark> watching, Mr. Lorry fell asleep at his post. On the tenth morning of his suspense, he was startled by the shining of the sun into the room where a heavy slumber had overtaken him when it was dark night.", "source": "A Tale of Two Cities — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 457, "w": 45, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "Waiting for news from the hospital, the family sat in the corridor, <mark data-word=\"anxious\">anxious</mark> and silent. Every time a door opened, they all looked up. At last a doctor came out, smiling, and they all breathed out at the same moment.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 458, "w": 45, "slot": 8, "also": [92], "text": "I always feel a little <mark data-word=\"anxious\">anxious</mark> before I speak in public. My hands get cold, my heart beats fast, and I think everyone will <mark data-word=\"notice\">notice</mark> every mistake. But after the first minute it passes, and I usually enjoy the rest.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 459, "w": 45, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Of course this is all very private, but I wished you to know what was going on. Don't be <mark data-word=\"anxious\">anxious</mark> about me, remember I am your 'prudent Amy', and be sure I will do nothing rashly. Send me as much advice as you like.", "source": "Little Women — Louisa May Alcott"},
  {"id": 460, "w": 46, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "When the old man lost his dog, he put up posters all over town: '<mark data-word=\"reward\">Reward</mark> for anyone who finds him.' Two days later a boy knocked on his door with the dog in his arms. The old man gave him fifty euros and a big piece of cake, and the boy said the cake was the better <mark data-word=\"reward\">reward</mark>.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 461, "w": 46, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "After weeks of hard training, the team won the final. Their <mark data-word=\"reward\">reward</mark> was a silver cup, a photo in the local newspaper and a free dinner at the best pizza place in town. 'Every early morning was worth it,' said the captain.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 462, "w": 46, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "My grandmother had a simple rule: work first, then the <mark data-word=\"reward\">reward</mark>. If we helped her in the garden all morning, we got a bowl of fresh strawberries with cream. Nobody ever had to ask us twice.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 463, "w": 46, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The shopkeeper found a wallet full of money outside his door and took it to the police. The owner, an old farmer, came the next day to thank him and offered him a <mark data-word=\"reward\">reward</mark>. The shopkeeper said no. 'Just buy your bread here,' he said. 'That's enough.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 464, "w": 46, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Teachers say that the best <mark data-word=\"reward\">reward</mark> for their work is not the money. It is the day, years later, when a student they had almost given up on stops them in the street to say, 'You changed my life.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 465, "w": 46, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "We trained our dog with small pieces of cheese. Every time he sat when we asked, he got a <mark data-word=\"reward\">reward</mark>. After a week he sat as soon as he saw the cheese, before anyone had said a word, and looked very proud of himself.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 466, "w": 46, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "For three years Maria studied every evening after work. It was hard, and she was often tired. But the <mark data-word=\"reward\">reward</mark> came at last: a new job, better money and, on her first day, her own office with a window.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 467, "w": 46, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The police offered a <mark data-word=\"reward\">reward</mark> of ten thousand euros to anyone who could help them find the stolen painting. Hundreds of people called with ideas, but in the end it was a cleaner who found it, rolled up behind a cupboard in the museum itself.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 468, "w": 46, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Every week the teacher gave a small <mark data-word=\"reward\">reward</mark> to the student who had worked hardest: a book, a coloured pencil, or the job of feeding the class fish. It was never anything big, but everyone wanted it, and the quietest boy in the class won it three times in a row.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 469, "w": 46, "slot": 9, "also": [94], "text": "A horrible conviction darted through Oak. With a sensation of bodily faintness he advanced: at one point the rails were broken through, and there he saw the footprints of his ewes. The dog came up, licked his hand, and made signs implying that he <mark data-word=\"expect\">expected</mark> some great <mark data-word=\"reward\">reward</mark> for signal services rendered. Oak looked over the precipice. The ewes lay dead and dying at its foot—a heap of two hundred mangled carcasses, representing in their condition just now at least two hundred more.", "source": "Far from the Madding Crowd — Thomas Hardy"},
  {"id": 470, "w": 47, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "The big dog stood at the gate and growled, showing its teeth. It was a clear <mark data-word=\"threat\">threat</mark>: come one step closer and I will bite. The postman understood it perfectly and left our letters on the wall outside.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 471, "w": 47, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "The woman sat with her arms crossed and her eyes upon the ground. Her husband had turned his seat a little so as to face her and kept his hammer-like hand upon the table as if it were in readiness to execute his <mark data-word=\"threat\">threat</mark> if she disobeyed him.", "source": "Bleak House — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 472, "w": 47, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "'If you park here again, I will call the police,' said the note on my windscreen. It was a polite <mark data-word=\"threat\">threat</mark>, but a <mark data-word=\"threat\">threat</mark> all the same. The next morning I found another place to park.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 473, "w": 47, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "She dressed herself once more for the street, locked her door and went out. When she emerged on the pavement, the day was still high, but a <mark data-word=\"threat\">threat</mark> of rain darkened the sky and cold gusts shook the signs projecting from the basement shops along the street. She reached Fifth Avenue and began to walk slowly northward. She was sufficiently familiar with Mrs. Dorset's habits to know that she could always be found at home after five.", "source": "The House of Mirth — Edith Wharton"},
  {"id": 474, "w": 47, "slot": 4, "also": [28], "text": "My mother's favourite <mark data-word=\"threat\">threat</mark> was, 'If you don't clean your room, I'll throw everything on the floor into the bin.' We never believed her — until one Saturday she came in with a black bag. After that, our rooms were very <mark data-word=\"tidy\">tidy</mark> for a month.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 475, "w": 47, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The factory workers made a <mark data-word=\"threat\">threat</mark>: if the managers did not raise their pay, they would stop work on Monday. The managers did not believe them. On Monday morning the machines were silent, and by Wednesday the managers had agreed.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 476, "w": 47, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The angry neighbour shouted a <mark data-word=\"threat\">threat</mark> over the fence: if our football landed in his garden one more time, he would keep it. The next day it landed there again. We never saw that ball again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 477, "w": 47, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The cat arched her back, puffed up her tail and hissed at the dog. It was a clear <mark data-word=\"threat\">threat</mark>, and the dog understood it at once. He walked backwards out of the kitchen without taking his eyes off her.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 478, "w": 47, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The teacher's <mark data-word=\"threat\">threat</mark> was simple: 'Anyone who talks during the test gets zero.' Nobody believed she would do it — until she took Paul's paper away in the first five minutes. After that, the room was so quiet you could hear the clock.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 479, "w": 47, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The kidnappers sent a letter with a <mark data-word=\"threat\">threat</mark>: pay the money by Friday, or you will never see your dog again. The family paid. The dog came home on Saturday, fat and happy, and nobody ever found out where he had been.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 480, "w": 48, "slot": 0, "also": [54], "text": "He asked me afterwards whether I had been frightened up there. I wanted to say no. Instead I chose to <mark data-word=\"admit\">admit</mark> it — yes, for one second — and there was no <mark data-word=\"shame\">shame</mark> in it at all. He said the day you stop being frightened is the day you should go home.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 481, "w": 48, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "When the teacher read my copied homework aloud and asked whose it really was, I felt my face burn with <mark data-word=\"shame\">shame</mark>. I could not look at my friend, whose work I had taken. After class I went to the teacher and told her the truth.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 482, "w": 48, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "Years later, my grandfather still felt <mark data-word=\"shame\">shame</mark> when he remembered how he had shouted at his father on the day they last saw each other. 'I never said sorry,' he told me. 'Don't make my mistake.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 483, "w": 48, "slot": 3, "also": [91], "text": "Mr. Harrison himself was sitting on his vineshaded veranda, in his shirt sleeves, enjoying his evening pipe. When he <mark data-word=\"realise\">realized</mark> who was coming up the path he sprang suddenly to his feet, bolted into the house, and shut the door. This was merely the uncomfortable result of his surprise, mingled with a good deal of <mark data-word=\"shame\">shame</mark> over his outburst of temper the day before.", "source": "Anne of Avonlea — L. M. Montgomery"},
  {"id": 484, "w": 48, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Another letter had to be written to Vronsky. \"I have told my husband,\" she wrote, and she sat a long while unable to write more. It was so coarse, so unfeminine. \"And what more am I to write to him?\" she said to herself. Again a flush of <mark data-word=\"shame\">shame</mark> spread over her face; she recalled his composure, and a feeling of anger against him impelled her to tear the sheet with the phrase she had written into tiny bits.", "source": "Anna Karenina — graf Leo Tolstoy"},
  {"id": 485, "w": 48, "slot": 5, "also": [97], "text": "The footballer who had pushed the referee left the field with his head down, full of <mark data-word=\"shame\">shame</mark>. The next day he went on television and <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologised</mark> to the referee, to his team and to all the children who had watched him.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 486, "w": 48, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Ben lied to his parents about where he had been, and they believed him. That was the worst part. The <mark data-word=\"shame\">shame</mark> kept him awake all night, and at breakfast he told them everything before they had even sat down.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 487, "w": 48, "slot": 7, "also": [65], "text": "'There is no <mark data-word=\"shame\">shame</mark> in asking for help,' our coach told us. 'The only thing to be ashamed of is <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretending</mark> you understand when you don't.' After that, even the quietest players started to ask questions.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 488, "w": 48, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "When the old woman saw that the boy had stolen apples from her tree, she did not shout. She just looked at him sadly and said, 'You could have asked.' He turned red with <mark data-word=\"shame\">shame</mark> and brought back every apple, and the next day he asked.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 489, "w": 48, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "After he broke his friend's bike and hid it, Tom could not sleep. The <mark data-word=\"shame\">shame</mark> sat on his chest like a stone. On Monday he told his friend everything and offered to pay for the repair with his birthday money.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 490, "w": 49, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "Every question I had to ask felt like a <mark data-word=\"burden\">burden</mark> for the person answering it. That is the wrong way to think and it cost me six months of English. People are not carrying you when they explain a word to you. They are just talking.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 491, "w": 49, "slot": 1, "also": [78, 91, 94], "text": "\"Dear, kind Rat,\" murmured Toad, \"how little you <mark data-word=\"realise\">realise</mark> my condition, and how very far I am from 'jumping up' now—if ever! But do not trouble about me. I hate being a <mark data-word=\"burden\">burden</mark> to my friends, and I do not <mark data-word=\"expect\">expect</mark> to be one much longer. <mark data-word=\"indeed\">Indeed</mark>, I almost hope not.\"", "source": "The Wind in the Willows — Kenneth Grahame"},
  {"id": 492, "w": 49, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "There was once on a time a poor man, who could no longer support his only son. Then said the son, \"Dear father, things go so badly with us that I am a <mark data-word=\"burden\">burden</mark> to you. I would rather go away and see how I can earn my bread.\" So the father gave him his blessing, and with great sorrow took leave of him.", "source": "Household Tales by Brothers Grimm — Jacob Grimm"},
  {"id": 493, "w": 49, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "\"There is no man under the sun so fortunate as I,\" he cried out. With a light heart and free from every <mark data-word=\"burden\">burden</mark> he now ran on until he was with his mother at home.", "source": "Household Tales by Brothers Grimm — Jacob Grimm"},
  {"id": 494, "w": 49, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The donkey carried a heavy <mark data-word=\"burden\">burden</mark> up the mountain path: two big bags of flour, a box of tools and a sleepy farmer's boy. It walked slowly but never stopped, and at the top the farmer gave it an apple and a long rest in the shade.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 495, "w": 49, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "When my father got ill, looking after him became a <mark data-word=\"burden\">burden</mark> for my mother. She did the shopping, the cooking, his medicine and his washing, and she still went to work. It was only when my aunt came to help that we saw how tired she really was.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 496, "w": 49, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The old man did not want to be a <mark data-word=\"burden\">burden</mark> on his children. 'You have your own lives,' he said. So when he could no longer climb the stairs, he sold his house and moved into a small flat on the ground floor, near the shops.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 497, "w": 49, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The soldiers walked for hours through the snow, each carrying a <mark data-word=\"burden\">burden</mark> of thirty kilos on his back. By the evening their shoulders ached so much that some of them dropped their packs as soon as they stopped and fell asleep on top of them.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 498, "w": 49, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Keeping the secret was a heavy <mark data-word=\"burden\">burden</mark> for Anna. She thought about it at school, at dinner and in bed at night. When at last she told her mother, she felt so light that she almost laughed.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 499, "w": 49, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "An old man was dozing in a great hooded chair in the hall. He lit a candle in the office and went before them to the stairs. They followed him in silence, their feet falling in soft thuds on the thickly carpeted stairs. She mounted the stairs behind the porter, her head bowed in the ascent, her frail shoulders curved as with a <mark data-word=\"burden\">burden</mark>, her skirt girt tightly about her.", "source": "Dubliners — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 500, "w": 50, "slot": 0, "also": [51], "text": "There was a <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> of brackets by the door and I stepped back to count them without looking behind me. That put me half a metre from the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark> of the roof. Dave did not shout. He said my name once, in a voice I had not heard him use before.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 501, "w": 50, "slot": 1, "also": [32], "text": "Mrs. Pontellier sprang out of bed and went into the next room. She soon came back and sat on the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark> of the bed, <mark data-word=\"lean\">leaning</mark> her head down on the pillow. She said nothing, and refused to answer her husband when he questioned her. When his cigar was smoked out he went to bed, and in half a minute he was fast asleep.", "source": "The Awakening, and Selected Short Stories — Kate Chopin"},
  {"id": 502, "w": 50, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The little boy stood at the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark> of the swimming pool, his toes over the side, looking down at the water. 'Jump!' called his father from below. He took a deep breath, closed his eyes and jumped.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 503, "w": 50, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "'Don't put your glass so close to the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark> of the table,' my mother said. 'Someone will knock it off.' Five minutes later my brother waved his arm while telling a story, and the glass flew onto the floor.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 504, "w": 50, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Our house is on the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark> of the town, where the last street ends and the fields begin. From the kitchen window we see houses on one side and cows on the other, and at night the town's lights stop just behind our garden.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 505, "w": 50, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "He took two bottles from the table and, carrying them to the fire, put them on the hob. Then he sat down again by the fire and took another drink from his bottle. Mr Lyons sat on the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark> of the table, pushed his hat towards the nape of his neck and began to swing his legs.", "source": "Dubliners — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 506, "w": 50, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The cliff path goes right along the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark>, and in some places there is only a metre of grass between you and a drop of a hundred metres to the sea. My mother held my little sister's hand the whole way and would not let her walk on the outside.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 507, "w": 50, "slot": 7, "also": [91], "text": "Presently we saw a curious thing: There were no clouds, the sun was going down in a limpid, gold-washed sky. Just as the lower <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark> of the red disk rested on the high fields against the horizon, a great black figure suddenly appeared on the face of the sun. We sprang to our feet, straining our eyes toward it. In a moment we <mark data-word=\"realise\">realized</mark> what it was. On some upland farm, a plough had been left standing in the field.", "source": "My Ántonia — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 508, "w": 50, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The four travelers walked with ease through the trees until they came to the farther <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark> of the wood. Then, to their surprise, they found before them a high wall which seemed to be made of white china. It was smooth, like the surface of a dish, and higher than their heads.", "source": "The Wonderful Wizard of Oz — L. Frank Baum"},
  {"id": 509, "w": 50, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The old plate had a small piece missing from its <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark>, so my grandmother always turned it so that the broken part faced her, not the guest. 'Nobody needs to see that,' she said. She used that plate for forty years.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 510, "w": 51, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "There was a <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> of dirty dishes in the sink so high that the top plate touched the tap. Nobody wanted to wash them. In the end my father rolled up his sleeves, put on the radio and washed them all, one by one, singing louder with every plate.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 511, "w": 51, "slot": 1, "also": [38], "text": "\"Yes,\" answered Sara, and suddenly a strange thought passed through her mind and made her face flush. She held the <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> of garments in her arms, and her chin rested upon the top of it to keep it <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark>. Something in the look of her straight-gazing eyes made Ermengarde lose her wits still more. She felt as if Sara had changed into a new kind of girl, and she had never known her before.", "source": "A Little Princess — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 512, "w": 51, "slot": 2, "also": [32], "text": "On my desk there is a <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> of books I want to read. Every time I finish one, I buy two more and put them on top. The <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> is now so tall that it has started to <mark data-word=\"lean\">lean</mark>, and my cat has learned to sit next to it, not on it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 513, "w": 51, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The children raked the autumn leaves into a huge <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> in the middle of the garden — red, yellow and brown, higher than their heads. Then, of course, they took a run and jumped into it, and in one minute the leaves were all over the grass again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 514, "w": 51, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The teacher put the <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> of exam papers on her desk and looked at it with a sigh. Thirty papers, three pages each. She made a big cup of coffee, took the first paper from the top and began.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 515, "w": 51, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Outside the farmhouse there was a neat <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> of wood for the winter, logs laid one on top of the other against the wall, as tall as a man. By March only a few pieces were left at the bottom, and the family was waiting for the spring.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 516, "w": 51, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "When I came home after two weeks away, there was a <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> of letters behind the door: bills, advertisements, a postcard from my aunt and a note from the post office. I sat on the floor with my coat still on and went through them one by one.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 517, "w": 51, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "At the laundry the clean clothes were folded and put in a <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> on the counter: shirts at the bottom, then trousers, then socks on the very top. The woman tied the whole <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> with string and handed it to me.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 518, "w": 51, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "My brother leaves his clothes in a <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> on the floor next to his bed — shirts, socks, jeans, all on top of each other. Every morning he takes something from the <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark>, smells it, and decides if it can be worn one more day.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 519, "w": 51, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The builders had left a <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> of bricks by the gate, one on top of the other, ready for the new wall. During the night a cat decided it was the perfect place to sleep, and in the morning she refused to move.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 520, "w": 52, "slot": 0, "also": [53], "text": "'Leave a small <mark data-word=\"gap\">gap</mark> between the boards,' the old carpenter told me. 'Wood moves.' Nobody had <mark data-word=\"warn\">warned</mark> me about that on my first job, and by August the floor I had laid in April stood up in the middle like a wave.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 521, "w": 52, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "My daughter smiled at me with a big <mark data-word=\"gap\">gap</mark> where her front tooth had been. She was very proud of it, and she pushed her tongue through the empty space all day to show everyone.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 522, "w": 52, "slot": 2, "also": [92], "text": "The sheep escaped through a <mark data-word=\"gap\">gap</mark> in the fence, a hole just wide enough for one animal at a time. By the time the farmer <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticed</mark>, half of them were eating the neighbour's cabbages.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 523, "w": 52, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "\"Show us the place,\" Syme said briefly, and without a word the man in the violet coat turned his back and walked towards a <mark data-word=\"gap\">gap</mark> in the hedge, which let in suddenly the light of a white road.", "source": "The Man Who Was Thursday: A Nightmare — G. K. Chesterton"},
  {"id": 524, "w": 52, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "'Mind the <mark data-word=\"gap\">gap</mark>!' says the voice in the London Underground, reminding passengers to be careful of the space between the train and the platform. At some stations the space is so wide that a child's foot could slip through it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 525, "w": 52, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "He said he had all Sir Walter Scott's works and all Lord Lytton's works at home and never tired of reading them. \"Of course,\" he said, \"there were some of Lord Lytton's works which boys couldn't read.\" Mahony asked why couldn't boys read them—a question which agitated and pained me because I was afraid the man would think I was as stupid as Mahony. The man, however, only smiled. I saw that he had great <mark data-word=\"gap\">gaps</mark> in his mouth between his yellow teeth.", "source": "Dubliners — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 526, "w": 52, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The cat escaped through a narrow <mark data-word=\"gap\">gap</mark> between the door and the wall. We did not think anything bigger than a mouse could fit through it, but she made her body flat and disappeared into the garden in a second.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 527, "w": 52, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "In the full cinema we found two free seats, but there was a <mark data-word=\"gap\">gap</mark> of five seats between them. A kind man moved over, so that my friend and I could sit together.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 528, "w": 52, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Through a <mark data-word=\"gap\">gap</mark> in the clouds, a single ray of sun came down onto the sea and made a bright circle of silver on the grey water. A minute later the clouds closed again, and the <mark data-word=\"gap\">gap</mark> was gone.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 529, "w": 52, "slot": 9, "also": [78], "text": "Under cover of this I pushed on, wet and shivering now, towards my own house. I walked among the trees trying to find the footpath. It was very dark <mark data-word=\"indeed\">indeed</mark> in the wood, for the lightning was now becoming infrequent, and the hail, which was pouring down in a torrent, fell in columns through the <mark data-word=\"gap\">gaps</mark> in the heavy foliage.", "source": "The war of the worlds — H. G. Wells"},
  {"id": 530, "w": 53, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "The weather forecast <mark data-word=\"warn\">warned</mark> of a big storm, so the fishermen stayed in the harbour that night and tied their boats with extra ropes. By midnight the waves were higher than the harbour wall, but nobody was out on the sea.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 531, "w": 53, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "My father <mark data-word=\"warn\">warned</mark> me not to touch the stove: 'It's still hot.' I was five, so of course I touched it. I still have a small white mark on my finger, and I still remember that he was right.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 532, "w": 53, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "A sign at the beach <mark data-word=\"warn\">warns</mark> swimmers about the strong current: 'Danger! Do not swim beyond the flags.' Every summer a few tourists ignore it, and every summer the lifeguards have to pull someone out of the water.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 533, "w": 53, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The old man <mark data-word=\"warn\">warned</mark> us not to walk on the frozen lake. 'The ice is thin in the middle,' he said. 'Last year a cow went through it.' We thanked him and took the long way round, along the shore.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 534, "w": 53, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The doctor <mark data-word=\"warn\">warned</mark> him that smoking would damage his lungs, but he did not listen. Ten years later he could not climb the stairs without stopping to breathe. 'She told me,' he said. 'I just didn't believe her.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 535, "w": 53, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Before we went into the cave, the guide <mark data-word=\"warn\">warned</mark> us to keep our heads down. 'The roof is low in some places,' she said. Two minutes later I heard a bump and a cry behind me. My brother had not been listening.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 536, "w": 53, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Birds <mark data-word=\"warn\">warn</mark> each other when a cat is near. One bird sees it and gives a sharp, loud cry, and in a second every bird in the garden flies up into the trees. The cat sits in the grass, looking very disappointed.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 537, "w": 53, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "I tried to <mark data-word=\"warn\">warn</mark> my friend that the step was broken, but I was too late. He put his foot on it, it moved, and he fell down the last three stairs. Luckily, the only thing he hurt was his pride.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 538, "w": 53, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The newspapers <mark data-word=\"warn\">warned</mark> people to stay at home during the heat wave. Old people were told to drink plenty of water and to keep out of the sun between noon and four. The streets were empty all afternoon.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 539, "w": 53, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The mountain guide <mark data-word=\"warn\">warned</mark> us that the weather could change very quickly up there. 'If you see dark clouds over the top, come down at once,' she said. At two o'clock the clouds came, and we were glad we had listened.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 540, "w": 54, "slot": 0, "also": [55], "text": "The last bracket disappeared on a Friday and every man on the floor would <mark data-word=\"deny\">deny</mark> taking it. I do not think anybody was lying. Things fall into buckets, into bags, into vans. But nobody wanted to <mark data-word=\"admit\">admit</mark> that he had not been watching.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 541, "w": 54, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "For a long time my father would not <mark data-word=\"admit\">admit</mark> that he needed glasses. He held the newspaper further and further away and said the letters were too small these days. In the end he misread a road sign and drove us twenty kilometres in the wrong direction. The next week he bought glasses.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 542, "w": 54, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "\"Mater, my dear—\" he began, with the whole force of his soul. Then he stopped. He would not say it. He would not <mark data-word=\"admit\">admit</mark> that he wanted to die, to have done. He would not own that life had beaten him, or that death had beaten him.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 543, "w": 54, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "I hate to <mark data-word=\"admit\">admit</mark> it, but my little sister was right. I had said the film would be boring, and I had refused to go. She went with her friends, came back laughing, and told me it was the best film of the year. When I finally watched it, I had to agree.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 544, "w": 54, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The boy finally <mark data-word=\"admit\">admitted</mark> that he had broken the window. At first he said a bird had flown into it, then that the wind had done it. But when his mother found the football under his bed, he looked at the floor and said quietly, 'It was me.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 545, "w": 54, "slot": 5, "also": [65], "text": "The minister <mark data-word=\"admit\">admitted</mark> on television that the government had made a mistake. It was not easy for him; he spoke slowly and did not look at the camera. But people respected him more for saying it than they would have if he had <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretended</mark> nothing was wrong.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 546, "w": 54, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "'All right, I <mark data-word=\"admit\">admit</mark> it,' said my brother, after I had asked him for the third time. 'I ate your chocolate.' He did not look sorry at all. 'But you left it on the table,' he added, 'so it was partly your fault.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 547, "w": 54, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "It took Tom an hour to <mark data-word=\"admit\">admit</mark> that we were lost. He had told everyone he knew the forest perfectly, so when the path disappeared, he kept walking and said nothing. Only when we passed the same fallen tree for the third time did he stop and say, 'I think we went wrong somewhere.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 548, "w": 54, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "She held out her hand as the train resumed its level rush, and they stood exchanging a few words in the aisle. Yes—he was going to Bellomont. He had heard she was to be of the party—he blushed again as he <mark data-word=\"admit\">admitted</mark> it. And was he to be there for a whole week? How delightful!", "source": "The House of Mirth — Edith Wharton"},
  {"id": 549, "w": 54, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "My grandmother never <mark data-word=\"admit\">admits</mark> that she is tired. After a whole day of cooking for twenty people, she still says, 'I'm fine, sit down,' and starts washing the dishes. Only when she falls asleep in her chair at nine o'clock do we know the truth.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 550, "w": 55, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "The politician <mark data-word=\"deny\">denied</mark> that he had taken the money. 'It is not true,' he told the reporters. 'I have never taken a single euro.' Two weeks later the newspapers printed a photo of him taking an envelope from a businessman.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 551, "w": 55, "slot": 1, "also": [54], "text": "This morning the man on duty reported to me that a little after midnight he was restless and kept saying his prayers somewhat loudly. I asked him if that was all; he replied that it was all he heard. There was something about his manner so suspicious that I asked him point blank if he had been asleep. He <mark data-word=\"deny\">denied</mark> sleep, but <mark data-word=\"admit\">admitted</mark> to having \"dozed\" for a while.", "source": "Dracula — Bram Stoker"},
  {"id": 552, "w": 55, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "When the teacher asked who had drawn on the board, every child <mark data-word=\"deny\">denied</mark> it. 'Not me,' said one; 'Not me,' said another. But one small boy had blue chalk all over his fingers, and he quickly put his hands behind his back.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 553, "w": 55, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "My brother <mark data-word=\"deny\">denies</mark> that he snores. 'I never make a sound,' he says every morning. So one night I recorded him on my phone and played it at breakfast. He listened for ten seconds, then said the phone was broken.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 554, "w": 55, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The company <mark data-word=\"deny\">denied</mark> that its factory had poisoned the river. 'Our water is clean,' a spokesman said. But the fishermen downstream had been finding dead fish for three weeks, and they had photos of the orange pipe.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 555, "w": 55, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "'Did you eat the last biscuit?' asked Mum. 'No!' said my sister. She <mark data-word=\"deny\">denied</mark> it firmly, looking straight into our mother's eyes. The only problem was the crumbs all round her mouth.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 556, "w": 55, "slot": 6, "also": [54], "text": "The footballer <mark data-word=\"deny\">denied</mark> touching the ball with his hand. 'It hit my chest,' he told the referee. But the television cameras showed the truth clearly, and after the match he had to <mark data-word=\"admit\">admit</mark> that he had lied.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 557, "w": 55, "slot": 7, "also": [17, 68], "text": "He would not have it that they were lovers. The intimacy between them had been kept so abstract, such a matter of the soul, all thought and <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark> <mark data-word=\"struggle\">struggle</mark> into consciousness, that he saw it only as a platonic friendship. He stoutly <mark data-word=\"deny\">denied</mark> there was anything else between them. Miriam was silent, or else she very quietly agreed. He was a fool who did not know what was happening to himself.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 558, "w": 55, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "In the meantime, we could never make out where he got the drink. That was the ship's mystery. Watch him as we pleased, we could do nothing to solve it; and when we asked him to his face, he would only laugh if he were drunk, and if he were sober <mark data-word=\"deny\">deny</mark> solemnly that he ever tasted anything but water.", "source": "Treasure Island — Robert Louis Stevenson"},
  {"id": 559, "w": 55, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Anna <mark data-word=\"deny\">denied</mark> being afraid of the dark. 'I just like to sleep with the light on,' she said. 'It's more comfortable.' Nobody in the family believed her, but nobody said anything either.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 560, "w": 56, "slot": 0, "also": [58], "text": "<mark data-word=\"attach\">Attach</mark> the bracket to the wall first, then hang the pipe — never the other way round. Do it backwards once and you will <mark data-word=\"avoid\">avoid</mark> that mistake for the rest of your life, because you will spend an afternoon holding twelve kilos above your head.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 561, "w": 56, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "Through street after street went George Willard, <mark data-word=\"avoid\">avoiding</mark> the people who passed. He crossed and recrossed the road. When he passed a street lamp he pulled his hat down over his face. He did not dare think. In his mind there was a fear but it was a new kind of fear.", "source": "Winesburg, Ohio: A Group of Tales of Ohio Small Town Life — Sherwood Anderson"},
  {"id": 562, "w": 56, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "I always try to <mark data-word=\"avoid\">avoid</mark> the main road at eight in the morning, when it is full of cars and buses. I take the small streets behind the park instead. It is a little longer, but I never sit in traffic, and I see the ducks on the way.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 563, "w": 56, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "Since their argument, my two aunts <mark data-word=\"avoid\">avoid</mark> each other. At family parties, if one sits in the kitchen, the other stays in the garden. When one arrives, the other suddenly remembers she has to leave. It has been going on for eleven years.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 564, "w": 56, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Doctors say you should <mark data-word=\"avoid\">avoid</mark> the midday sun in summer. Between twelve and three the sun is at its strongest, and even half an hour without a hat can burn your skin. Go out in the morning or the evening, and spend the middle of the day in the shade.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 565, "w": 56, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Gabriel tried to cover his agitation by taking part in the dance with great energy. He <mark data-word=\"avoid\">avoided</mark> her eyes for he had seen a sour expression on her face. But when they met in the long chain he was surprised to feel his hand firmly pressed.", "source": "Dubliners — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 566, "w": 56, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The cat <mark data-word=\"avoid\">avoids</mark> my uncle. When he comes into the house, she disappears under the bed and does not come out until he has left. Nobody knows why; he has never done anything to her. Maybe she just doesn't like his loud voice.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 567, "w": 56, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "Sonia gazed at him astonished. His tone seemed strange to her; a cold shiver ran over her, but in a moment she guessed that the tone and the words were a mask. He spoke to her looking away, as though to <mark data-word=\"avoid\">avoid</mark> meeting her eyes.", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 568, "w": 56, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "\"Even the child's hideous and affected,\" thought Anna. To <mark data-word=\"avoid\">avoid</mark> seeing anyone, she got up quickly and seated herself at the opposite window of the empty carriage. A misshapen-looking peasant covered with dirt, in a cap from which his tangled hair stuck out all round, passed by that window, stooping down to the carriage wheels. \"There's something familiar about that hideous peasant,\" thought Anna. And remembering her dream, she moved away to the opposite door, shaking with terror.", "source": "Anna Karenina — graf Leo Tolstoy"},
  {"id": 569, "w": 56, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "He seemed to be approaching cautiously. He did not go on to the bridge, but stood aside on the pavement, doing all he could to <mark data-word=\"avoid\">avoid</mark> Raskolnikov's seeing him. He had observed Dounia for some time and had been making signs to her. She fancied he was signalling to beg her not to speak to her brother, but to come to him.", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 570, "w": 57, "slot": 0, "also": [59], "text": "We could not <mark data-word=\"delay\">delay</mark> the job until Monday, so I decided to <mark data-word=\"replace\">replace</mark> the missing part with a temporary one and fix it with two extra bolts. It held all weekend. On Monday the right bracket arrived and I changed it before lunch.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 571, "w": 57, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "The heavy snow <mark data-word=\"delay\">delayed</mark> all the trains that morning. Ours should have left at eight, but it only left at eleven, and we spent three hours on the cold platform, drinking coffee from the machine and watching the board.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 572, "w": 57, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "We had to <mark data-word=\"delay\">delay</mark> the wedding by a month because the bride broke her leg skiing. The new date was in May, and in the end everyone agreed it was better: the flowers were out, and she could dance.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 573, "w": 57, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "'Don't <mark data-word=\"delay\">delay</mark> — see a doctor today,' said my friend, looking at the cut on my hand. I wanted to wait until Monday. By Sunday evening the hand was red and hot, and I spent the night in hospital.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 574, "w": 57, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "After an hour's <mark data-word=\"delay\">delay</mark>, the great nobleman appeared. His face was more cadaverous than ever, his shoulders had rounded, and he seemed to me to be an altogether older man than he had been the morning before. He greeted us with a stately courtesy and seated himself at his desk, his red beard streaming down on the table.", "sense": "a time of waiting when something happens later than planned (noun)", "source": "The Return of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 575, "w": 57, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The builders <mark data-word=\"delay\">delayed</mark> the new school again and again. First it was the rain, then a missing part, then a problem with the roof. It should have opened in September; the children finally walked in the following March.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 576, "w": 57, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "A problem with the engine <mark data-word=\"delay\">delayed</mark> our flight by four hours. The airline gave everyone a ticket for a free sandwich, and a man with a guitar started playing in the waiting room, so in the end it was not so bad.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 577, "w": 57, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "My sister always <mark data-word=\"delay\">delays</mark> doing her homework until the last minute. She says she works better under pressure. So every Sunday at ten in the evening, the whole family hears her running around the house looking for a pen.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 578, "w": 57, "slot": 8, "also": [22], "text": "The rain <mark data-word=\"delay\">delayed</mark> the start of the tennis match by two hours. The players waited in the changing rooms, and the <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> sat under umbrellas, eating sandwiches. At four o'clock the sun came out, and the match finally began.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 579, "w": 57, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Mr. Melbury was not in the room. Having to attend to matters in the yard, he had <mark data-word=\"delay\">delayed</mark> putting on his afternoon coat and waistcoat till the doctor's appearance, when, not wishing to be backward in receiving him, he entered the parlor hastily buttoning up those garments.", "source": "The Woodlanders — Thomas Hardy"},
  {"id": 580, "w": 58, "slot": 0, "also": [89], "text": "Before you send the parcel, <mark data-word=\"attach\">attach</mark> a label with the address to the top of the box. Use strong <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark>, not glue, or it may fall off on the way. Last Christmas my present to my aunt arrived without a label, and it took the post office three weeks to find her.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 581, "w": 58, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "My son <mark data-word=\"attach\">attached</mark> a small bell to the cat's collar, so that the birds in the garden could hear her coming. It worked: now she never catches anything, and she sits on the wall looking very annoyed.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 582, "w": 58, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The climbers <mark data-word=\"attach\">attached</mark> themselves to each other with a long rope. If one of them slipped on the ice, the others could hold him. On the steepest part of the mountain, the rope was the only thing between them and a fall of a thousand metres.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 583, "w": 58, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The farmer <mark data-word=\"attach\">attached</mark> the plough to the back of the tractor with a thick metal pin and pulled it hard to check it would hold. Then he climbed into the seat and drove out into the field, where the birds were already waiting for the worms.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 584, "w": 58, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Our old dog's lead had a hook at the end, and you <mark data-word=\"attach\">attach</mark> it to a ring on her collar. The problem was that she knew the sound of that hook. As soon as she heard it click, she started pulling towards the door.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 585, "w": 58, "slot": 5, "also": [38], "text": "The children <mark data-word=\"attach\">attached</mark> a long tail made of ribbons to the bottom of their kite. 'It keeps it <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark> in the wind,' their grandfather explained. Without the tail the kite spun round and round; with it, it flew high and straight.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 586, "w": 58, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "My grandmother <mark data-word=\"attach\">attached</mark> a photo of each grandchild to the fridge with small magnets. There are eleven of us now, and the fridge door is completely covered. When a new baby was born last year, she had to move the shopping list to the side to make space.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 587, "w": 58, "slot": 7, "also": [20], "text": "The builders <mark data-word=\"attach\">attached</mark> the new <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelf</mark> to the wall with four long screws. 'It will hold a hundred kilos,' one of them said. My wife immediately filled it with her cookbooks, which weigh about that much.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 588, "w": 58, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "In old trains the carriages were <mark data-word=\"attach\">attached</mark> to each other with heavy chains and hooks. A railway worker had to stand between two carriages to connect them, and it was one of the most dangerous jobs on the railway.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 589, "w": 58, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "When you apply for a job by email, remember to <mark data-word=\"attach\">attach</mark> your CV to the message. My friend wrote a beautiful letter to twenty companies and forgot the file every time. Only one of them wrote back, to ask where it was.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 590, "w": 59, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "The light bulb in the kitchen went out with a small pop, so I stood on a chair and <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaced</mark> it with a new one. The new bulb was much brighter, and suddenly we could see all the dust on the top of the cupboards.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 591, "w": 59, "slot": 1, "also": [36], "text": "My phone was five years old and very slow, so last month I finally <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaced</mark> it. The new one is fast and takes beautiful photos, but I still miss the old one, which had a <mark data-word=\"crack\">crack</mark> across the screen shaped like a river.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 592, "w": 59, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The old wooden windows in our house let in the cold, so we <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaced</mark> them with new ones. The difference was amazing: that winter the house was warm, and our heating bill was half what it had been.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 593, "w": 59, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "When the goalkeeper hurt his hand in the first half, the coach <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaced</mark> him with a young player from the bench. The boy was only seventeen, but he stopped every shot, and at the end of the match the fans carried him on their shoulders.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 594, "w": 59, "slot": 4, "also": [6], "text": "Every few years the town <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaces</mark> the old trees along the main street. The old ones are cut down, and young ones are planted in the same holes. For a while the street looks <mark data-word=\"bare\">bare</mark>, but after three summers the young trees are green and full.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 595, "w": 59, "slot": 5, "also": [92], "text": "I broke my mother's favourite cup, so I went to four shops to find another one exactly the same. When I <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaced</mark> it, she did not <mark data-word=\"notice\">notice</mark> the difference for a whole month. Then she said, 'This cup feels lighter,' and I had to tell her.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 596, "w": 59, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "In many shops, machines have <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaced</mark> the people at the tills. You scan your own shopping and pay with a card. It is fast, but my grandmother hates it. 'I used to have a chat with the girl,' she says. 'The machine doesn't talk.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 597, "w": 59, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The tyres on my bicycle were old and smooth, so I <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaced</mark> them before the winter. The new ones had deep lines in the rubber and held the road even on wet leaves.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 598, "w": 59, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "My shoes had holes in both soles, so I finally <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaced</mark> them. I carried the old ones to the bin, then stood there for a long time, remembering all the places they had walked with me, before I let them go.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 599, "w": 59, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The school <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaced</mark> its old blackboards with big white screens. The teachers were nervous at first, but the children loved them. Only the old maths teacher kept a piece of chalk in his pocket, just in case.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 600, "w": 60, "slot": 0, "also": [62], "text": "My English did not <mark data-word=\"improve\">improve</mark> because I was living in the country. It improved on the evenings when I was tired and did the twenty minutes anyway. You cannot <mark data-word=\"rely\">rely</mark> on a language arriving by itself. Nobody is bringing it to you.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 601, "w": 60, "slot": 1, "also": [64], "text": "After a fourteen-hour day my grandmother would have said that you <mark data-word=\"deserve\">deserve</mark> a rest, and she would have been right. But those are exactly the evenings that made anything <mark data-word=\"improve\">improve</mark> at all. The easy days taught me nothing whatsoever.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 602, "w": 60, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "At the beginning of the year my handwriting was terrible; even I could not read it. My teacher gave me an exercise book and told me to write one page every evening. By the summer my writing had <mark data-word=\"improve\">improved</mark> so much that my mother asked who had written my homework.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 603, "w": 60, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The new road <mark data-word=\"improve\">improved</mark> life in the village enormously. Before it was built, the trip to the hospital took two hours; now it takes thirty minutes. The shop has more fresh food, and young people no longer have to leave to find work.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 604, "w": 60, "slot": 4, "also": [66], "text": "In the morning, when I came out, the early church-bell was ringing, and he was walking up and down with his mother. He addressed me as if nothing had happened, and I could do no less than reply. I had struck him hard enough to give him the toothache, I <mark data-word=\"suppose\">suppose</mark>. At all events his face was tied up in a black silk handkerchief, which, with his hat perched on the top of it, was far from <mark data-word=\"improve\">improving</mark> his appearance.", "source": "David Copperfield — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 605, "w": 60, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The doctor said my grandfather's health would <mark data-word=\"improve\">improve</mark> if he walked every day. At first he walked to the end of the street and back. After a month he walked to the park, and after three months he was climbing the hill behind the church.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 606, "w": 60, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "\"There, if I didn't forget to tell you his name! I'm as bad as the Man. And he is dirty, too, isn't he? — I mean, the boy is — just like Fluffy and Buffy were when you took them in. But I reckon he'll <mark data-word=\"improve\">improve</mark> all right by washing, just as they did, and — Oh, I 'most forgot again,\" she broke off with a laugh.", "source": "Pollyanna — Eleanor H. Porter"},
  {"id": 607, "w": 60, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "I practised the piano every day for a year, and my playing slowly <mark data-word=\"improve\">improved</mark>. At first I could only play with one hand; by Christmas I could play a whole song for my family, and nobody left the room.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 608, "w": 60, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The weather <mark data-word=\"improve\">improved</mark> in the afternoon. The rain stopped, the clouds moved away, and the sun came out over the wet streets. By five o'clock the cafés had put their tables outside again, and everybody was sitting in the sun.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 609, "w": 60, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Our team was the worst in the league last year, but the new coach <mark data-word=\"improve\">improved</mark> it quickly. He made the players run every morning and taught them to pass the ball instead of shooting from everywhere. This year they finished third.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 610, "w": 61, "slot": 0, "also": [63], "text": "That winter I could not <mark data-word=\"afford\">afford</mark> a teacher, so I had to <mark data-word=\"reduce\">reduce</mark> everything else: no taxis, one coffee a week, shoes repaired instead of replaced. I wanted my English to grow faster than my savings, and for four months it did.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 611, "w": 61, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "When the grey cub came back to life and again took interest in the far white wall, he found that the population of his world had been <mark data-word=\"reduce\">reduced</mark>. Only one sister remained to him. The rest were gone. As he grew stronger, he found himself compelled to play alone, for the sister no longer lifted her head nor moved about. His little body rounded out with the meat he now ate; but the food had come too late for her.", "source": "White Fang — Jack London"},
  {"id": 612, "w": 61, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The shop <mark data-word=\"reduce\">reduced</mark> the price of its winter coats by half at the end of February. My sister, who had been looking at one red coat for three months, ran there before work and bought it for sixty euros instead of a hundred and twenty.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 613, "w": 61, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "To <mark data-word=\"reduce\">reduce</mark> the noise from the busy road, the town built a tall wall of wood and plants along it. The people in the houses behind it say they can now sleep with the windows open, which they could never do before.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 614, "w": 61, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "\"Oh no, dear!\" said Lydgate. \"But look here,\" he continued, drawing a paper from his pocket and opening it; \"here is Dover's account. See, I have marked a number of articles, which if we returned them would <mark data-word=\"reduce\">reduce</mark> the amount by thirty pounds and more. I have not marked any of the jewellery.\" Lydgate had really felt this point of the jewellery very bitter to himself; but he had overcome the feeling by severe argument.", "source": "Middlemarch — George Eliot"},
  {"id": 615, "w": 61, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The doctor told my father to <mark data-word=\"reduce\">reduce</mark> the amount of salt he ate. So my mother put less salt in the soup, less on the potatoes and none on the table. After a month his blood pressure was lower, and he said he could finally taste the food.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 616, "w": 61, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "He went on scrubbing, and paid no attention. I soon saw that he was <mark data-word=\"reduce\">reducing</mark> my size. He bore hard on his mitten, and from under it rolled little cylinders, like maccaroni. It could not be dirt, for it was too white. He pared me down in this way for a long time.", "source": "The Innocents Abroad — Mark Twain"},
  {"id": 617, "w": 61, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The factory <mark data-word=\"reduce\">reduced</mark> the number of workers from five hundred to two hundred when the new machines arrived. The town felt it at once: fewer people in the cafés, fewer children in the school, more empty shops in the main street.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 618, "w": 61, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "'Turn off the lights when you leave a room,' my father always said. 'It will <mark data-word=\"reduce\">reduce</mark> the electricity bill.' We laughed at him, but when we paid our own bills years later, we turned off every light in the house.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 619, "w": 61, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Eating less sugar is the easiest way to <mark data-word=\"reduce\">reduce</mark> the number of holes in your teeth, my dentist says. I stopped putting sugar in my tea, and at my next check-up, for the first time in ten years, she found nothing to fix.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 620, "w": 62, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "You can always <mark data-word=\"rely\">rely</mark> on my grandmother. If she says she will be at the station at six, she is there at a quarter to six, with a flask of tea and something to eat. In seventy years she has never forgotten a birthday.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 621, "w": 62, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "Farmers <mark data-word=\"rely\">rely</mark> on the weather. If the rain does not come in spring, the seeds do not grow; if it comes too heavily in summer, the harvest is ruined. That is why every farmer I know listens to the forecast the way other people listen to the news.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 622, "w": 62, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "Blind people often <mark data-word=\"rely\">rely</mark> on their dogs to guide them through the city. A well-trained dog stops at every kerb, walks around holes in the pavement and waits for the green light. My neighbour says his is the best colleague he has ever had.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 623, "w": 62, "slot": 3, "also": [91], "text": "When I was learning to drive, I <mark data-word=\"rely\">relied</mark> completely on my phone to find the way. One day the battery died in the middle of the countryside, and I <mark data-word=\"realise\">realised</mark> I had no idea where I was. Since then I always keep a paper map in the car.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 624, "w": 62, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Small villages in the mountains <mark data-word=\"rely\">rely</mark> on one road. When snow closes it in winter, nobody can get in or out, and the shop runs out of bread in three days. So in November every family fills its cupboards as if for a long journey.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 625, "w": 62, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "\"So you ought to be. You want to cut a figure in the world, and I reckon Peter Featherstone is the only one you've got to trust to.\" Here the old man's eyes gleamed with a curiously mingled satisfaction in the consciousness that this smart young fellow <mark data-word=\"rely\">relied</mark> upon him, and that the smart young fellow was rather a fool for doing so.", "source": "Middlemarch — George Eliot"},
  {"id": 626, "w": 62, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "You can <mark data-word=\"rely\">rely</mark> on the six-forty bus. It is old and it smells of diesel, but in nine years it has failed me twice, and both times there was snow on the road.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 627, "w": 62, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "My grandmother could not read a map, so she <mark data-word=\"rely\">relied</mark> on landmarks instead: the blue gate, the tree that was struck by lightning, the shop that used to be a bakery. She never once got lost.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 628, "w": 62, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The whole morning <mark data-word=\"rely\">relies</mark> on one man remembering to unlock the yard at six. Nobody has ever written it down anywhere. When he was ill in February we stood outside in the rain for forty minutes.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 629, "w": 62, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Do not <mark data-word=\"rely\">rely</mark> on your memory in this job. Write the number on your hand if you have to. I lost half a Tuesday once because I was certain the part was a twelve, and it was a two.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 630, "w": 63, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "It was not till he was twenty years old that the family could ever <mark data-word=\"afford\">afford</mark> to go away for a holiday. Mrs. Morel had never been away for a holiday, except to see her sister, since she had been married. Now at last Paul had saved enough money, and they were all going.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 631, "w": 63, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "We wanted a bigger flat, but we could not <mark data-word=\"afford\">afford</mark> one in the city centre. The rents there were twice what we earned together. So we moved to a small town an hour away, where we have a garden and my son has his own room.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 632, "w": 63, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "When I was a student, I could not <mark data-word=\"afford\">afford</mark> to eat in restaurants. I cooked big pots of rice and beans on Sunday and ate them all week. Now, when I see students counting coins at the supermarket, I remember exactly how it felt.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 633, "w": 63, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "My grandfather saved for ten years before he could <mark data-word=\"afford\">afford</mark> his first car. It was small and old and green, and he washed it every Sunday. When he finally sold it, forty years later, he cried.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 634, "w": 63, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "December came, and some snow. He stayed at home all the while now. They could not <mark data-word=\"afford\">afford</mark> a nurse. Annie came to look after her mother; the parish nurse, whom they loved, came in morning and evening.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 635, "w": 63, "slot": 5, "also": [67], "text": "\"I am afraid,\" said she, <mark data-word=\"hesitate\">hesitating</mark>, \"whichever I choose I shall wish I had taken another. Look at this lovely crimson! it would be so warm in winter. But spring is coming on, you know. I wish I could have a gown for every season,\" said she, dropping her voice—as we all did in Cranford whenever we talked of anything we wished for but could not <mark data-word=\"afford\">afford</mark>.", "source": "Cranford — Elizabeth Cleghorn Gaskell"},
  {"id": 636, "w": 63, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The concert tickets cost two hundred euros each. My friend could <mark data-word=\"afford\">afford</mark> them, because she had a good job; I could not, because I was still at university. She went, and she sent me a video of every song.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 637, "w": 63, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The school trip to Paris cost more than many families could <mark data-word=\"afford\">afford</mark>, so the teachers organised a cake sale, a car wash and a concert. By June they had raised enough money for every child in the class to go.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 638, "w": 63, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "'Can we <mark data-word=\"afford\">afford</mark> a new washing machine?' my mother asked, looking at the bank statement. My father did the sums on the back of an envelope. 'If we don't go on holiday this year,' he said. So we stayed at home, and the clothes were clean.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 639, "w": 63, "slot": 9, "also": [92], "text": "When prices in the market went up, many old people could no longer <mark data-word=\"afford\">afford</mark> fresh fruit. A young farmer <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticed</mark>, and every Saturday at closing time he gave away the apples and pears he had not sold.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 640, "w": 64, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "\"Do you know, I was so angry, Kitty,\" Alice went on as soon as they were comfortably settled again, \"when I saw all the mischief you had been doing, I was very nearly opening the window, and putting you out into the snow! And you'd have <mark data-word=\"deserve\">deserved</mark> it, you little mischievous darling! What have you got to say for yourself? Now don't interrupt me!\" she went on, holding up one finger.", "source": "Through the Looking-Glass — Lewis Carroll"},
  {"id": 641, "w": 64, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "After working every weekend for six months, my mother <mark data-word=\"deserve\">deserved</mark> a holiday. So we sent her to the sea for a week, with no phone, no work and no children. She came back brown and smiling, and she slept for two days.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 642, "w": 64, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "'Mr. Peggotty,' he said, 'you are a thoroughly good fellow, and <mark data-word=\"deserve\">deserve</mark> to be as happy as you are tonight. My hand upon it! Ham, I give you joy, my boy. My hand upon that, too!", "source": "David Copperfield — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 643, "w": 64, "slot": 3, "also": [22], "text": "The team played brilliantly and <mark data-word=\"deserve\">deserved</mark> to win, but in the last minute the other side scored a lucky goal. The players sat on the grass, too tired to be angry. The <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> stood and clapped them anyway.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 644, "w": 64, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "My sister studied every night for a year, and she got the highest mark in the whole school. She <mark data-word=\"deserve\">deserved</mark> it more than anyone I know. At the ceremony my father clapped so loudly that people turned round to look at him.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 645, "w": 64, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The dog saved the little girl from the river, and the whole town said he <mark data-word=\"deserve\">deserved</mark> a medal. The mayor gave him one — a small silver disc on a red ribbon — and a very large bone, which he liked much better.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 646, "w": 64, "slot": 6, "also": [43], "text": "'You were <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark> to your teacher, and you <mark data-word=\"deserve\">deserve</mark> to be punished,' said my father. 'No phone for a week.' I wanted to argue, but I knew he was right. I had said something terrible, and I was ashamed.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 647, "w": 64, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "Eight years before he had seen his friend off at the North Wall and wished him godspeed. Gallaher had got on. You could tell that at once by his travelled air, his well-cut tweed suit, and fearless accent. Few fellows had talents like his and fewer still could remain unspoiled by such success. Gallaher's heart was in the right place and he had <mark data-word=\"deserve\">deserved</mark> to win.", "source": "Dubliners — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 648, "w": 64, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The waiter was friendly, fast and funny, and he brought my son an extra ice cream for free. 'He <mark data-word=\"deserve\">deserves</mark> a big tip,' said my wife, and for once I agreed with her immediately.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 649, "w": 64, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "'Nobody <mark data-word=\"deserve\">deserves</mark> to be treated like that,' said the teacher when she saw the older boys laughing at the new girl. 'Not her, not you, not anyone.' The boys went quiet and looked at their shoes.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 650, "w": 65, "slot": 0, "also": [66], "text": "My worst habit was this one: I would <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretend</mark> that I had understood, smile, and walk away with nothing. I <mark data-word=\"suppose\">suppose</mark> half the people in that city were doing exactly the same thing in a different language.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 651, "w": 65, "slot": 1, "also": [68], "text": "I <mark data-word=\"struggle\">struggled</mark> with long words for two years. Not with the meaning — with the mouth. And a man who cannot say a word properly begins to <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretend</mark> that he does not need it, and then his English stops growing where his courage stopped.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 652, "w": 65, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "Paul sat <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretending</mark> to read. He knew his mother wanted to upbraid him. He also wanted to know what had made her ill, for he was troubled. So, instead of running away to bed, as he would have liked to do, he sat and waited.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 653, "w": 65, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "Her mind began to play with thoughts of making friends with John Hardy. When he came into the room with the wood in his arms, she <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretended</mark> to be busy with her studies but watched him eagerly. When he had put the wood in the box and turned to go out, she put down her head and blushed.", "source": "Winesburg, Ohio: A Group of Tales of Ohio Small Town Life — Sherwood Anderson"},
  {"id": 654, "w": 65, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "When my little brother does not want to go to school, he <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretends</mark> to be ill. He holds his stomach, speaks in a weak voice and asks for tea. But as soon as my mother says he can stay at home, he jumps out of bed and turns on the television.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 655, "w": 65, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Before leaving the house, Vronsky went into her room. She would have <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretended</mark> to be looking for something on the table, but ashamed of making a pretense, she looked straight in his face with cold eyes.", "source": "Anna Karenina — graf Leo Tolstoy"},
  {"id": 656, "w": 65, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "To induce her to look up he <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretended</mark> to be going away, and when this failed he sat on the end of the bed and tapped her gently with his foot. \"Wendy,\" he said, \"don't withdraw. I can't help crowing, Wendy, when I'm pleased with myself.\" Still she would not look up, though she was listening eagerly.", "source": "Peter Pan — J. M. Barrie"},
  {"id": 657, "w": 65, "slot": 7, "also": [94], "text": "We rowed on all that day through the rain, and very melancholy work it was. We <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretended</mark>, at first, that we enjoyed it. We said it was a change, and that we liked to see the river under all its different aspects. We said we could not <mark data-word=\"expect\">expect</mark> to have it all sunshine, nor should we wish it. We told each other that Nature was beautiful, even in her tears.", "source": "Three men in a boat (to say nothing of the dog) — Jerome K. Jerome"},
  {"id": 658, "w": 65, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Children love to <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretend</mark>. My daughter spends hours <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretending</mark> to be a doctor, a shopkeeper or a dragon, and she changes her voice for each one. Yesterday she was a cat all afternoon and would only drink milk from a bowl.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 659, "w": 65, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "I saw my old teacher across the street, but I was in a hurry, so I <mark data-word=\"pretend\">pretended</mark> not to see him and looked at my phone. He called my name anyway, loudly and happily, and I felt terrible. We had coffee, and I was late for everything else.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 660, "w": 66, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "'Where's Dad?' asked my little sister. 'I <mark data-word=\"suppose\">suppose</mark> he's still at work,' said my mother, looking at the clock. 'He usually comes home at six, and it's only half past five.' She was right: at ten past six we heard his key in the door.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 661, "w": 66, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "On the bench in the garden Seth stirred uneasily. Releasing the hand of the girl, he thrust his hands into his trouser pockets. A desire to impress the mind of his companion with the importance of the resolution he had made came over him and he nodded his head toward the house. \"Mother'll make a fuss, I <mark data-word=\"suppose\">suppose</mark>,\" he whispered.", "source": "Winesburg, Ohio: A Group of Tales of Ohio Small Town Life — Sherwood Anderson"},
  {"id": 662, "w": 66, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The streets were wet and the grass was shining when I woke up, so I <mark data-word=\"suppose\">supposed</mark> it had rained in the night. Later my neighbour told me the truth: the town had washed the streets at five in the morning, and his garden hose had been on all night.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 663, "w": 66, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "'I don't know where my keys are,' I said. 'I <mark data-word=\"suppose\">suppose</mark> I left them in the car.' It was the most likely place, because I always forget them there. I went down to the street — and there they were, still hanging in the door.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 664, "w": 66, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The restaurant was empty on a Saturday evening, so we <mark data-word=\"suppose\">supposed</mark> the food must be bad. We went to the busy place across the street instead. The next day we read that the empty restaurant had just opened, and it had the best reviews in town.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 665, "w": 66, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "All children, except one, grow up. They soon know that they will grow up, and the way Wendy knew was this. One day when she was two years old she was playing in a garden, and she plucked another flower and ran with it to her mother. I <mark data-word=\"suppose\">suppose</mark> she must have looked rather delightful, for Mrs. Darling put her hand to her heart and cried, \"Oh, why can't you remain like this for ever!\"", "source": "Peter Pan — J. M. Barrie"},
  {"id": 666, "w": 66, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Nobody answered when I rang the bell, and all the windows were dark. I <mark data-word=\"suppose\">supposed</mark> my friends had gone out, so I left the present by the door. In fact, they were all hiding behind the sofa, waiting to shout 'Surprise!' — and they waited for an hour.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 667, "w": 66, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "'Is the shop open on Sundays?' 'I <mark data-word=\"suppose\">suppose</mark> so,' said my father. 'Most shops are these days.' But when we got there, the door was locked, and a small sign said: Closed on Sundays. We should have checked.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 668, "w": 66, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "He was wearing a suit and carrying a heavy bag, so I <mark data-word=\"suppose\">supposed</mark> he was a businessman. When we started talking on the train, I found out he was a clown on his way to a children's party. The bag was full of balloons.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 669, "w": 66, "slot": 9, "also": [17], "text": "\"I <mark data-word=\"suppose\">suppose</mark> soldiers feel like this when they are on a long and <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark> march,\" she often said to herself. She liked the sound of the phrase, \"long and <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark> march.\" It made her feel rather like a soldier.", "source": "A Little Princess — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 670, "w": 67, "slot": 0, "also": [69], "text": "My first <mark data-word=\"attempt\">attempt</mark> at the telephone was a failure — I <mark data-word=\"hesitate\">hesitated</mark> so long that the man at the other end said hello three times and put the phone down. The second attempt came two days later. I wrote the first sentence on paper before I dialled.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 671, "w": 67, "slot": 1, "also": [91], "text": "The woman <mark data-word=\"hesitate\">hesitated</mark>. She did not <mark data-word=\"realise\">realize</mark> it herself, but she had so long been accustomed to wanting what she did not have, that to state off-hand what she DID want seemed impossible — until she knew what she had. Obviously, however, she must say something.", "source": "Pollyanna — Eleanor H. Porter"},
  {"id": 672, "w": 67, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "As they came out of church, many of those who had attended the service stood waiting to see them pass. As they neared the gate, a man who stood with his hat in his hand made a step forward and then <mark data-word=\"hesitate\">hesitated</mark>. He was a middle-aged farmer, with a careworn face.", "source": "Little Lord Fauntleroy — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 673, "w": 67, "slot": 3, "also": [50], "text": "The little girl stood at the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark> of the pool and <mark data-word=\"hesitate\">hesitated</mark>. The water looked cold and deep, and she was not sure she could do it. She looked at her father, looked at the water, took one step back — and then jumped.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 674, "w": 67, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "He stood <mark data-word=\"hesitate\">hesitating</mark> in the gateway. To go into the street, to go a walk for appearance' sake was revolting; to go back to his room, even more revolting. \"And what a chance I have lost for ever!\" he muttered, standing aimlessly in the gateway, just opposite the porter's little dark room, which was also open. Suddenly he started. From the porter's room, two paces away from him, something shining under the bench to the right caught his eye....", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 675, "w": 67, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "When the waiter asked what I wanted, I <mark data-word=\"hesitate\">hesitated</mark>. The fish sounded good, but so did the pasta, and I had never tried the soup. 'Take your time,' he said kindly, and went to another table while I looked at the menu again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 676, "w": 67, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "\"No, I must go. I shall probably find some work awaiting me. I would stay to show you over the house, but Barrymore will be a better guide than I. Good-bye, and never <mark data-word=\"hesitate\">hesitate</mark> night or day to send for me if I can be of service.\"", "source": "The Hound of the Baskervilles — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 677, "w": 67, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "I was surprised at my own emotion in seeing her. She held me at arm's length for a moment, a hand on each shoulder, and looked at me with a sort of glad scrutiny. She seemed to <mark data-word=\"hesitate\">hesitate</mark>, and then pecked little kiss off my cheek.", "source": "Tono-Bungay — H. G. Wells"},
  {"id": 678, "w": 67, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "I <mark data-word=\"hesitate\">hesitated</mark>. Her lips moved, and though they formed no words, she commanded me with her eyes, plainly as speech, to go to the help of the unfortunate man. \"Please,\" she managed to whisper, and I could but obey.", "source": "The Sea-Wolf — Jack London"},
  {"id": 679, "w": 67, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "'Don't <mark data-word=\"hesitate\">hesitate</mark> to call me if you need anything,' said the nurse as she left. 'Even at three in the morning — that's what I'm here for.' At three in the morning my father's pain came back, and I pressed the button at once.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 680, "w": 68, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "My grandmother <mark data-word=\"struggle\">struggles</mark> with the stairs now. She holds the rail with both hands and stops to rest on every third step. It takes her five minutes to get to the top, but she refuses to move to a flat on the ground floor.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 681, "w": 68, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "When I first moved to Germany, I <mark data-word=\"struggle\">struggled</mark> to understand anything. People spoke too fast, the words were too long, and every letter from the bank took me an hour with a dictionary. After a year it became easier, and after two years I was dreaming in German.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 682, "w": 68, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The old man <mark data-word=\"struggle\">struggled</mark> to open the heavy door of the post office. His hands shook, and the wind kept pushing it shut. A young woman ran over and held it open for him, and he thanked her three times.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 683, "w": 68, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "After the factory closed, many families in our street <mark data-word=\"struggle\">struggled</mark> to pay their bills. Parents took two jobs, children wore their older brothers' clothes, and my mother started a free soup kitchen in the church hall on Fridays.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 684, "w": 68, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "It was after midnight, and I was <mark data-word=\"struggle\">struggling</mark> to stay awake. My eyes kept closing over the book, and I read the same sentence five times. In the end I gave up, put the book on my face and fell asleep on the sofa.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 685, "w": 68, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The small boat <mark data-word=\"struggle\">struggled</mark> against the wind and the waves, moving forward only a few metres every minute. The two fishermen pulled on the oars with all their strength, and after an hour, wet and exhausted, they finally reached the harbour.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 686, "w": 68, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "My son <mark data-word=\"struggle\">struggled</mark> with maths for years. Numbers seemed to swim on the page, and he cried over his homework almost every evening. Then a new teacher showed him how to draw the problems as pictures, and slowly, slowly, it began to make sense.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 687, "w": 68, "slot": 7, "also": [35], "text": "The mother cat <mark data-word=\"struggle\">struggled</mark> to carry her kitten up the steps, holding it gently by the neck. It was almost too big for her now, and its feet <mark data-word=\"drag\">dragged</mark> on every stair. At the top she put it down and lay next to it, breathing hard.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 688, "w": 68, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "With her broken arm, Mia <mark data-word=\"struggle\">struggled</mark> to do the simplest things: tying her shoes, cutting bread, washing her hair. After a week she had learned to do almost everything with her left hand, and she was very proud of her new handwriting.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 689, "w": 68, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The old car <mark data-word=\"struggle\">struggled</mark> up the steep hill, its engine making a terrible noise. Halfway up, my father asked us all to get out and walk. Without us inside, it just made it to the top, and we climbed back in, laughing.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 690, "w": 69, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "My first <mark data-word=\"attempt\">attempt</mark> at baking bread was a disaster. The loaf came out of the oven flat, hard and black on the bottom. My second <mark data-word=\"attempt\">attempt</mark> was a little better, and by the tenth, my family was asking for more.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 691, "w": 69, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "Last of our visitors, there was Mr. Jellyby. Really when he used to come in of an evening, and ask Caddy in his meek voice how she was, and then sit down with his head against the wall, and make no <mark data-word=\"attempt\">attempt</mark> to say anything more, I liked him very much.", "source": "Bleak House — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 692, "w": 69, "slot": 2, "also": [94], "text": "Providence had taken her at her word. Here was the task, not what she had <mark data-word=\"expect\">expected</mark>, but better because self had no part in it. Now, could she do it? She decided that she would try, and in her first <mark data-word=\"attempt\">attempt</mark> she found the helps I have suggested.", "source": "Little Women — Louisa May Alcott"},
  {"id": 693, "w": 69, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The prisoner made three <mark data-word=\"attempt\">attempts</mark> to escape. The first time he climbed the wall, the second time he dug a tunnel, and the third time he hid in the laundry van. All three times he was caught within a day.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 694, "w": 69, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "After two failed <mark data-word=\"attempt\">attempts</mark>, my father passed his driving test at the age of fifty-five. He drove straight to our house, sounded the horn until we all came out, and took us for a very slow ride round the block.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 695, "w": 69, "slot": 5, "also": [7], "text": "Maud received my report with obvious relief, and the sight of smoke which later rose in the galley put her in a more <mark data-word=\"cheerful\">cheerful</mark> mood. The next day, and the next, we saw the galley smoke rising, and sometimes we caught glimpses of him on the poop. But that was all. He made no <mark data-word=\"attempt\">attempt</mark> to come ashore. This we knew, for we still maintained our night-watches.", "source": "The Sea-Wolf — Jack London"},
  {"id": 696, "w": 69, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "In 1953, after many <mark data-word=\"attempt\">attempts</mark> by climbers from different countries, two men finally reached the top of Everest. Earlier teams had turned back because of storms, lack of oxygen or exhaustion. This time the weather was kind, and the two men stood on the highest point on earth.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 697, "w": 69, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "I set off one morning, accompanied by Charley, for Somers Town. As I approached the house, I was strongly inclined to turn back, for I felt what a desperate <mark data-word=\"attempt\">attempt</mark> it was to make an impression on Mr. Skimpole and how extremely likely it was that he would signally defeat me. However, I thought that being there, I would go through with it.", "source": "Bleak House — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 698, "w": 69, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "He had scarcely moved since her arrival. Sometimes his eyes were open, sometimes closed. When they were open, there was no meaning in their unwinking stare at one spot straight before them, unless for a moment the brow knitted into a faint expression of anger, or surprise. Then, Mortimer Lightwood would speak to him, and on occasions he would be so far roused as to make an <mark data-word=\"attempt\">attempt</mark> to pronounce his friend's name.", "source": "Our Mutual Friend — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 699, "w": 69, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "I assented gladly, and we all descended together. Our prisoner made no <mark data-word=\"attempt\">attempt</mark> at escape, but stepped calmly into the cab which had been his, and we followed him. Lestrade mounted the box, whipped up the horse, and brought us in a very short time to our destination.", "source": "A Study in Scarlet — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 700, "w": 70, "slot": 0, "also": [72], "text": "My uncle <mark data-word=\"rarely\">rarely</mark> left the village — four times in forty years, he said, and twice of those for funerals. <mark data-word=\"eventually\">Eventually</mark> he came to visit me, took one look at the traffic outside the station, and said he understood now why I telephoned on Sundays instead of writing.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 701, "w": 70, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "My grandfather <mark data-word=\"rarely\">rarely</mark> watched television. Maybe once a month, when there was an important football match, he sat in front of it with a cup of tea. The rest of the time he read, worked in the garden or talked to his neighbours over the fence.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 702, "w": 70, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "There was another question he could not decide: why were they all so fond of Sonia? She did not try to win their favour; she <mark data-word=\"rarely\">rarely</mark> met them, sometimes only she came to see him at work for a moment. And yet everybody knew her, they knew that she had come out to follow him, knew how and where she lived. She never gave them money, did them no particular services.", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 703, "w": 70, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "Dinner was laid in the best of these rooms; the second was his dressing-room; the third, his bedroom. He told us that he held the whole house, but <mark data-word=\"rarely\">rarely</mark> used more of it than we saw.", "source": "Great Expectations — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 704, "w": 70, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "It <mark data-word=\"rarely\">rarely</mark> snows in our town — maybe one day every five or six years. So when we woke up last January and the streets were white, nobody went to work. The whole town was outside, building snowmen and throwing snowballs like children.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 705, "w": 70, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "My brother lives in Australia, so we <mark data-word=\"rarely\">rarely</mark> see each other. The last time was three years ago, at our sister's wedding. We talk on the phone every Sunday, but it's not the same as sitting at the same table.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 706, "w": 70, "slot": 6, "also": [95], "text": "Our cat <mark data-word=\"rarely\">rarely</mark> comes into the living room. She <mark data-word=\"prefer\">prefers</mark> the kitchen, where the food is, and the bedroom, where the soft blanket is. When she does walk in and sit on the sofa, we all stop talking and look at her in surprise.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 707, "w": 70, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "In this part of the desert it <mark data-word=\"rarely\">rarely</mark> rains — sometimes not once in a whole year. But when it does, the sand is covered with flowers within a few days, and people drive from far away to see them before they disappear.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 708, "w": 70, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "She <mark data-word=\"rarely\">rarely</mark> varied from her swinging, forward, intense walk. Occasionally she ran with Paul down the fields. Then her eyes blazed naked in a kind of ecstasy that frightened him. But she was physically afraid. If she were getting over a stile, she gripped his hands in a little hard anguish, and began to lose her presence of mind.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 709, "w": 70, "slot": 9, "also": [35], "text": "Round and round he went, whirling and turning and reversing, trying to shake off the fifty-pound weight that <mark data-word=\"drag\">dragged</mark> at his throat. The bull-dog did little but keep his grip. Sometimes, and <mark data-word=\"rarely\">rarely</mark>, he managed to get his feet to the earth and for a moment to brace himself against White Fang. But the next moment his footing would be lost and he would be <mark data-word=\"drag\">dragging</mark> around in the whirl of one of White Fang's mad gyrations. Cherokee identified himself with his instinct.", "source": "White Fang — Jack London"},
  {"id": 710, "w": 71, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "The first time I ran five kilometres, I <mark data-word=\"barely\">barely</mark> made it to the end. My legs were shaking, I could hardly breathe, and I finished just before the park closed. A year later I ran the same five kilometres in half the time.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 711, "w": 71, "slot": 1, "also": [73], "text": "In January I <mark data-word=\"barely\">barely</mark> had enough money for the rent and I did not tell my grandmother. But something was changing <mark data-word=\"gradually\">gradually</mark>, underneath everything. One evening I watched a whole film without subtitles and followed most of it, and I had not even been trying.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 712, "w": 71, "slot": 2, "also": [37], "text": "He slipped an arm through hers, as Eady had done, and fancied it was faintly pressed against her side, but neither of them moved. It was so dark under the spruces that he could <mark data-word=\"barely\">barely</mark> see the shape of her head beside his shoulder. He longed to stoop his cheek and <mark data-word=\"rub\">rub</mark> it against her scarf. He would have liked to stand there with her all night in the blackness.", "source": "Ethan Frome — Edith Wharton"},
  {"id": 713, "w": 71, "slot": 3, "also": [52], "text": "We <mark data-word=\"barely\">barely</mark> caught the train. We ran onto the platform just as the doors were closing, and my friend put his foot in the <mark data-word=\"gap\">gap</mark> to stop them. We fell into our seats, breathing hard, and the train started to move.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 714, "w": 71, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "17 September. Night. — I write this and leave it to be seen, so that no one may by any chance get into trouble through me. This is an exact record of what took place to-night. I feel I am dying of weakness, and have <mark data-word=\"barely\">barely</mark> strength to write, but it must be done if I die in the doing.", "source": "Dracula — Bram Stoker"},
  {"id": 715, "w": 71, "slot": 5, "also": [32], "text": "The old man spoke so quietly that I could <mark data-word=\"barely\">barely</mark> hear him. I had to <mark data-word=\"lean\">lean</mark> close to his mouth to catch the words. 'Thank you,' he was saying, again and again. 'Thank you for coming.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 716, "w": 71, "slot": 6, "also": [20], "text": "After paying the rent, we had <mark data-word=\"barely\">barely</mark> enough money for food. For three months we ate soup, bread and potatoes, and at the end of each week there were only a few coins left in the jar on the kitchen <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelf</mark>.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 717, "w": 71, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The baby was so tired that she could <mark data-word=\"barely\">barely</mark> keep her eyes open. Her head kept falling forward over her bowl of soup, and twice her nose almost went into it. Her mother picked her up, and she was asleep before they reached the bedroom.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 718, "w": 71, "slot": 8, "also": [68], "text": "Leach's rage was no longer impotent. Here at last was something ready to hand. And for the first time since the stabbing the Cockney had appeared outside the galley without his knife. The words had <mark data-word=\"barely\">barely</mark> left his mouth when he was knocked down by Leach. Three times he <mark data-word=\"struggle\">struggled</mark> to his feet, striving to gain the galley, and each time was knocked down.", "source": "The Sea-Wolf — Jack London"},
  {"id": 719, "w": 71, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "She paused at the mouth of the cave and looked the wall over carefully. Then, on one side and the other, she ran along the base of the wall to where its abrupt bulk merged from the softer-lined landscape. Returning to the cave, she entered its narrow mouth. For a short three feet she was compelled to crouch, then the walls widened and rose higher in a little round chamber nearly six feet in diameter. The roof <mark data-word=\"barely\">barely</mark> cleared her head.", "source": "White Fang — Jack London"},
  {"id": 720, "w": 72, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "\"I have no doubt that anxiety and exposure have driven him off his head. He has rushed about the moor in a crazy state and <mark data-word=\"eventually\">eventually</mark> fallen over here and broken his neck.\"", "source": "The Hound of the Baskervilles — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 721, "w": 72, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "We waited for the bus for forty minutes in the rain. People came, looked at the timetable and left. <mark data-word=\"eventually\">Eventually</mark>, just as we had decided to walk, it came round the corner — and behind it came two more.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 722, "w": 72, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "My parents argued about a name for the new puppy for two weeks. Dad wanted Max; Mum wanted Bella. <mark data-word=\"eventually\">Eventually</mark> they asked my little sister, who called him Potato. He is still called Potato.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 723, "w": 72, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "I tried to fix the old radio for a whole weekend. I took it apart, cleaned every piece and put it back together three times. <mark data-word=\"eventually\">Eventually</mark>, late on Sunday night, it made a small noise and then began to play music.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 724, "w": 72, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Our cat was missing for six weeks. We put up posters, asked the neighbours and walked the streets every evening, calling her name. <mark data-word=\"eventually\">Eventually</mark> we stopped hoping. Then one morning she was sitting on the doorstep, thin and dirty, as if she had only gone out for an hour.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 725, "w": 72, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Learning to swim took me a very long time. For months I could only float, then only swim a few metres before I sank. But I went to the pool every week, and <mark data-word=\"eventually\">eventually</mark>, when I was nearly forty, I swam my first full length.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 726, "w": 72, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The old house stood empty for years. Its windows were broken, and the garden became a small forest. <mark data-word=\"eventually\">Eventually</mark> a young couple bought it, and after two years of hard work it was the prettiest house in the street.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 727, "w": 72, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "He and I went up to London. He had the advice of an eminent oculist; and he <mark data-word=\"eventually\">eventually</mark> recovered the sight of that one eye. He cannot now see very distinctly: he cannot read or write much; but he can find his way without being led by the hand: the sky is no longer a blank to him—the earth no longer a void.", "source": "Jane Eyre: An Autobiography — Charlotte Brontë"},
  {"id": 728, "w": 72, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "My grandmother wrote letters to the town council about the broken street light for eleven months. They never answered. But she kept writing, one letter a week, and <mark data-word=\"eventually\">eventually</mark> a man in a yellow van came and fixed it. She framed the last letter.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 729, "w": 72, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "At first my sister hated her new school. She cried every morning and said she had no friends. But slowly things changed, and <mark data-word=\"eventually\">eventually</mark> she made two close friends. Now she cries when the summer holidays begin, because she has to leave them.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 730, "w": 73, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "After the operation, my mother's strength came back <mark data-word=\"gradually\">gradually</mark>. In the first week she could only sit up in bed; in the second she walked to the window; by the end of the month she was cooking dinner again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 731, "w": 73, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "He said it as if he was so accustomed to the idea that it had ceased to matter to him at all. He seemed to like the sound of Mary's voice. As she went on talking he listened in a drowsy, interested way. Once or twice she wondered if he were not <mark data-word=\"gradually\">gradually</mark> falling into a doze. But at last he asked a question which opened up a new subject.", "source": "The Secret Garden — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 732, "w": 73, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "As the sun went down, the sky <mark data-word=\"gradually\">gradually</mark> changed colour — from blue to yellow, then to orange and pink, and at last to dark purple. We sat on the beach and watched it without speaking until the first stars came out.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 733, "w": 73, "slot": 3, "also": [60, 92], "text": "My English <mark data-word=\"improve\">improved</mark> <mark data-word=\"gradually\">gradually</mark>. There was no single day when I suddenly understood everything. But one evening I <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticed</mark> that I had watched a whole film without subtitles, and I could not remember when I had stopped needing them.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 734, "w": 73, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The water in the lake is very cold at first, so walk in <mark data-word=\"gradually\">gradually</mark>: first your feet, then your knees, then your waist. After a few minutes your body gets used to it, and then you can swim.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 735, "w": 73, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The village <mark data-word=\"gradually\">gradually</mark> became a town. First a railway station was built, then a factory, then new streets of houses for the workers. My grandmother, who was born there, says she did not see it happening — she only found one day that she did not know her neighbours.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 736, "w": 73, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "At last, after about an hour, she rapped long and low at the window. <mark data-word=\"gradually\">Gradually</mark> the sound penetrated to him. When, in despair, she had ceased to tap, she saw him stir, then lift his face blindly.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 737, "w": 73, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The noise of the party <mark data-word=\"gradually\">gradually</mark> got quieter as the guests went home, one by one. By two in the morning there were only three of us left in the kitchen, eating the last of the cake and talking about nothing.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 738, "w": 73, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "When you start running, increase the distance <mark data-word=\"gradually\">gradually</mark>. Run two kilometres in the first week, three in the second, four in the third. If you try to run ten on the first day, you will hurt your legs and give up.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 739, "w": 73, "slot": 9, "also": [50], "text": "As he sat gazing into the clear running of the water, Archibald Craven <mark data-word=\"gradually\">gradually</mark> felt his mind and body both grow quiet, as quiet as the valley itself. He wondered if he were going to sleep, but he was not. He sat and gazed at the sunlit water and his eyes began to see things growing at its <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark>.", "source": "The Secret Garden — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 740, "w": 74, "slot": 0, "also": [78], "text": "The call was <mark data-word=\"indeed\">indeed</mark> difficult. I asked the man to repeat himself twice and my voice was <mark data-word=\"slightly\">slightly</mark> too loud, the way it goes when you are frightened. But I got the delivery date, and I wrote it down correctly, and that was the whole job.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 741, "w": 74, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "The soup was <mark data-word=\"slightly\">slightly</mark> too salty, so I added a little water and a few potatoes. It was not perfect, but it was much better. My guests said nothing and asked for a second bowl, which I took as a good sign.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 742, "w": 74, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "\"Advance, fair damsels,\" she said in her happy dream-voice, \"and be seated at the banquet table. My noble father, the king, who is absent on a long journey, has commanded me to feast you.\" She turned her head <mark data-word=\"slightly\">slightly</mark> toward the corner of the room.", "source": "A Little Princess — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 743, "w": 74, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The new shoes were <mark data-word=\"slightly\">slightly</mark> too big — only half a size, but enough for my heel to move up and down when I walked. I put a thin extra sole inside them, and after that they fitted perfectly.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 744, "w": 74, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "My brother is <mark data-word=\"slightly\">slightly</mark> taller than me — only one centimetre. But he is very proud of that centimetre, and every time we stand next to each other, he stands on his toes to make it three.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 745, "w": 74, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The train was <mark data-word=\"slightly\">slightly</mark> late, only five minutes, so we did not miss our connection. We walked, not ran, to the next platform and even had time to buy a coffee.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 746, "w": 74, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "All night long I watched by her. She never stirred, but slept on and on in a deep, tranquil, life-giving, health-giving sleep. Her lips were <mark data-word=\"slightly\">slightly</mark> parted, and her breast rose and fell with the regularity of a pendulum. There was a smile on her face, and it was evident that no bad dreams had come to disturb her peace of mind.", "source": "Dracula — Bram Stoker"},
  {"id": 747, "w": 74, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "I opened the window <mark data-word=\"slightly\">slightly</mark>, just a few centimetres, to let some fresh air into the room. It was enough: the heavy smell of paint went out, the cool evening came in, and my headache began to go away.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 748, "w": 74, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The picture on the wall was <mark data-word=\"slightly\">slightly</mark> crooked. Most people did not see it, but my aunt could not stop looking at it. In the end she stood up in the middle of dinner and moved it one centimetre to the left, and then she could eat.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 749, "w": 74, "slot": 9, "also": [96], "text": "The price has gone up <mark data-word=\"slightly\">slightly</mark> since last year — from two euros to two euros ten. Nobody <mark data-word=\"complain\">complains</mark> about ten cents, but my grandfather says that is how it always starts.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 750, "w": 75, "slot": 0, "also": [77], "text": "It costs <mark data-word=\"roughly\">roughly</mark> two hundred for the part, and <mark data-word=\"besides\">besides</mark> that you lose a day waiting for it to arrive. Which is why the old men keep everything: a shelf of broken taps that will save them one Tuesday in five years.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 751, "w": 75, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "It takes <mark data-word=\"roughly\">roughly</mark> an hour to drive to the airport — sometimes fifty minutes, sometimes seventy, depending on the traffic. To be safe, we always leave two hours before the flight.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 752, "w": 75, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "There were <mark data-word=\"roughly\">roughly</mark> two hundred people at the wedding. Nobody knew the exact number, because some guests brought friends and some did not come at all, but the cook had made food for two hundred and there was nothing left.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 753, "w": 75, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "My grandmother never measures anything when she cooks. She takes <mark data-word=\"roughly\">roughly</mark> a cup of flour, <mark data-word=\"roughly\">roughly</mark> two eggs — 'a big one and a small one' — and a bit of sugar. And somehow her cakes are always perfect.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 754, "w": 75, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The fish I caught was <mark data-word=\"roughly\">roughly</mark> forty centimetres long. I did not have a ruler, so I measured it against my arm. My friends said it was probably smaller; I say it was probably bigger.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 755, "w": 75, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The journey from London to Edinburgh by train takes <mark data-word=\"roughly\">roughly</mark> four and a half hours. If you are lucky, you can see the sea from the window for the last part of the trip.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 756, "w": 75, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "<mark data-word=\"roughly\">Roughly</mark> half of the students in my class walk to school. The rest come by bus or by bike, and three are driven by their parents, which the others think is very funny.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 757, "w": 75, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "Our town has <mark data-word=\"roughly\">roughly</mark> ten thousand people — about the same as when my grandfather was young. Some families have left for the city, some new ones have arrived, and the number has stayed more or less the same.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 758, "w": 75, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "'How much will it cost to fix the roof?' I asked. The builder looked up at it for a minute. '<mark data-word=\"roughly\">Roughly</mark> two thousand,' he said. 'I'll tell you exactly after I climb up there.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 759, "w": 75, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "A cat sleeps for <mark data-word=\"roughly\">roughly</mark> sixteen hours a day — more when it is old, less when it is young and hungry. Ours seems to sleep for twenty, and wakes up only for food and for my keyboard.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 760, "w": 76, "slot": 0, "also": [79], "text": "The job is <mark data-word=\"mainly\">mainly</mark> indoor work in winter, and <mark data-word=\"therefore\">therefore</mark> the winter men stay and the summer men leave. That is the whole economics of the trade, Dave said, and it explains why he never trusts anybody in his first June.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 761, "w": 76, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "I had a temporary lapse of memory, and forgot all my French. I drove them out <mark data-word=\"mainly\">mainly</mark> by gesture, and opened the window, to the universal horror. I intimated the death scene was postponed, and, as a matter of fact, my uncle did not die until the next night.", "source": "Tono-Bungay — H. G. Wells"},
  {"id": 762, "w": 76, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "'Well, it's <mark data-word=\"mainly\">mainly</mark> because I don't want a husband. Men are all right for friends, but as soon as you marry them they turn into cranky old fathers, even the wild ones. They begin to tell you what's sensible and what's foolish, and want you to stick at home all the time.", "source": "My Ántonia — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 763, "w": 76, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The people in our village are <mark data-word=\"mainly\">mainly</mark> farmers. A few work in the town, and there is one teacher, one doctor and one baker. But if you walk down the main street at six in the morning, almost everyone you meet is on the way to a field.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 764, "w": 76, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "My diet is <mark data-word=\"mainly\">mainly</mark> vegetables, rice and fish. I eat meat perhaps once a week, and sweets only on birthdays. My doctor says it is the reason my heart is so healthy.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 765, "w": 76, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The tourists who come to our island are <mark data-word=\"mainly\">mainly</mark> from Germany and Britain. In summer you hear German on the beach and English in the bars, and the waiters can say 'Another coffee?' in five languages.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 766, "w": 76, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Tom was introduced to the Judge; but his tongue was tied, his breath would hardly come, his heart quaked—partly because of the awful greatness of the man, but <mark data-word=\"mainly\">mainly</mark> because he was her parent. He would have liked to fall down and worship him, if it were in the dark. The Judge put his hand on Tom's head and called him a fine little man, and asked him what his name was.", "source": "The Adventures of Tom Sawyer, Complete — Mark Twain"},
  {"id": 767, "w": 76, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "Our garden is <mark data-word=\"mainly\">mainly</mark> grass, with a few flowers along the fence and one old apple tree in the corner. My father would like a vegetable garden, but nobody has time to look after it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 768, "w": 76, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "I read <mark data-word=\"mainly\">mainly</mark> in the evenings, in bed, before I fall asleep. Sometimes I read on the train too, but usually I just look out of the window. A good book can keep me awake until two in the morning.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 769, "w": 76, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The work is <mark data-word=\"mainly\">mainly</mark> outdoors, which sounds pleasant until January. In summer people ask how I got so brown; in winter nobody asks anything at all, because nobody stands outside long enough to talk.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 770, "w": 77, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "'Being here, let us stay. You ought to see it out, and I won't leave you. <mark data-word=\"besides\">Besides</mark>, that lonely girl with the dark hair runs in my head. It was little more than a glimpse we had of her that last time, and yet I almost see her waiting by the fire to-night.", "source": "Our Mutual Friend — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 771, "w": 77, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "\"Will you help me?\" she said, as he remained standing by her. \"You have still a straight back and young legs, it would be a trifle to you. <mark data-word=\"besides\">Besides</mark>, my house is not so very far from here, it stands there on the heath behind the hill. How soon you would bound up thither.\" The young man took compassion on the old woman.", "source": "Household Tales by Brothers Grimm — Jacob Grimm"},
  {"id": 772, "w": 77, "slot": 2, "also": [2], "text": "Arm's length? The length of an arm is not much. Barbara's was not a long arm, by any means, and <mark data-word=\"besides\">besides</mark>, she didn't hold it out straight, but bent a little. Kit was so near her when they shook hands, that he could see a small tiny tear, yet <mark data-word=\"tremble\">trembling</mark> on an eyelash.", "source": "The Old Curiosity Shop — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 773, "w": 77, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "I don't want to go to the party. I'm tired, and I have to get up early tomorrow. <mark data-word=\"besides\">Besides</mark>, I don't know anyone there except you, and you always disappear after ten minutes.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 774, "w": 77, "slot": 4, "also": [33], "text": "Dorothea did not answer on the instant: it was crossing her mind that she could not receive him in this library, where her husband's prohibition seemed to dwell. She looked towards the window. Could she go out and meet him in the grounds? The sky was heavy, and the trees had begun to shiver as at a coming storm. <mark data-word=\"besides\">Besides</mark>, she <mark data-word=\"shrink\">shrank</mark> from going out to him.", "source": "Middlemarch — George Eliot"},
  {"id": 775, "w": 77, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "\"Because I had suggested that he should come over. When he did not come I was surprised, and I naturally became alarmed for his safety when I heard cries upon the moor. By the way\" — his eyes darted again from my face to Holmes's — \"did you hear anything else <mark data-word=\"besides\">besides</mark> a cry?\"", "source": "The Hound of the Baskervilles — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 776, "w": 77, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "We can't get a dog. Our flat is too small, and nobody is at home during the day. <mark data-word=\"besides\">Besides</mark>, your little brother is afraid of dogs. Maybe when we have a house with a garden.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 777, "w": 77, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "I'll walk to work today. It's a beautiful morning, and the bus is always full. <mark data-word=\"besides\">Besides</mark>, the doctor told me I should walk more, and forty minutes of walking is better than forty minutes of standing on a bus.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 778, "w": 77, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The old car is still good. It starts every morning, it has never broken down on the motorway, and it is paid for. <mark data-word=\"besides\">Besides</mark>, my father says, a new car loses a lot of its value on the first day you drive it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 779, "w": 77, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Later in the day he sent for me. Ordinarily I would not have come without special reason, but just at present I am so interested in him that I would gladly make an effort. <mark data-word=\"besides\">Besides</mark>, I am glad to have anything to help to pass the time.", "source": "Dracula — Bram Stoker"},
  {"id": 780, "w": 78, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "'Is it true that you swam across the lake?' asked the teacher. 'It is <mark data-word=\"indeed\">indeed</mark>,' said my grandfather proudly. 'Three kilometres, in cold water, when I was seventy.' And he showed her the newspaper photo to prove it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 781, "w": 78, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "\"Yes,\" said Ben. \"I know her and she knows me.\" And he turned his back on her and went and stood looking out of the window, as if the sight of her was hateful to him, as <mark data-word=\"indeed\">indeed</mark> it was.", "source": "Little Lord Fauntleroy — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 782, "w": 78, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "Even then Mrs. Darling was placid. \"Let me try, dear,\" she said, and <mark data-word=\"indeed\">indeed</mark> that was what he had come to ask her to do, and with her nice cool hands she tied his tie for him, while the children stood around to see their fate decided.", "source": "Peter Pan — J. M. Barrie"},
  {"id": 783, "w": 78, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The guidebook said the view from the top was worth the climb, and it was <mark data-word=\"indeed\">indeed</mark>. After three hours on the steep path, we stood on the rocks and saw the whole coast below us, the sea shining all the way to the horizon.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 784, "w": 78, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "'Thank you very much <mark data-word=\"indeed\">indeed</mark> for your help,' said the old lady, when I carried her shopping up the four floors to her flat. She gave me a biscuit and a long handshake, and told me I was the kindest young man in the building.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 785, "w": 78, "slot": 5, "also": [17], "text": "\"It is fear, Mr. Holmes. It is terror.\" She raised her veil as she spoke, and we could see that she was <mark data-word=\"indeed\">indeed</mark> in a pitiable state of agitation, her face all drawn and grey, with restless frightened eyes, like those of some hunted animal. Her features and figure were those of a woman of thirty, but her hair was shot with premature grey, and her expression was <mark data-word=\"weary\">weary</mark> and haggard.", "source": "The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 786, "w": 78, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "My friends told me the new café made the best cakes in town. I did not believe them — until I tried the chocolate one. It was <mark data-word=\"indeed\">indeed</mark> the best cake I had ever eaten, and I went back the next day for another piece.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 787, "w": 78, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The weather forecast said it would be a very cold night, and it was cold <mark data-word=\"indeed\">indeed</mark>: in the morning the water in the dog's bowl was solid ice, and the car windows were white with frost.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 788, "w": 78, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Sara turned suddenly to find her standing by the table, looking very queer <mark data-word=\"indeed\">indeed</mark>. She had shut her eyes, and was twisting her face in strange convulsive contortions, her hands hanging stiffly clenched at her sides. She looked as if she was trying to lift some enormous weight.", "source": "A Little Princess — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 789, "w": 78, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "He said he would be late, and he was late <mark data-word=\"indeed\">indeed</mark> — three hours late. By the time he arrived, the dinner was cold, the guests had gone home, and his wife had fallen asleep on the sofa.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 790, "w": 79, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "I thought I had read in his face that he would like to speak to me alone. I <mark data-word=\"therefore\">therefore</mark> resolved to put myself in his way next evening, as he came home from his work. Having settled this with myself, I fell asleep.", "source": "David Copperfield — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 791, "w": 79, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "They thought they had only a girl to deal with and that, <mark data-word=\"therefore\">therefore</mark>, they could ride roughshod over her. But she would show them their mistake. They wouldn't have dared to have treated her like that if she had been a man. But she would see that her daughter got her rights: she wouldn't be fooled.", "source": "Dubliners — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 792, "w": 79, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "The road over the mountain was closed because of snow. We <mark data-word=\"therefore\">therefore</mark> had to take the long way round the lake, which added two hours to the journey. We arrived late, but we saw the most beautiful sunset of the whole holiday.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 793, "w": 79, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "\"We must consult as to what is to be done,\" he said as we descended the stairs. In the hall he opened the dining-room door, and we passed in, he closing the door carefully behind him. The shutters had been opened, but the blinds were already down, with that obedience to the etiquette of death which the British woman of the lower classes always rigidly observes. The room was, <mark data-word=\"therefore\">therefore</mark>, dimly dark. It was, however, light enough for our purposes.", "source": "Dracula — Bram Stoker"},
  {"id": 794, "w": 79, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The shop had sold out of the cake my mother wanted. She <mark data-word=\"therefore\">therefore</mark> decided to make one herself, the first cake she had baked in twenty years. It was not beautiful, but everyone at the party asked for the recipe.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 795, "w": 79, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The little room was cooler now, and the air blew in fresh and sweet. Outside, the birds were twittering joyously, and Pollyanna flew to the window to talk to them. She saw then that down in the garden her aunt was already out among the rosebushes. With rapid fingers, <mark data-word=\"therefore\">therefore</mark>, she made herself ready to join her.", "source": "Pollyanna — Eleanor H. Porter"},
  {"id": 796, "w": 79, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The man did not even grunt this time, nor turn his head. Pollyanna decided that of course he did not hear her. The next time, <mark data-word=\"therefore\">therefore</mark> (which happened to be the following day), she spoke up louder.", "source": "Pollyanna — Eleanor H. Porter"},
  {"id": 797, "w": 79, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "Tom had not studied at all, and he <mark data-word=\"therefore\">therefore</mark> failed the exam. His parents were not angry; they only said, 'Now you know.' The next time, he studied every evening for a month, and he passed with the second-best mark in the class.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 798, "w": 79, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "It rained heavily all morning, and the football match was <mark data-word=\"therefore\">therefore</mark> moved to the next Saturday. The children were disappointed, but they spent the afternoon playing table football in the school hall instead.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 799, "w": 79, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Just as they were setting off, the gentlemen returned. They had taken out a young dog, who had spoilt their sport, and sent them back early. Their time and strength, and spirits, were, <mark data-word=\"therefore\">therefore</mark>, exactly ready for this walk, and they entered into it with pleasure.", "source": "Persuasion — Jane Austen"},
  {"id": 800, "w": 80, "slot": 0, "also": [83], "text": "It rained all morning, so the <mark data-word=\"crew\">crew</mark> ate under a plastic <mark data-word=\"shelter\">shelter</mark> beside the trucks. Eight men, one bench, and a radio that nobody was listening to. Those forty minutes are the part I miss now that I have an office.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 801, "w": 80, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "When the storm began, we ran to the old bus stop at the end of the road. It was a small wooden <mark data-word=\"shelter\">shelter</mark> with a roof and three walls, and we stood inside it, wet and laughing, while the rain crashed down on the roof for half an hour.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 802, "w": 80, "slot": 2, "also": [35], "text": "He sprang through a gate into a field, <mark data-word=\"drag\">dragging</mark> the reluctant Pompey after him. We had hardly got under the <mark data-word=\"shelter\">shelter</mark> of the hedge when the carriage rattled past. I caught a glimpse of Dr. Armstrong within, his shoulders bowed, his head sunk on his hands, the very image of distress. I could tell by my companion's graver face that he also had seen.", "source": "The Return of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 803, "w": 80, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "She had consented to go away, to leave her home. Was that wise? She tried to weigh each side of the question. In her home anyway she had <mark data-word=\"shelter\">shelter</mark> and food; she had those whom she had known all her life about her. Of course she had to work hard, both in the house and at business.", "source": "Dubliners — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 804, "w": 80, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "He set to work to carry out a new idea that had come to him. He extended the fire into a large circle. Inside this circle he crouched, his sleeping outfit under him as a protection against the melting snow. When he had thus disappeared within his <mark data-word=\"shelter\">shelter</mark> of flame, the whole pack came curiously to the rim of the fire to see what had become of him.", "source": "White Fang — Jack London"},
  {"id": 805, "w": 80, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "In the mountains there are small stone huts where walkers can find <mark data-word=\"shelter\">shelter</mark> if the weather turns bad. Inside there is a bench, a blanket and sometimes a little food left by the last visitors. More than one life has been saved in them.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 806, "w": 80, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "After the earthquake, thousands of families had lost their homes. The army built a camp of big tents in the park, where people could find <mark data-word=\"shelter\">shelter</mark>, hot food and a place to sleep until their houses were repaired.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 807, "w": 80, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The cows stood together under the big oak tree, using it as a <mark data-word=\"shelter\">shelter</mark> from the rain. Their backs were dry, their tails swung slowly, and they watched the wet field with patient brown eyes until the sun came out again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 808, "w": 80, "slot": 8, "also": [50], "text": "The town has a <mark data-word=\"shelter\">shelter</mark> for homeless dogs at the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edge</mark> of the forest. Lost and unwanted dogs are brought there, fed and cared for until a new family takes them home. We found our dog there — a small brown one with one ear up and one ear down.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 809, "w": 80, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "During the war, when the planes came, people ran down into a <mark data-word=\"shelter\">shelter</mark> under the school. It was dark and cold, and it smelled of wet earth, but the thick concrete walls kept everyone inside safe. My grandmother remembers singing there with the other children.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 810, "w": 81, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "There was a <mark data-word=\"leak\">leak</mark> in the roof, just above my bed. Every time it rained, a drop of water fell on my pillow every few seconds. For a week I slept with a bucket next to my head, until my father climbed up and fixed the broken tile.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 811, "w": 81, "slot": 1, "also": [82], "text": "Halfway up the wall I found water running down from the floor above — a <mark data-word=\"leak\">leak</mark> that nobody had reported. When I touched the switch a small <mark data-word=\"spark\">spark</mark> jumped out and the lights went off along the whole corridor. Nobody was hurt, and Marek told the story all week.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 812, "w": 81, "slot": 2, "also": [36, 59], "text": "The plumber found the <mark data-word=\"leak\">leak</mark> under the kitchen sink: a small <mark data-word=\"crack\">crack</mark> in the pipe, from which water had been dripping for weeks into the cupboard. He <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaced</mark> the pipe in ten minutes and charged us for an hour.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 813, "w": 81, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "I had an idea that the well had sprung a <mark data-word=\"leak\">leak</mark>; that some of the wall stones near the bottom had fallen and exposed fissures that allowed the water to escape. I measured the chain — 98 feet. Then I called in a couple of monks, locked the door, took a candle, and made them lower me in the bucket.", "source": "A Connecticut Yankee in King Arthur's Court — Mark Twain"},
  {"id": 814, "w": 81, "slot": 4, "also": [50], "text": "Soon she was leaking—not at any particular point, but everywhere. She did not spring a <mark data-word=\"leak\">leak</mark>, I mean, but water came in first of all near the decaying <mark data-word=\"edge\">edges</mark> of her planks, and then through them.", "source": "Tono-Bungay — H. G. Wells"},
  {"id": 815, "w": 81, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "If you smell gas in your house, there may be a <mark data-word=\"leak\">leak</mark>. Do not turn on any lights, do not use your phone inside, open the windows and leave at once. Then call the gas company from outside.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 816, "w": 81, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "My bicycle tyre was flat every morning, so there had to be a <mark data-word=\"leak\">leak</mark> somewhere. My brother put the tube in a bowl of water and pressed it slowly, part by part, until a line of tiny bubbles came out of one small hole.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 817, "w": 81, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "They got their supper and a nap, and about midnight we sallied out through a solitude so wholly vacant and complete that it quite overpassed the required conditions. We took possession of the well and its surroundings. My boys were experts in all sorts of things, from the stoning up of a well to the constructing of a mathematical instrument. An hour before sunrise we had that <mark data-word=\"leak\">leak</mark> mended in ship-shape fashion, and the water began to rise. Then we stowed our fireworks in the chapel, locked up the place, and went home to bed.", "source": "A Connecticut Yankee in King Arthur's Court — Mark Twain"},
  {"id": 818, "w": 81, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The boat had a <mark data-word=\"leak\">leak</mark>, and water was coming in through a hole in the bottom. One of us rowed as fast as he could while the other two threw water out with a bucket and a cooking pot. We reached the shore just in time, with water up to our ankles.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 819, "w": 81, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The old bucket had a small <mark data-word=\"leak\">leak</mark> in the side, so by the time I carried it from the well to the garden, half the water was gone. My grandfather put a piece of chewing gum over the hole, and it worked for the rest of the summer.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 820, "w": 82, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "When the blacksmith hit the red-hot metal with his hammer, bright orange <mark data-word=\"spark\">sparks</mark> flew in every direction. The children watching at the door jumped back, laughing, although he had told them they were quite safe where they stood.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 821, "w": 82, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "'Don't sit so close to the campfire,' said my father. 'A <mark data-word=\"spark\">spark</mark> could land on your jacket.' A moment later a small <mark data-word=\"spark\">spark</mark> jumped out of the fire and made a tiny black hole in my sleeve.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 822, "w": 82, "slot": 2, "also": [59], "text": "The old light switch in the hall was broken. When you pressed it, a small blue <mark data-word=\"spark\">spark</mark> jumped between the wires with a little crackle. My mother refused to touch it until an electrician came and <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaced</mark> it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 823, "w": 82, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "People lit fires long before matches existed. They hit two special stones together until a <mark data-word=\"spark\">spark</mark> fell onto some dry grass. Then they blew on it very gently, and the tiny <mark data-word=\"spark\">spark</mark> grew into a flame.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 824, "w": 82, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The man was in no hurry, and struck again with the flint and steel. As the <mark data-word=\"spark\">sparks</mark> fell thick and bright about him, I could see his hands, and touches of his face, and could make out that he was seated and bending over the table; but nothing more. Presently I saw his blue lips again, breathing on the tinder, and then a flare of light flashed up, and showed me Orlick.", "source": "Great Expectations — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 825, "w": 82, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The train's metal wheels made <mark data-word=\"spark\">sparks</mark> as it braked hard, a long line of little orange flashes in the dark. From the platform it looked like fireworks, and a little boy next to me clapped his hands.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 826, "w": 82, "slot": 6, "also": [22], "text": "In the sudden thud, hiss, and glare of the igniting trees, the panic-stricken <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> seems to have swayed hesitatingly for some moments. <mark data-word=\"spark\">Sparks</mark> and burning twigs began to fall into the road, and single leaves like puffs of flame. Hats and dresses caught fire. Then came a crying from the common. There were shrieks and shouts, and suddenly a mounted policeman came galloping through the confusion with his hands clasped over his head, screaming.", "source": "The war of the worlds — H. G. Wells"},
  {"id": 827, "w": 82, "slot": 7, "also": [36], "text": "\"And so,\" thought I, \"could I with you.\" I should have said so, perhaps, but a livid, vivid <mark data-word=\"spark\">spark</mark> leapt out of a cloud at which I was looking, and there was a <mark data-word=\"crack\">crack</mark>, a crash, and a close rattling peal; and I thought only of hiding my dazzled eyes against Mr. Rochester's shoulder.", "source": "Jane Eyre: An Autobiography — Charlotte Brontë"},
  {"id": 828, "w": 82, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "When I pulled my wool jumper over my head in the dark, I saw tiny <mark data-word=\"spark\">sparks</mark> of electricity in it, little blue flashes that crackled near my ears. My little sister thought I was magic and made me do it again and again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 829, "w": 82, "slot": 9, "also": [51], "text": "The fire at the old mill started with a single <mark data-word=\"spark\">spark</mark> from a broken machine. It fell into a <mark data-word=\"pile\">pile</mark> of dry wood, and within ten minutes the whole building was burning. Luckily, nobody was inside.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 830, "w": 83, "slot": 0, "also": [84], "text": "Our <mark data-word=\"crew\">crew</mark> starts the <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark> at six thirty, which means the alarm goes at five. For the first year I hated that hour. Now it is the only part of the day when nobody wants anything from me.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 831, "w": 83, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "The ship's <mark data-word=\"crew\">crew</mark> worked day and night during the storm. Some pulled the ropes, some pumped the water out, and the cook kept everyone going with hot soup. When they reached the harbour, the captain shook every one of their hands.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 832, "w": 83, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "A film <mark data-word=\"crew\">crew</mark> arrived in our village last summer: cameramen, lighting people, a director and forty others. For two weeks they filled the café and the hotel, and half the children in the village got a part as 'boy in the street'.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 833, "w": 83, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The <mark data-word=\"crew\">crew</mark> of the plane — two pilots and six flight attendants — stood at the door and said goodbye to every passenger. They had been working for fourteen hours, but they were still smiling.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 834, "w": 83, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "After the storm, a <mark data-word=\"crew\">crew</mark> of workers from the electricity company arrived to repair the fallen lines. They worked in the rain until two in the morning, and at last the lights came back on in every house in the valley.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 835, "w": 83, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The fire <mark data-word=\"crew\">crew</mark> reached the burning house in six minutes. Two firefighters went inside while the others pulled the hoses from the truck. They brought out a woman, her baby and, last of all, a very angry cat.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 836, "w": 83, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "In the tumultuous business of cutting-in and attending to a whale, there is much running backwards and forwards among the <mark data-word=\"crew\">crew</mark>. Now hands are wanted here, and then again hands are wanted there. There is no staying in any one place; for at one and the same time everything has to be done everywhere. It is much the same with him who endeavors the description of the scene. We must now retrace our way a little.", "source": "Moby-Dick — Herman Melville"},
  {"id": 837, "w": 83, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The rowing <mark data-word=\"crew\">crew</mark> had trained together for four years. Eight people pulled their oars at exactly the same moment, like one body, and their boat flew over the water. When they won the gold medal, they all cried.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 838, "w": 83, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "I am not going to relate that voyage in detail. It was fairly prosperous. The ship proved to be a good ship, the <mark data-word=\"crew\">crew</mark> were capable seamen, and the captain thoroughly understood his business. But before we came the length of Treasure Island, two or three things had happened which require to be known.", "source": "Treasure Island — Robert Louis Stevenson"},
  {"id": 839, "w": 83, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Our road was closed for a week while a <mark data-word=\"crew\">crew</mark> of builders put in new pipes. Every morning at seven the machines started, and every evening at five the men sat on the wall and drank tea before they went home.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 840, "w": 84, "slot": 0, "also": [85], "text": "The first <mark data-word=\"wage\">wage</mark> covered the rent and the food and nothing else at all. The second one covered the rent, the food and a phone card. I remember counting it on the bed after a night <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark>, in coins, twice, to be sure of it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 841, "w": 84, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "My mother works as a nurse, and her <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark> changes every week. Sometimes she works from seven in the morning until three, sometimes from three until eleven, and sometimes all night. We have a calendar on the fridge so we know when she will be home.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 842, "w": 84, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "My father worked the night <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark> at the hospital for twenty years. He left the house at nine in the evening, when we were going to bed, and came home at seven in the morning, when we were eating breakfast. For most of my childhood I saw him only at weekends.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 843, "w": 84, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "And the children felt secure when their father was in bed. They lay and talked softly awhile. Then they started as the lights went suddenly sprawling over the ceiling from the lamps that swung in the hands of the colliers tramping by outside, going to take the nine o'clock <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark>. They listened to the voices of the men, imagined them dipping down into the dark valley. Sometimes they went to the window and watched the three or four lamps growing tinier and tinier, swaying down the fields in the darkness.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 844, "w": 84, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The factory never stops. The morning <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark> starts at six, the afternoon <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark> at two and the night <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark> at ten. When one group of workers goes home, the next group is already putting on their overalls.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 845, "w": 84, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Anna works the night <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark> at the bakery, from ten in the evening until six in the morning. When the rest of the town wakes up, the bread is ready and she is going home to sleep. She has not seen a sunset on a weekday for two years.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 846, "w": 84, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "After a twelve-hour <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark> at the hospital, the young doctor fell asleep on the bus home and missed her stop. She woke up at the end of the line, laughed, and took the same bus back again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 847, "w": 84, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "Waiters in busy restaurants often work a double <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark>: lunch and dinner, with only two hours of rest in between. By the end of the evening their feet hurt so much that some of them take their shoes off on the way to the car.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 848, "w": 84, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "In the summer my brother worked the early <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark> at a hotel, from five until one. He hated getting up in the dark, but he loved having the whole afternoon free to go to the beach.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 849, "w": 84, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The miners' <mark data-word=\"shift\">shift</mark> ended at four. When the siren sounded, the men came up out of the ground, black with coal dust, blinking in the daylight, and walked home together through the village, talking and laughing.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 850, "w": 85, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "In my first job I earned a very low <mark data-word=\"wage\">wage</mark> — five euros an hour, washing dishes in a hotel kitchen. But I saved every coin, and at the end of the summer I bought my first bicycle with my own money.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 851, "w": 85, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "Mr. Shimerda made grandmother sit down on the only chair and pointed his wife to a stool beside her. Standing before them with his hand on Ántonia's shoulder, he talked in a low tone, and his daughter translated. He wanted us to know that they were not beggars in the old country; he made good <mark data-word=\"wage\">wages</mark>, and his family were respected there. He left Bohemia with more than a thousand dollars in savings, after their passage money was paid.", "source": "My Ántonia — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 852, "w": 85, "slot": 2, "also": [63], "text": "The factory workers asked for higher <mark data-word=\"wage\">wages</mark>. Prices in the shops had gone up, but their pay had stayed the same for three years, and many of them could not <mark data-word=\"afford\">afford</mark> the rent. After a week of talks, the owners agreed to pay them ten percent more.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 853, "w": 85, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "When his wife had died, Jurgis made for the nearest saloon, but he did not do that now, though he had his week's <mark data-word=\"wage\">wages</mark> in his pocket. He walked and walked, seeing nothing, splashing through mud and water. Later on he sat down upon a step and hid his face in his hands and for half an hour or so he did not move.", "source": "The Jungle — Upton Sinclair"},
  {"id": 854, "w": 85, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "They had had a long argument with Ambrosch about Ántonia's allowance for clothes and pocket-money. It was his plan that every cent of his sister's <mark data-word=\"wage\">wages</mark> should be paid over to him each month, and he would provide her with such clothing as he thought necessary. When Mrs. Harling told him firmly that she would keep fifty dollars a year for Ántonia's own use, he declared they wanted to take his sister to town and dress her up and make a fool of her.", "source": "My Ántonia — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 855, "w": 85, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Every Friday afternoon the farmer paid the workers their <mark data-word=\"wage\">wages</mark> in cash, counting the notes into each hand. Then the men went straight to the pub, and by Monday some of them had nothing left.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 856, "w": 85, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Waiters in that restaurant earn a small <mark data-word=\"wage\">wage</mark>, but the tips are good. On a busy Saturday night, one waiter told me, he can take home more in tips than in a whole week of pay.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 857, "w": 85, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "When I was sixteen, I worked in a shop on Saturdays. The <mark data-word=\"wage\">wage</mark> was not much, but it was mine. I kept it in a tin under my bed and counted it every Sunday morning, dreaming about what I would buy.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 858, "w": 85, "slot": 8, "also": [77], "text": "When they had gotten him to sleep, however, they sat by the kitchen fire and talked it over in frightened whispers. They were in for a siege, that was plainly to be seen. Jurgis had only about sixty dollars in the bank, and the slack season was upon them. Both Jonas and Marija might soon be earning no more than enough to pay their board, and <mark data-word=\"besides\">besides</mark> that there were only the <mark data-word=\"wage\">wages</mark> of Ona and the pittance of the little boy.", "source": "The Jungle — Upton Sinclair"},
  {"id": 859, "w": 85, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The new law says that no one can be paid less than the minimum <mark data-word=\"wage\">wage</mark>, a fixed amount for each hour of work. Before the law, some workers in the fields earned only half of it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 860, "w": 86, "slot": 0, "also": [87], "text": "'Hold the <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark> steady,' I said, and then I said it again, louder. I was three metres up with a <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolt</mark> between my teeth and a man below me who was looking at his telephone. That is how people fall.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 861, "w": 86, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "Accepting the hand of the officer, she began the descent of the <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark>, at the foot of which the boat waited. The officer followed her. A large cloak was spread at the stern; the officer requested her to sit down upon this cloak, and placed himself beside her.", "source": "The three musketeers — Alexandre Dumas"},
  {"id": 862, "w": 86, "slot": 2, "also": [32], "text": "The cat climbed the tallest tree in the garden and then sat on a high branch, crying, too scared to come down. My father got the long <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark> from the shed, <mark data-word=\"lean\">leaned</mark> it against the trunk and climbed up step by step. The moment he reached the branch, the cat jumped down by itself.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 863, "w": 86, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "Felton continued to descend. Near the bottom of the <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark>, when he found no more support for his feet, he clung with his hands; at length, arrived at the last step, he let himself hang by the strength of his wrists, and touched the ground. He stooped down, picked up the bag of money, and placed it between his teeth.", "source": "The three musketeers — Alexandre Dumas"},
  {"id": 864, "w": 86, "slot": 4, "also": [38], "text": "The painter stood at the top of his <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark>, three metres above the street, painting the window frames of the old shop. Every time a bus passed, the <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark> shook a little, and his assistant held it <mark data-word=\"steady\">steady</mark> with both hands.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 865, "w": 86, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "To get into the tree house, you had to climb a rope <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark> that hung from the branches. It swung from side to side with every step, and my little sister needed ten minutes to reach the top. Coming down was even slower.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 866, "w": 86, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "They found the <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark> so heavy they could not pull it up, so the Scarecrow fell off the wall and the others jumped down upon him so that the hard floor would not hurt their feet. Of course they took pains not to light on his head and get the pins in their feet. When all were safely down they picked up the Scarecrow, whose body was quite flattened out, and patted his straw into shape again.", "source": "The Wonderful Wizard of Oz — L. Frank Baum"},
  {"id": 867, "w": 86, "slot": 7, "also": [22], "text": "The firefighters raised a long <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark> to the third-floor window, where an old man was waving a towel. One firefighter climbed up quickly, helped him onto the <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark> and came down with him, one step at a time, while the <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> below held its breath.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 868, "w": 86, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Toto jumped out of Dorothy's arms and hid under the bed, and the girl started to get him. Aunt Em, badly frightened, threw open the trap door in the floor and climbed down the <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark> into the small, dark hole. Dorothy caught Toto at last and started to follow her aunt. When she was halfway across the room there came a great shriek from the wind, and the house shook so hard that she lost her footing and sat down suddenly upon the floor.", "source": "The Wonderful Wizard of Oz — L. Frank Baum"},
  {"id": 869, "w": 86, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "I ran back at once, told the watchman to get three or four men immediately and follow me into the grounds of Carfax, in case our friend might be dangerous. I got a <mark data-word=\"ladder\">ladder</mark> myself, and crossing the wall, dropped down on the other side. I could see Renfield's figure just disappearing behind the angle of the house, so I ran after him. On the far side of the house I found him pressed close against the old ironbound oak door of the chapel.", "source": "Dracula — Bram Stoker"},
  {"id": 870, "w": 87, "slot": 0, "also": [88, 89], "text": "With my third wage I bought my own <mark data-word=\"drill\">drill</mark>. Then a measuring <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark>, then a box with every <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolt</mark> I used most often, sorted by size. A man who arrives with his own tools is a different man on a site, and everybody there knows it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 871, "w": 87, "slot": 1, "also": [20, 41], "text": "The <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelf</mark> fell down because one <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolt</mark> had come <mark data-word=\"loose\">loose</mark>. My father looked at the wall, then at the <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolt</mark> in his hand, and went to the shop for longer, thicker ones. 'Four of these,' he said, 'and you could stand on it.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 872, "w": 87, "slot": 2, "also": [40], "text": "The playground swing was held to the frame by two big <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolts</mark>. Every spring, a man from the council came with a spanner and made sure they were <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark>, because if one of them failed, a child could fall.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 873, "w": 87, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "Our new wardrobe came in a flat box with a bag of screws and <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolts</mark> and a small tool. The instructions showed a smiling man building it in twenty minutes. It took me four hours, and at the end there was one <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolt</mark> left over.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 874, "w": 87, "slot": 4, "also": [40, 13, 59], "text": "Engineers checked every <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolt</mark> on the old railway bridge. There were more than ten thousand of them, holding the steel beams together, and each one had to be <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark>. They found twelve that were <mark data-word=\"rusty\">rusty</mark> and <mark data-word=\"replace\">replaced</mark> them the same week.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 875, "w": 87, "slot": 5, "also": [40], "text": "The mechanic took the wheel off my car by undoing five <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolts</mark> with a special tool. He put them in his pocket so as not to lose them, changed the tyre, and then put them back one by one, very <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark>.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 876, "w": 87, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The old man fixed the broken garden chair with a long metal <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolt</mark> and a nut. He pushed the <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolt</mark> through both pieces of wood and turned the nut on the other side until the leg was firm again. The chair lasted another ten years.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 877, "w": 87, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "When I pulled the little blue boat out of the lake, I saw that the <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolt</mark> holding the engine to the back was nearly out. Another hour on the water, and the engine would have fallen to the bottom of the lake.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 878, "w": 87, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "The factory made nothing but <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolts</mark> — millions of them every year, big and small, for cars, bridges and washing machines. My uncle worked there for thirty years and could tell the size of any <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolt</mark> just by looking at it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 879, "w": 87, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "When the wind blew, the old sign above the shop swung and creaked, because one of the <mark data-word=\"bolt\">bolts</mark> that held it to the wall had broken. One stormy night the sign fell into the street. Luckily it was three in the morning, and nobody was walking there.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 880, "w": 88, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "To hang the mirror, my father had to <mark data-word=\"drill\">drill</mark> two holes in the wall. The noise was terrible, like a giant dentist, and white dust fell onto the floor. Then he pushed the plugs into the holes, and the mirror went up in five minutes.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 881, "w": 88, "slot": 1, "also": [40], "text": "The dentist said she would have to <mark data-word=\"drill\">drill</mark> the bad tooth before she could fill it. I closed my eyes and held the arms of the chair very <mark data-word=\"tight\">tight</mark>. It took three minutes, and afterwards I could not feel my lip for an hour.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 882, "w": 88, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "In the dry villages, engineers came to <mark data-word=\"drill\">drill</mark> a deep well. Their machine went down through the rock for two days, eighty metres into the ground, until at last clean water came up. The whole village came to watch and cheered.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 883, "w": 88, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "'Don't <mark data-word=\"drill\">drill</mark> there!' shouted my mother, just as my brother touched the wall with the machine. 'There's an electric cable behind it!' He stopped, checked with a small detector, and moved the hole twenty centimetres to the left.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 884, "w": 88, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The fishermen had to <mark data-word=\"drill\">drill</mark> a hole through the ice before they could fish. They used a long hand tool that turned like a giant screw, and it took ten minutes to get through half a metre of ice to the dark water below.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 885, "w": 88, "slot": 5, "also": [20], "text": "Before we could put up the new <mark data-word=\"shelf\">shelves</mark>, we had to <mark data-word=\"drill\">drill</mark> six holes in the kitchen wall. The first two went in easily, but the third hit something hard, and the machine screamed and stopped. There was a metal pipe inside the wall.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 886, "w": 88, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Woodpeckers <mark data-word=\"drill\">drill</mark> holes in trees with their beaks, hitting the wood up to twenty times a second. They are looking for insects under the bark, and sometimes they make holes big enough to live in.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 887, "w": 88, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "Oil companies <mark data-word=\"drill\">drill</mark> deep under the sea to find oil. The machines stand on huge platforms far from land, and the workers live there for two weeks at a time, with grey water in every direction.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 888, "w": 88, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "My grandfather made a birdhouse for our garden. He cut the wood, nailed it together and then <mark data-word=\"drill\">drilled</mark> a round hole in the front, just big enough for a small bird. By April a family of blue tits had moved in.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 889, "w": 88, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The builder <mark data-word=\"drill\">drilled</mark> a hole through the thick stone wall so that the new pipe could go through it. It took nearly an hour, and the whole house shook. When he finished, we could see daylight through a hole the size of a coin.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 890, "w": 89, "slot": 0, "also": [91], "text": "I measured the window with a <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark> before buying new curtains: one metre forty wide, one metre sixty high. At the shop I <mark data-word=\"realise\">realised</mark> I had written the numbers the wrong way round, and I had to go home and measure again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 891, "w": 89, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "Mrs. Darling examined them very carefully; they were skeleton leaves, but she was sure they did not come from any tree that grew in England. She crawled about the floor, peering at it with a candle for marks of a strange foot. She rattled the poker up the chimney and tapped the walls. She let down a <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark> from the window to the pavement, and it was a sheer drop of thirty feet, without so much as a spout to climb up by.", "source": "Peter Pan — J. M. Barrie"},
  {"id": 892, "w": 89, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "\"It will be clear enough to you soon,\" he said, in an off-hand way. \"I think that there is nothing else of importance here, but I will look.\" He whipped out his lens and a <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark> measure, and hurried about the room on his knees, measuring, comparing, examining, with his long thin nose only a few inches from the planks, and his beady eyes gleaming and deep-set like those of a bird.", "source": "The Sign of the Four — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 893, "w": 89, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The box was falling apart, so I put strong brown <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark> all around it before sending it. By the time I finished, you could hardly see the cardboard, and the man at the post office laughed and asked if there was a lion inside.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 894, "w": 89, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The poster kept falling off my bedroom wall, so I stuck it up again with four pieces of <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark>, one in each corner. It stayed there for three years, and when I finally took it down, the paint came off with it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 895, "w": 89, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The tailor put a <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark> around my waist, then around my chest, and wrote the numbers in a little book. 'Your suit will be ready in two weeks,' he said. When I went back, it fitted perfectly.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 896, "w": 89, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "My little son covered his broken toy car with so much <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark> that you could no longer see what colour it was. But the wheels turned again, and he said it was now the strongest car in the world.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 897, "w": 89, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The police put yellow <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark> across the street to stop people walking into the area where the old building had fallen. People stood behind it and took photos with their phones until an officer asked them, politely, to go home.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 898, "w": 89, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "I cut my finger in the garden, so I covered the cut with a plaster and some white medical <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark>. My grandmother said her grandmother used honey and a clean cloth, and that it worked just as well.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 899, "w": 89, "slot": 9, "also": [50], "text": "To paint straight lines on the wall, my sister first stuck long strips of <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark> along the <mark data-word=\"edge\">edges</mark>. When the paint was dry, she pulled the <mark data-word=\"tape\">tape</mark> off slowly, and there was a perfect sharp line between the blue and the white.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 900, "w": 90, "slot": 0, "also": [94], "text": "He offered me a small crew of my own in July. I said I would <mark data-word=\"consider\">consider</mark> it for a week, which was not what he <mark data-word=\"expect\">expected</mark> — most men say yes in the room. I telephoned my grandmother instead, and she asked whether I was eating properly.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 901, "w": 90, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "Before buying the house, we <mark data-word=\"consider\">considered</mark> everything carefully: the price, the distance to school, the noise from the road and the size of the garden. We visited it three times, once at night. Then we decided, and we have never been sorry.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 902, "w": 90, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "We said nothing further to each other. I was still <mark data-word=\"consider\">considering</mark> whether I would go away or not when the man came back and sat down beside us again. Hardly had he sat down when Mahony, catching sight of the cat which had escaped him, sprang up and pursued her across the field. The man and I watched the chase. The cat escaped once more and Mahony began to throw stones at the wall she had escaladed.", "source": "Dubliners — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 903, "w": 90, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "My daughter wants to study medicine abroad. It's a big decision, so we are <mark data-word=\"consider\">considering</mark> it very carefully. We have read about the universities, talked to two doctors and made a long list of good and bad points on the fridge.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 904, "w": 90, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "The job in the city paid more, but it meant two hours on the train every day. Maria <mark data-word=\"consider\">considered</mark> the offer for a whole week. In the end she said no — she decided that time with her children was worth more than the extra money.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 905, "w": 90, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "'Don't answer now,' said the manager. 'Take the weekend and <mark data-word=\"consider\">consider</mark> it. Talk to your family.' So I did. I walked by the river on Saturday, thinking about it from every side, and on Monday morning I said yes.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 906, "w": 90, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Before you get a dog, <mark data-word=\"consider\">consider</mark> how much time you have. A dog needs a walk twice a day, every day, in rain and snow, for fifteen years. Many people only think about the cute puppy, not the old dog it will become.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 907, "w": 90, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The judge listened to both sides for three days. Then she said she needed time to <mark data-word=\"consider\">consider</mark> everything she had heard, and that she would give her decision in two weeks. The whole town waited.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 908, "w": 90, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Mrs. Morel <mark data-word=\"consider\">considered</mark>. She would have been glad now for her son to fall in love with some woman who would—she did not know what. But he fretted so, got so furious suddenly, and again was melancholic. She wished he knew some nice woman—She did not know what she wished, but left it vague.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 909, "w": 90, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "My brother is <mark data-word=\"consider\">considering</mark> selling his car. He only uses it at weekends, and it costs him a lot in insurance and parking. He has been thinking about it for months, and every week he changes his mind.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 910, "w": 91, "slot": 0, "also": [93], "text": "Last spring a client rang and I did not <mark data-word=\"recognise\">recognise</mark> his voice. He was angry about a delay, and as he talked I began to <mark data-word=\"realise\">realise</mark> that the mistake was not ours. I asked him to look at page four of the drawings again, and he went very quiet.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 911, "w": 91, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "I was halfway to the station when I <mark data-word=\"realise\">realised</mark> I had left my passport on the kitchen table. I ran back home, grabbed it and ran to the station again. I reached the platform just as the train doors were closing.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 912, "w": 91, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "Raskolnikov got up from the sofa. As he had mounted the stairs to Razumihin's, he had not <mark data-word=\"realise\">realised</mark> that he would be meeting his friend face to face. Now, in a flash, he knew, that what he was least of all disposed for at that moment was to be face to face with anyone in the wide world. His spleen rose within him.", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 913, "w": 91, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "All the while the peewits were screaming in the field. When he came to, he wondered what was near his eyes, curving and strong with life in the dark, and what voice it was speaking. Then he <mark data-word=\"realise\">realised</mark> it was the grass, and the peewit was calling. The warmth was Clara's breathing heaving. He lifted his head, and looked into her eyes.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 914, "w": 91, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "When I looked at the old photo more closely, I <mark data-word=\"realise\">realised</mark> that the girl in the background was my grandmother. I had looked at that picture a hundred times and never seen her there before.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 915, "w": 91, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "At first I thought the man in the park was talking to himself. Then I <mark data-word=\"realise\">realised</mark> he was talking on the phone through a tiny earpiece. I felt a little silly for being worried about him.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 916, "w": 91, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Only when she moved to another country did Sara <mark data-word=\"realise\">realise</mark> how much she loved her mother's cooking. For the first time, she called home to ask for recipes, and every Sunday she tried one, standing in her small kitchen far from home.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 917, "w": 91, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "It was only after the guests had gone that I <mark data-word=\"realise\">realised</mark> I had forgotten to serve the dessert. The beautiful cake was still in the fridge, untouched. We ate it ourselves, the two of us, for three days.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 918, "w": 91, "slot": 8, "also": [2], "text": "The first light of day shone into the room. The Brahman saw that Siddhartha was <mark data-word=\"tremble\">trembling</mark> softly in his knees. In Siddhartha's face he saw no <mark data-word=\"tremble\">trembling</mark>, his eyes were fixed on a distant spot. Then his father <mark data-word=\"realise\">realized</mark> that even now Siddhartha no longer dwelt with him in his home, that he had already left him.", "source": "Siddhartha — Hermann Hesse"},
  {"id": 919, "w": 91, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Halfway through the film, my friend suddenly <mark data-word=\"realise\">realised</mark> that she had already seen it. 'I know how it ends!' she whispered, and then, to everyone's horror, she told us.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 920, "w": 92, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "'Did you <mark data-word=\"notice\">notice</mark> anything strange about the man at the door?' asked the police officer. I thought for a moment. 'Yes,' I said. 'He was wearing sunglasses, and it was raining.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 921, "w": 92, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "In the dressing-room behind the stage she was introduced to the secretary of the Society, Mr Fitzpatrick. She smiled and shook his hand. He was a little man, with a white vacant face. She <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticed</mark> that he wore his soft brown hat carelessly on the side of his head and that his accent was flat. He held a programme in his hand and, while he was talking to her, he chewed one end of it into a moist pulp.", "source": "Dubliners — James Joyce"},
  {"id": 922, "w": 92, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "As he walked through the office, Raskolnikov <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticed</mark> that many people were looking at him. Among them he saw the two porters from the house, whom he had invited that night to the police station. They stood there waiting. But he was no sooner on the stairs than he heard the voice of Porfiry Petrovitch behind him. Turning round, he saw the latter running after him, out of breath.", "source": "Crime and Punishment — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 923, "w": 92, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "My mother <mark data-word=\"notice\">notices</mark> everything. If I cut my hair one centimetre, she sees it. If I am sad, she knows before I say a word. And if I have eaten a biscuit before dinner, she knows that too.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 924, "w": 92, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "I walked past the new café for weeks before I <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticed</mark> it. It was small, between a bank and a shoe shop, with no big sign. When I finally went in, it had the best coffee in the street.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 925, "w": 92, "slot": 5, "also": [30], "text": "\"That is because it makes you think of something else,\" said Sara, <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapping</mark> the coverlet round her until only her small dark face was to be seen looking out of it. \"I've <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticed</mark> this. What you have to do with your mind, when your body is miserable, is to make it think of something else.\"", "source": "A Little Princess — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 926, "w": 92, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Nobody <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticed</mark> that the old dog was missing until dinner time, when his bowl stayed full. We searched the whole house and garden and finally found him asleep in the car, which my father had left open that afternoon.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 927, "w": 92, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The teacher <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticed</mark> that Tom was not writing anything during the test. She walked over quietly and saw that he had no pen. Without a word, she put one on his desk and walked on.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 928, "w": 92, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "When my grandfather shaved off his beard after forty years, it took my grandmother two days to <mark data-word=\"notice\">notice</mark>. 'Something is different about you,' she said at breakfast, looking at him closely. 'Did you get new glasses?'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 929, "w": 92, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Agafea Mihalovna, seeing that it was coming to a quarrel, gently put down her cup and withdrew. Kitty did not even <mark data-word=\"notice\">notice</mark> her. The tone in which her husband had said the last words wounded her, especially because he evidently did not believe what she had said.", "source": "Anna Karenina — graf Leo Tolstoy"},
  {"id": 930, "w": 93, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "Lena Lingard laughed, as if this pleased her. I had not <mark data-word=\"recognise\">recognized</mark> her for a moment, either. I had never seen her before with a hat on her head—or with shoes and stockings on her feet, for that matter. And here she was, brushed and smoothed and dressed like a town girl, smiling at us with perfect composure.", "source": "My Ántonia — Willa Cather"},
  {"id": 931, "w": 93, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "I had not seen my cousin for fifteen years, and when she opened the door, I did not <mark data-word=\"recognise\">recognise</mark> her. The little girl with long hair was now a tall woman with short grey hair. Only when she laughed did I know her — she still laughed exactly the same way.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 932, "w": 93, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "Both his clothes and the straw were saturated with rain. His arms were flung over his head; his face was flushed to an unnatural crimson. His eyes had a burning brightness, and though they met her own, she perceived that he did not <mark data-word=\"recognise\">recognize</mark> her.", "source": "The Woodlanders — Thomas Hardy"},
  {"id": 933, "w": 93, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "Two-eyes lived a long time in happiness. Once two poor women came to her in her castle, and begged for alms. She looked in their faces, and <mark data-word=\"recognise\">recognized</mark> her sisters, One-eye, and Three-eyes, who had fallen into such poverty that they had to wander about and beg their bread from door to door.", "source": "Household Tales by Brothers Grimm — Jacob Grimm"},
  {"id": 934, "w": 93, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "Kitty admired her more than ever, and more and more acute was her suffering. Kitty felt overwhelmed, and her face showed it. When Vronsky saw her, coming across her in the mazurka, he did not at once <mark data-word=\"recognise\">recognize</mark> her, she was so changed.", "source": "Anna Karenina — graf Leo Tolstoy"},
  {"id": 935, "w": 93, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "From this petrified state, he was awoken by a hand touching his shoulder. Instantly, he <mark data-word=\"recognise\">recognised</mark> this touch, this tender, bashful touch, and regained his senses. He rose and greeted Vasudeva, who had followed him. And when he looked into Vasudeva's friendly face, into the small wrinkles, which were as if they were filled with nothing but his smile, into the happy eyes, then he smiled too.", "source": "Siddhartha — Hermann Hesse"},
  {"id": 936, "w": 93, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "My grandfather has lost his memory, and some days he does not <mark data-word=\"recognise\">recognise</mark> us. He looks at my mother politely, like a stranger. But when she sings the old songs from his childhood, his eyes change, and sometimes he says her name.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 937, "w": 93, "slot": 7, "also": [92], "text": "Of course, this trivial incident could not with me end in that. I often met that officer afterwards in the street and <mark data-word=\"notice\">noticed</mark> him very carefully. I am not quite sure whether he <mark data-word=\"recognise\">recognised</mark> me, I imagine not; I judge from certain signs.", "source": "Notes from the Underground — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 938, "w": 93, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Twenty years after I left, I went back to my village. The school had changed and the shop had changed, but I <mark data-word=\"recognise\">recognised</mark> the old stone bridge at once. I sat on it, just as I did as a boy, and watched the water for an hour.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 939, "w": 93, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Our dog <mark data-word=\"recognise\">recognised</mark> the sound of our car from far away. Long before we reached the house, he was already standing at the gate, wagging his tail. When other cars passed, he never moved.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 940, "w": 94, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "When she came into the bedroom, he was already in bed. His lips were sternly compressed, and his eyes looked away from her. Anna got into her bed, and lay <mark data-word=\"expect\">expecting</mark> every minute that he would begin to speak to her again. She both feared his speaking and wished for it.", "source": "Anna Karenina — graf Leo Tolstoy"},
  {"id": 941, "w": 94, "slot": 1, "also": [37], "text": "\"Won't you sit down?\" He indicated an armchair at a writing-table covered with papers. He sat down himself, and, <mark data-word=\"rub\">rubbing</mark> his little hands with short fingers covered with white hairs, he bent his head on one side. But as soon as he was settled in this position a moth flew over the table. The lawyer, with a swiftness that could never have been <mark data-word=\"expect\">expected</mark> of him, opened his hands, caught the moth, and resumed his former attitude.", "source": "Anna Karenina — graf Leo Tolstoy"},
  {"id": 942, "w": 94, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "We <mark data-word=\"expect\">expected</mark> the journey to take two hours, but it took five. There was an accident on the motorway, then road works, and then we got lost in the last village. We arrived just in time for dinner, very hungry and very tired.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 943, "w": 94, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "I didn't <mark data-word=\"expect\">expect</mark> so many people to come to my birthday party. I had invited twenty, and forty came — friends of friends, neighbours and a man nobody knew. We ran out of food at nine and ordered pizza.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 944, "w": 94, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "We were not <mark data-word=\"expect\">expecting</mark> visitors on a Tuesday evening, so when the doorbell rang at nine, we all looked at each other. It was my uncle from Canada, whom we had not seen for ten years. He had wanted to surprise us, and he certainly did.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 945, "w": 94, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "The weather forecast told us to <mark data-word=\"expect\">expect</mark> heavy rain in the afternoon, so we took umbrellas and coats. The sun shone all day, and we carried the umbrellas around the city for nothing.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 946, "w": 94, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "A red square of light had sprung out of the darkness. In the middle of it was the black figure of the secretary, his head advanced, peering out into the night. It was evident that he was <mark data-word=\"expect\">expecting</mark> someone. Then at last there were steps in the road, a second figure was visible for an instant against the light, the door shut, and all was black once more.", "source": "The Return of Sherlock Holmes — Arthur Conan Doyle"},
  {"id": 947, "w": 94, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "She sprang to her feet and actually uttered a cry of terror. It broke from her before she had time to check it. She had thought of these new-comers as being thousands of miles away, when she had ever thought of them at all, which she had scarcely done for years. She had never <mark data-word=\"expect\">expected</mark> to see them again.", "source": "Little Lord Fauntleroy — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 948, "w": 94, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "My parents <mark data-word=\"expect\">expected</mark> me to become a doctor like my father. I tried, but after a year at university I knew it was wrong for me. I became a teacher instead, and now they say it was the best decision I ever made.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 949, "w": 94, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The test was much easier than I <mark data-word=\"expect\">expected</mark>. I had studied all weekend for difficult questions, and most of them were things we had done in class. I finished early and spent the last half hour checking my answers.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 950, "w": 95, "slot": 0, "also": [98], "text": "He did not <mark data-word=\"encourage\">encourage</mark> people often, so the sentence stayed with me. I would still <mark data-word=\"prefer\">prefer</mark> the mornings when nobody speaks to me before seven — but I have a crew of my own now, and a crew talks.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 951, "w": 95, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "She stood and looked at him. He knew she wanted to come with him, but he <mark data-word=\"prefer\">preferred</mark> to be alone. She made him feel imprisoned when she was there, as if he could not get a free deep breath, as if there were something on top of him. She felt his desire to be free of her.", "source": "Sons and Lovers — D. H. Lawrence"},
  {"id": 952, "w": 95, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "'Would you like tea or coffee?' asked my aunt. 'I <mark data-word=\"prefer\">prefer</mark> tea, thank you,' I said. She looked surprised — everyone else in the family drinks coffee — but she made me a big cup of tea, and after that she always had my favourite kind in the cupboard.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 953, "w": 95, "slot": 3, "also": [22], "text": "My brother likes the city, with its noise, shops and <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowds</mark>. I <mark data-word=\"prefer\">prefer</mark> the countryside: quiet mornings, long walks and a sky full of stars at night. When we visit each other, one of us is always bored.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 954, "w": 95, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "No man <mark data-word=\"prefer\">prefers</mark> to sleep two in a bed. In fact, you would a good deal rather not sleep with your own brother. I don't know how it is, but people like to be private when they are sleeping.", "source": "Moby-Dick — Herman Melville"},
  {"id": 955, "w": 95, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Most cats <mark data-word=\"prefer\">prefer</mark> warm places. Ours sleeps on the radiator in winter and in the sunniest window in summer, and if you put her on the cold kitchen floor, she walks straight back to her warm spot, looking offended.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 956, "w": 95, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "I <mark data-word=\"prefer\">prefer</mark> to travel by train rather than by plane. It is slower, but I can read, look out of the window and walk around, and I arrive in the middle of the city instead of an hour away from it.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 957, "w": 95, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "This went on for two or three weeks before any new idea came to him. Being slow and ponderous, it always took him a long time to reach a new idea. As a rule, he did not like new ideas, but <mark data-word=\"prefer\">preferred</mark> old ones. After two or three weeks, however, during which, instead of getting better, matters really grew worse, a novel plan slowly and deliberately dawned upon him. He would go to see Dick.", "source": "Little Lord Fauntleroy — Frances Hodgson Burnett"},
  {"id": 958, "w": 95, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "My grandfather always <mark data-word=\"prefer\">preferred</mark> old films to new ones. 'Too much noise and too many explosions,' he said about modern cinema. On Sunday afternoons he watched black-and-white films from the fifties, and he knew every word.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 959, "w": 95, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "Some children <mark data-word=\"prefer\">prefer</mark> to play alone. My son can spend a whole afternoon building a city out of blocks without saying a word, while his sister needs five friends and a lot of shouting to have fun.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 960, "w": 96, "slot": 0, "also": [97], "text": "The men on my crew <mark data-word=\"complain\">complain</mark> about exactly the things I used to <mark data-word=\"complain\">complain</mark> about, and some weeks I have to <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologise</mark> for a decision I got wrong. Nobody warned me that this would be most of the job.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 961, "w": 96, "slot": 1, "also": [97], "text": "The hotel room was dirty and the shower was cold, so we went down to <mark data-word=\"complain\">complain</mark>. The manager listened, <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologised</mark> and moved us to a better room with a view of the sea. In the end it was the best room we have ever had.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 962, "w": 96, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "My neighbours <mark data-word=\"complain\">complained</mark> about the noise from our party. At midnight one of them knocked on the door in his pyjamas and asked us, very politely, to turn the music down. We did, and the next day we took him a piece of cake.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 963, "w": 96, "slot": 3, "also": [30], "text": "As soon as it was broad daylight the doctor came, and Winterborne entered the house with him. Marty said that her father was <mark data-word=\"wrap\">wrapped</mark> up and ready, as usual, to be put into his chair. They ascended the stairs, and soon seated him. He began at once to <mark data-word=\"complain\">complain</mark> of the tree, and the danger to his life and Winterborne's house-property in consequence.", "source": "The Woodlanders — Thomas Hardy"},
  {"id": 964, "w": 96, "slot": 4, "also": [], "text": "My grandfather never <mark data-word=\"complain\">complained</mark>, even when he was in pain. 'What good would it do?' he said. But my grandmother always knew when something was wrong, because on those days he was even quieter than usual.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 965, "w": 96, "slot": 5, "also": [33], "text": "The girls did not <mark data-word=\"complain\">complain</mark>, but they huddled up close together, and set their lips firm, and every time a drop touched them, they visibly <mark data-word=\"shrink\">shrank</mark> and shuddered. It was a noble sight to see them suffering thus in silence, but it unnerved me altogether. I am too sensitive.", "source": "Three men in a boat (to say nothing of the dog) — Jerome K. Jerome"},
  {"id": 966, "w": 96, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "Through all our troubles, I never but once heard my mother <mark data-word=\"complain\">complain</mark> of our want of money. As summer was coming on she observed to Mary and me, \"What a desirable thing it would be for your papa to spend a few weeks at a watering-place. I am convinced the sea-air and the change of scene would be of incalculable service to him. But then, you see, there's no money,\" she added, with a sigh.", "source": "Agnes Grey — Anne Brontë"},
  {"id": 967, "w": 96, "slot": 7, "also": [95], "text": "'I keep that room to myself still; but I cannot always desert Mrs. Heep, you know. And so,' said Agnes, quietly, 'I feel obliged to bear her company, when I might <mark data-word=\"prefer\">prefer</mark> to be alone. But I have no other reason to <mark data-word=\"complain\">complain</mark> of her. If she tires me, sometimes, by her praises of her son, it is only natural in a mother.", "source": "David Copperfield — Charles Dickens"},
  {"id": 968, "w": 96, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Passengers often <mark data-word=\"complain\">complain</mark> that the bus is late, but the drivers have their own problems: road works, traffic, and people who take five minutes to find their money. My uncle drove a bus for twenty years and says the job is harder than it looks.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 969, "w": 96, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "The soup was cold, so I called the waiter and <mark data-word=\"complain\">complained</mark>. He took it away without a word and brought back a hot bowl, together with a free glass of wine. 'Sorry,' he said. 'The kitchen is very busy tonight.'", "source": ""},
  {"id": 970, "w": 97, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "When I stepped on a woman's foot on the full bus, I <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologised</mark> at once. 'I'm so sorry!' I said. She smiled and said it was fine, although she was holding her foot and trying not to cry.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 971, "w": 97, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "My brother broke my headphones and did not say a word. Three days later he came into my room, looked at the floor and <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologised</mark>. 'Sorry,' he said. 'I'll buy you new ones.' And he did, with the money from his birthday.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 972, "w": 97, "slot": 2, "also": [94], "text": "As for herself, she determined to go too. No message came from Henry; perhaps he <mark data-word=\"expect\">expected</mark> her to <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologize</mark>. Now that she had time to think over her own tragedy, she was unrepentant. She neither forgave him for his behaviour nor wished to forgive him. Her speech to him seemed perfect.", "source": "Howards End — E. M. Forster"},
  {"id": 973, "w": 97, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "The train company <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologised</mark> for the long wait. A voice said 'We are very sorry for any trouble' every ten minutes, which made everyone on the platform laugh after the fifth time.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 974, "w": 97, "slot": 4, "also": [43], "text": "'You were <mark data-word=\"rude\">rude</mark> to your grandmother,' said my mother. 'Go and <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologise</mark>.' I went into the kitchen, where Grandma was peeling potatoes, and told her I was sorry. She gave me a potato and a knife, and we peeled them together.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 975, "w": 97, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "Sometimes I was positively choked with resentment. At last I determined to challenge my enemy to a duel. I composed a splendid, charming letter to him, imploring him to <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologise</mark> to me, and hinting rather plainly at a duel in case of refusal. The letter was so composed that if the officer had had the least understanding of the sublime and the beautiful he would certainly have flung himself on my neck and have offered me his friendship. And how fine that would have been!", "source": "Notes from the Underground — Fyodor Dostoyevsky"},
  {"id": 976, "w": 97, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The famous actor was forty minutes late for the interview. When he finally came in, he <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologised</mark> again and again, explained that his car had broken down and bought coffee for everyone in the room.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 977, "w": 97, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "In Britain people <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologise</mark> all the time — even when it is not their fault. If you step on someone's foot, they will probably say 'Sorry!' to you. My friend from Spain found this very funny during her first month in London.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 978, "w": 97, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "After the argument, neither of them wanted to <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologise</mark> first. For three days the two sisters did not speak. Then the younger one left a note on her sister's pillow: 'Sorry. I miss you.' That evening they cooked dinner together, laughing, as if nothing had happened.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 979, "w": 97, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "At nine Mr. McCaskey came. He carried his coat on his arm and his pipe in his teeth; and he <mark data-word=\"apologise\">apologised</mark> for disturbing the boarders on the steps as he selected spots of stone between them on which to set his size 9, width Ds.", "source": "The Four Million — O. Henry"},
  {"id": 980, "w": 98, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "My teacher <mark data-word=\"encourage\">encouraged</mark> me to enter the writing competition. 'Your stories are good,' she said. 'Believe me, you can do this.' I didn't win, but I came third, and I have been writing ever since.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 981, "w": 98, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "When my son was learning to ride his bike, we ran next to him, shouting, 'Keep going! You can do it!' Every time he fell, we <mark data-word=\"encourage\">encouraged</mark> him to get back on. By the end of the afternoon he was riding round the park on his own.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 982, "w": 98, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "\"Quite so, quite so,\" he agreed. Helen asked herself why she had snubbed him. Once or twice during the day she had <mark data-word=\"encourage\">encouraged</mark> him to criticize, and then had pulled him up short. Was she afraid of him presuming?", "source": "Howards End — E. M. Forster"},
  {"id": 983, "w": 98, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "Good parents <mark data-word=\"encourage\">encourage</mark> their children to try new things, even if they fail. My mother let me try piano, football, painting and chess. I was bad at most of them, but I found out what I loved, and I was never afraid to try.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 984, "w": 98, "slot": 4, "also": [22], "text": "The <mark data-word=\"crowd\">crowd</mark> <mark data-word=\"encourage\">encouraged</mark> the runners as they came up the last hill, clapping and shouting the names printed on their shirts. One old man, who was almost walking, heard his name, smiled and started to run again.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 985, "w": 98, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "My friend was afraid to sing in front of people, but we <mark data-word=\"encourage\">encouraged</mark> her to try at the school concert. She was nervous, and her voice shook at first, but by the end the whole hall was on its feet.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 986, "w": 98, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "The doctor <mark data-word=\"encourage\">encouraged</mark> my father to start walking every day after his heart operation. 'Start with ten minutes,' she said, 'and a little more each week.' Six months later he walked five kilometres to our house and knocked on the door, very pleased with himself.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 987, "w": 98, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The coach <mark data-word=\"encourage\">encourages</mark> every player, not just the best ones. When the youngest boy missed an easy goal, the coach did not shout. He said, 'Good try. Next time, look at the corner.' The next time, the boy scored.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 988, "w": 98, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Our town <mark data-word=\"encourage\">encourages</mark> people to cycle to work. It has built new bike paths, put bike racks outside every office, and on the first Friday of every month, anyone who arrives by bike gets a free breakfast in the main square.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 989, "w": 98, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "My grandmother <mark data-word=\"encourage\">encouraged</mark> me to study abroad. 'I never had the chance,' she said. 'You go, and see the world for both of us.' She gave me her savings for the ticket, and every week I sent her a postcard.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 990, "w": 99, "slot": 0, "also": [], "text": "Anyone who stays calm on a telephone will <mark data-word=\"achieve\">achieve</mark> more than a fast worker who shouts. Dave said that once, about me, when he did not know that I could hear him. It was the first thing I ever <mark data-word=\"achieve\">achieved</mark> in that country that had nothing to do with my hands.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 991, "w": 99, "slot": 1, "also": [], "text": "After four years of training, she finally <mark data-word=\"achieve\">achieved</mark> her dream: a place in the national team. On the day she got the letter, she ran around the house, screaming, and then sat down on the floor and cried.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 992, "w": 99, "slot": 2, "also": [], "text": "My grandfather left school at twelve, but he <mark data-word=\"achieve\">achieved</mark> a lot in his life. He started with one small shop and ended with ten, and he made sure all his children went to university.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 993, "w": 99, "slot": 3, "also": [], "text": "'You can <mark data-word=\"achieve\">achieve</mark> almost anything if you work at it a little every day,' my piano teacher used to say. 'Ten minutes every day is better than two hours once a week.' She was right: after a year of ten minutes a day, I could play my first real piece.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 994, "w": 99, "slot": 4, "also": [94], "text": "The team <mark data-word=\"achieve\">achieved</mark> something nobody had <mark data-word=\"expect\">expected</mark>: they won the league in their first season. The players were young, the stadium was small and there was little money, but they trained harder than anyone else.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 995, "w": 99, "slot": 5, "also": [], "text": "My goal this year is to run a marathon. To <mark data-word=\"achieve\">achieve</mark> it, I run four times a week, eat better and go to bed early. I have not run the whole forty-two kilometres yet, but last Sunday I ran thirty.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 996, "w": 99, "slot": 6, "also": [], "text": "When she was eighty, my great-aunt <mark data-word=\"achieve\">achieved</mark> something she had wanted all her life: she learned to swim. It took her a whole summer of lessons in the town pool, and on the last day the swimming teacher gave her a medal.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 997, "w": 99, "slot": 7, "also": [], "text": "The village <mark data-word=\"achieve\">achieved</mark> what the government could not. Without any help, the people collected money, built a small bridge over the river and opened it with a party. Now the children can walk to school in ten minutes instead of an hour.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 998, "w": 99, "slot": 8, "also": [], "text": "Climbing the mountain was the hardest thing I have ever done, and the thing I am proudest to have <mark data-word=\"achieve\">achieved</mark>. My legs hurt, the air was thin, and twice I wanted to turn back. But when I stood at the top, I felt that I could do anything.", "source": ""},
  {"id": 999, "w": 99, "slot": 9, "also": [], "text": "After ten years of work, the scientists <mark data-word=\"achieve\">achieved</mark> what everyone had said was impossible: a medicine that could stop the disease. On the day it was announced, the head of the team went home early and slept for fourteen hours.", "source": ""},
];
